// Scenario Lab asset library — The Righteous Mind - Jonathan Haidt
const _AL_righteous_mind = {"id":"righteous_mind","title":"The Righteous Mind - Jonathan Haidt","chapters":[{"id":"rm_intro","title":"Introduction: Can We All Get Along?","pages":"1-15","assets":[{"asset_type":"DECEPTION_AUDIT_FILE","domain":"Moral Psychology | Political Division","topic":"The Righteous Mind Paradox: Why Smart People Disagree About Morality","subject_baseline":"Two equally intelligent, well-educated people read the same news article about immigration policy. One sees a moral imperative to welcome refugees. The other sees a moral imperative to protect borders. Both are certain of their righteousness. Both believe the other side is not just wrong but morally deficient.","interrogation_transcript":"Haidt argues that morality is not derived from pure reason. The rationalist tradition (Kohlberg, Piaget) claimed children develop moral reasoning through stages toward universal principles. But Haidt found that people reach moral conclusions intuitively, then use reason to justify those intuitions post-hoc. Two groups with different moral intuitions cannot reason their way to agreement because reasoning is the servant of intuition, not its master.","observed_nonverbal_log":["Each side experiences genuine moral emotion about their position","Each side marshals sophisticated reasoning to support their view","Neither side can understand how the other fails to see the obvious truth","The disagreement is not about facts but about which moral principles take priority"],"question":"If both sides are smart and well-informed, why can't reasoned debate resolve political and moral disagreements?","correct_diagnosis":"The social intuitionist model: moral judgments are caused by quick, automatic intuitions, not by slow, conscious reasoning. Reasoning comes after the fact to justify the already-reached conclusion. When two people have different moral intuitions (different moral foundations activated), no amount of reasoning can bridge the gap because each side uses reason to defend its own intuitive position. The key insight: we are not disinterested truth-seekers; we are intuitive lawyers defending our team.","distractors":["One side simply lacks the relevant information and would change their mind if presented with better facts","Intelligence differences - the less intelligent side fails to follow the logic of the superior argument","Emotional interference - emotions cloud judgment on controversial topics and prevent clear reasoning"],"diagnostic_breakdown":"Stage 1 - Intuitive Flash: A policy triggers an immediate intuitive judgment (this feels right/wrong). Stage 2 - Reasoned Defense: The brain mobilizes arguments to support the intuition. Stage 3 - Confirmation Bias: We search for supporting evidence and dismiss counterevidence. Stage 4 - Moral Certainty: The combination of strong intuition + supporting arguments produces unshakeable conviction. The division between good people is not about ignorance or stupidity but about different moral intuitions wired by evolution and shaped by culture.","player_mission":"If both sides are smart and well-informed, why can't reasoned debate resolve political and moral disagreements?","difficulty_level":"Intermediate","visual_frame_description":"A vast archive where three principle-seals glow over three aisles - Rider and Elephant, Six Taste Receptors, Chimp and Bee - and filing clerks race to stamp each incoming claim with the right seal while a Darwin quote about cooperative groups hangs above the door.","key_concepts":["Rodney King's appeal 'Can we all get along?' was made famous on May 1, 1992, after a jury failed to convict four Los Angeles police officers, triggering six days of rioting in which fifty-three people were killed and more than seven thousand buildings were torched","The word righteous comes from the old Norse rettviss and old English rihtwis, both meaning 'just, upright, virtuous,' and now carries religious connotations because it is usually used to translate the Hebrew word tzedek","Self-righteous means 'convinced of one's own righteousness, especially in contrast with the actions and beliefs of others; narrowly moralistic and intolerant'","Haidt chose the title The Righteous Mind over The Moral Mind to convey that human nature is not just intrinsically moral, it's also intrinsically moralistic, critical, and judgmental","An obsession with righteousness, leading inevitably to self-righteousness, is the normal human condition - a feature of our evolutionary design, not a bug that crept into minds that would otherwise be objective and rational","Righteous minds made it possible for human beings, but no other animals, to produce large cooperative groups, tribes, and nations without the glue of kinship","Our righteous minds guarantee that our cooperative groups will always be cursed by moralistic strife","Some degree of conflict among groups may even be necessary for the health and development of any society","One modern definition of righteous captures its linkage with judgmentalism: 'arising from an outraged sense of justice, morality, or fair play'","The book quotes Matthew: 'Why do you see the speck in your neighbor's eye, but do not notice the log in your own eye?'","The eighth-century Chinese Zen master Sen-ts'an wrote that the struggle between 'for' and 'against' is the mind's worst disease","Haidt says that a world without moralism, gossip, and judgment would quickly decay into chaos"]}]},{"id":"rm_ch01","title":"Chapter 1: Where Does Morality Come From?","pages":"18-51","assets":[{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Moral Psychology | Theories of Moral Development","topic":"Rationalist vs Intuitionist Models of Moral Judgment","column_a_label":"Rationalist Model (Kohlberg) - Moral reasoning comes first. We reason to moral conclusions through conscious deliberation and universal principles.","column_b_label":"Social Intuitionist Model (Haidt) - Moral intuition comes first. Reasoning is post hoc justification of intuitive judgments.","rows":[{"scenario":"A child sees someone push another child off a swing and says that is wrong because it hurts people","correct_column":"B","reason":"The intuitive flash that hurting is wrong comes first; the because it hurts explanation is post-hoc justification. Young children judge wrongness instantly before they can articulate reasons."},{"scenario":"A philosopher weighs the trolley problem and constructs a logical argument for utilitarianism over deontology","correct_column":"A","reason":"Conscious reasoning about abstract principles is the rationalist ideal, though Haidt would argue the philosopher chose utilitarianism because it felt right intuitively first"},{"scenario":"A person condemns a harmless taboo violation (eating your dead dog) but cannot explain why it is wrong","correct_column":"B","reason":"Moral dumbfounding - strong intuitive condemnation with no rational justification - is evidence for the intuitionist model"},{"scenario":"Kohlbergs subjects justify moral decisions by referencing universal principles of justice after deliberation","correct_column":"A","reason":"Kohlbergs model predicts that mature morality emerges from reaching abstract principles through reasoning stages"},{"scenario":"People make rapid character judgments of others within milliseconds of seeing their face","correct_column":"B","reason":"Automatic intuitive judgment occurs far too quickly for conscious reasoning to be the primary mechanism"}],"explanation":"Haidts social intuitionist model challenges 30 years of rationalist dominance. The evidence: (1) moral judgments occur rapidly and automatically, (2) moral dumbfounding shows we often cannot justify our intuitions, (3) brain damage that spares emotion but impairs reasoning preserves moral judgment, (4) hypnotic post-hypnotic suggestions can create moral disgust without any reasoned basis. The rationalist project of building morality on universal reason misunderstands how the mind actually works. Intuition is the elephant; reasoning is the rider.","player_mission":"","difficulty_level":"Advanced","visual_frame_description":"A library of rules with two great shelves - Moral and Conventional - and a widening third shelf labelled The Thicker Moral Order, where the Hua's food rules, purity laws, and a widow's fish dinner land while a card flips over to explain the informational assumptions behind each call.","key_concepts":["In the dog story, a family's dog is killed by a car in front of their house, and they cook and eat it, with nobody seeing them; most well-educated people felt an initial flash of disgust but hesitated before calling it morally wrong","In the chicken story, a man buys a chicken at the supermarket each week, has sexual intercourse with it before cooking, then eats it - a kind of recycling that some research subjects called an efficient use of natural resources","Educated liberal Westerners give nuanced answers acknowledging the man's right to do what he wants as long as he hurts no one, while most people on the planet think sex with a chicken carcass is morally wrong","Haidt called his invented stories 'harmless taboo violations' and found the ones that worked best fell into two categories: disgust and disrespect","One of his disrespect stories: a woman cleaning out her closet finds her old American flag, cuts it up into pieces, and uses the rags to clean her bathroom","In 38 percent of the 1,620 times that people heard a harmless-offensive story, they claimed that somebody was harmed, even though the stories were written to remove all conceivable harm","Subjects usually offered their invented victims up halfheartedly and almost apologetically, as in 'Well, I don't know, maybe the woman will feel guilty afterward about throwing out her flag?'","A child justified condemning the flag shredder by claiming the rags might clog up the toilet and cause it to overflow","Haidt trained his interviewers to correct people gently when they made claims that contradicted the text of the story, such as by pointing out that nobody saw the woman","When their invented victims were challenged, many subjects said things like 'I know it's wrong, but I just can't think of a reason why' - they were morally dumbfounded","The dumbfounded subjects were reasoning and working hard, but it was not reasoning in search of truth; it was reasoning in support of their emotional reactions","Haidt published these findings in one of the top psychology journals in October 1993, and almost nobody cited the work, even to attack it, in the first five years"]},{"asset_type":"CUE_SCRUBBER_STATION","domain":"Moral Psychology | Moral Dumbfounding","topic":"Moral Dumbfounding: The Strong Intuition Without Reasons","visual_frame_description":"A researcher presents a scenario: A brother and sister decide to make love while on vacation, using two forms of birth control. No one is hurt. They keep it a secret and feel closer afterward. The scenario is designed to have no identifiable victim or harm. Participants overwhelmingly condemn it as morally wrong. But when asked why, they struggle: It is just wrong. I cannot explain it but I know it is wrong.","target_channel":"Intuition-Without-Reason Gap | Post-Hoc Justification Failure","question":"Why do people passionately condemn an act as morally wrong even when they cannot articulate any coherent reason for their condemnation?","correct_cue":"Moral dumbfounding occurs when the intuitive system (the elephant) delivers a strong moral judgment, but the reasoning system (the rider) cannot find legitimate justifications. The intuition operates faster than and independently of reasoning. When the rider is caught without arguments, it does not change the elephants direction - it falls silent or invents weak post-hoc justifications. The persistence of the judgment despite the absence of reasons proves that intuition, not reason, drives moral judgment.","distractors":["People are simply embarrassed to admit they are not sure and pretend to be certain due to social pressure","The scenario triggers disgust which people mistake for moral judgment through misattribution of arousal","There is actually hidden harm - the act threatens social order in ways people sense but cannot articulate"],"underlying_psychology":"Haidt, Koller, and Dias 1993 introduced moral dumbfounding. The phenomenon reveals that moral judgment is intuitive, not rational. People can know something is wrong without knowing why. The rationalist tradition (Kohlberg, Turiel) held that moral judgments are based on principles like harm and justice. Dumbfounding shows that people often judge based on raw intuition - disgust, disrespect, or a sense of sacredness - and only then look for harm-based reasons. The rider searches desperately for a justification but the elephant already made the call.","player_mission":"Why do people passionately condemn an act as morally wrong even when they cannot articulate any coherent reason for their condemnation?","difficulty_level":"Beginner","key_concepts":["In the dog story, a family's dog is killed by a car in front of their house, and they cook and eat it, with nobody seeing them; most well-educated people felt an initial flash of disgust but hesitated before calling it morally wrong","In the chicken story, a man buys a chicken at the supermarket each week, has sexual intercourse with it before cooking, then eats it - a kind of recycling that some research subjects called an efficient use of natural resources","Educated liberal Westerners give nuanced answers acknowledging the man's right to do what he wants as long as he hurts no one, while most people on the planet think sex with a chicken carcass is morally wrong","Haidt called his invented stories 'harmless taboo violations' and found the ones that worked best fell into two categories: disgust and disrespect","One of his disrespect stories: a woman cleaning out her closet finds her old American flag, cuts it up into pieces, and uses the rags to clean her bathroom","In 38 percent of the 1,620 times that people heard a harmless-offensive story, they claimed that somebody was harmed, even though the stories were written to remove all conceivable harm","Subjects usually offered their invented victims up halfheartedly and almost apologetically, as in 'Well, I don't know, maybe the woman will feel guilty afterward about throwing out her flag?'","A child justified condemning the flag shredder by claiming the rags might clog up the toilet and cause it to overflow","Haidt trained his interviewers to correct people gently when they made claims that contradicted the text of the story, such as by pointing out that nobody saw the woman","When their invented victims were challenged, many subjects said things like 'I know it's wrong, but I just can't think of a reason why' - they were morally dumbfounded","The dumbfounded subjects were reasoning and working hard, but it was not reasoning in search of truth; it was reasoning in support of their emotional reactions","Haidt published these findings in one of the top psychology journals in October 1993, and almost nobody cited the work, even to attack it, in the first five years"]}]},{"id":"rm_ch02a","title":"Chapter 2: The Intuitive Dog and Its Rational Tail (Part 1)","pages":"52-71","assets":[{"asset_type":"CUE_SCRUBBER_STATION","domain":"Moral Psychology | Dual Process Theory","topic":"The Rider and the Elephant: Why Intuition Leads and Reason Follows","visual_frame_description":"A 6-ton elephant with a tiny rider on top. The rider holds the reins and believes he is steering. But when the elephant wants to go a different direction, the rider is powerless to stop it. The rider can sometimes influence the elephant gently, over time, but any sudden change of direction requires the elephant to cooperate. The rider exists to serve the elephant.","target_channel":"Intuition Dominance | Post-Hoc Rationalization Detection","question":"If the rider (conscious reasoning) appears to be in control but the elephant (automatic intuition) actually steers, how can we tell which is really driving?","correct_cue":"Three telltale signs reveal the elephant is in charge: (1) Speed - intuitive judgments form in milliseconds, conscious reasoning takes seconds; the judgment is already made before reasoning can contribute. (2) Moral dumbfounding - when reasoning fails to justify the intuition, the judgment persists anyway. (3) Post-hoc confabulation - brain-damaged patients and split-brain patients generate plausible-sounding reasons for decisions their non-verbal brain made, proving the rider is primarily an explainer not a decider. The rider is the press secretary, not the president.","distractors":["The rider controls the elephant completely - conscious reasoning steers moral judgment and the elephant provides emotional energy","The elephant and rider are equals in a partnership where reason and intuition each handle different domains","The rider can always override the elephant with sufficient willpower and concentration"],"underlying_psychology":"The rider/elephant metaphor derives from decades of dual-process research (Kahneman System 1/System 2, Bargh automaticity, Wilson adaptive unconscious). The elephant represents: automatic, intuitive, affective, fast, effortless, and nearly the entire mental process. The rider: controlled, deliberate, analytical, slow, effortful, and a very small part of the mind. The rider evolved not to lead but to serve as the elephants lawyer - explaining and justifying intuitive reactions to others in the social group. The rider is useful: it can plan ahead, warn of dangers, and sometimes train the elephant through repetition. But it cannot simply command the elephant to change course.","player_mission":"If the rider (conscious reasoning) appears to be in control but the elephant (automatic intuition) actually steers, how can we tell which is really driving?","difficulty_level":"Beginner","key_concepts":["Scott Murphy brought thirty UVA students into the lab, one at a time, for an extended interview, explaining that his job was to challenge their reasoning no matter what they said","In the Heinz dilemma, Scott couldn't whip up any dumbfounding, because people offered good reasons and would not abandon principles such as 'Life is more important than property'","In the 'roach juice' scenario, Scott opened a can of apple juice, then dipped a sterilized cockroach into the cup with a tea strainer and asked the subject to take a sip; 37 percent were willing","In the soul-selling scenario, Scott offered subjects $2 to sign a paper selling their soul to him after death, with a note that the form was not a legal or binding contract; only 23 percent signed without any goading","Scott convinced an extra 10 percent to sip the juice and an extra 17 percent to sign the soul-selling paper, but most people in both scenarios clung to their initial refusal","Many of the subjects who refused to sell their souls were atheists who didn't believe in souls and yet still felt uncomfortable about signing","In the Julie-and-Mark story, a brother and sister on summer vacation in France decide to make love, using birth control pills and a condom; only 20 percent of subjects said it was OK","In the Jennifer story, a vegetarian hospital worker who incinerates a fresh human cadaver cuts off a piece of flesh, cooks it, and eats it; only 13 percent said it was OK","In the harmless-taboo scenarios people generated far more reasons and discarded far more reasons than in any of the other scenarios, flailing around as Scott proved each reason irrelevant","One dumbfounded incest-story subject said: 'There's just no way I could change my mind but I just don't know how to - how to show what I'm feeling, what I feel about it. It's crazy!'","The quantified analyses showed big differences between responses to the harmless-taboo scenarios and responses to the Heinz dilemma","The results supported Hume, not Jefferson or Plato: people made moral judgments quickly and emotionally, and moral reasoning was mostly a post hoc search for reasons to justify judgments already made"]},{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Moral Psychology | Reason as Servant","topic":"The Post-Hoc Reasoner: Why We Are Not the Rational Animals We Think We Are","stage_1_observation":"Participants read a description of a man who commits a murder. They are asked whether he should be executed. Then they read a case of corporate fraud that destroyed thousands of retirement accounts. They are asked about punishment for that too. For each case they generate elaborate reasons for their sentencing decisions: deterrence, justice, proportionality. Then the researchers reveal that the order of cases was randomized and that participants had been assigned to either harsh or lenient anchor conditions. Participants sentencing decisions were heavily influenced by the anchor, but their reasons made no mention of it - they genuinely believed their reasons caused their decisions.","stage_1_question":"If the real cause of sentencing differences was the random anchor, why did participants produce elaborate, confident, and entirely irrelevant explanations for their decisions?","stage_1_correct_answer":"The rider (conscious reasoning) does not know what the elephant (intuition) did or why. The elephant made the sentencing decision based on the anchoring effect (an intuitive comparison). The rider then scanned the situation for plausible justifications and found some - the nature of the crime, the need for deterrence - that sounded like good reasons. The rider genuinely believed these reasons caused the decision because the rider has no access to the elephants actual processing. We are strangers to ourselves.","stage_2_intervention":"You just made a snap judgment about a job candidate in 30 seconds. You told your colleague: I do not think she is the right fit - she lacks confidence. Your colleague points out she has 10 years of progressively senior experience. Now examine your own reasoning: can you articulate the actual basis for your judgment?","stage_2_correct_answer":"Apply the suspicion test to your own reasoning. The elephants judgment may have been based on: (1) similarity to someone you disliked in the past, (2) her voice tone reminded you of a critical teacher, (3) you were in a bad mood from the previous meeting and misattributed it. The confidence explanation you gave is a plausible-sounding post-hoc construction. The test: if the real cause were something irrelevant (mood, similarity, tiredness), would your reasoning change? Probably not - you would still believe your confidence assessment. The rider is the last to know. The remedy: slow down, suspend judgment, and seek disconfirming evidence before the elephant decides.","boss_battle_explanation":"Layer 1 - Intuitive Primacy: The elephant decides in milliseconds. Layer 2 - Reason as Press Secretary: The rider constructs plausible-sounding explanations without access to the real causes. Layer 3 - Explanation Blindness: We believe our post-hoc explanations because the rider is genuinely unaware of its ignorance. Layer 4 - The Suspicion Test: When you catch yourself producing confident, instant explanations for your judgments, ask: What would the elephant have done if it had been influenced by something irrelevant? The answer is exactly what you just did.","player_mission":"","difficulty_level":"Intermediate","visual_frame_description":"A stark interview room where a single lamp lights a subject and a challenger with a clipboard; on the table sit a cup of apple juice, a white box with a sterilized cockroach, a $2 soul contract, and a stack of transcripts that refuse to move toward change.","key_concepts":["Scott Murphy brought thirty UVA students into the lab, one at a time, for an extended interview, explaining that his job was to challenge their reasoning no matter what they said","In the Heinz dilemma, Scott couldn't whip up any dumbfounding, because people offered good reasons and would not abandon principles such as 'Life is more important than property'","In the 'roach juice' scenario, Scott opened a can of apple juice, then dipped a sterilized cockroach into the cup with a tea strainer and asked the subject to take a sip; 37 percent were willing","In the soul-selling scenario, Scott offered subjects $2 to sign a paper selling their soul to him after death, with a note that the form was not a legal or binding contract; only 23 percent signed without any goading","Scott convinced an extra 10 percent to sip the juice and an extra 17 percent to sign the soul-selling paper, but most people in both scenarios clung to their initial refusal","Many of the subjects who refused to sell their souls were atheists who didn't believe in souls and yet still felt uncomfortable about signing","In the Julie-and-Mark story, a brother and sister on summer vacation in France decide to make love, using birth control pills and a condom; only 20 percent of subjects said it was OK","In the Jennifer story, a vegetarian hospital worker who incinerates a fresh human cadaver cuts off a piece of flesh, cooks it, and eats it; only 13 percent said it was OK","In the harmless-taboo scenarios people generated far more reasons and discarded far more reasons than in any of the other scenarios, flailing around as Scott proved each reason irrelevant","One dumbfounded incest-story subject said: 'There's just no way I could change my mind but I just don't know how to - how to show what I'm feeling, what I feel about it. It's crazy!'","The quantified analyses showed big differences between responses to the harmless-taboo scenarios and responses to the Heinz dilemma","The results supported Hume, not Jefferson or Plato: people made moral judgments quickly and emotionally, and moral reasoning was mostly a post hoc search for reasons to justify judgments already made"]},{"asset_type":"DECEPTION_AUDIT_FILE","domain":"Neuroscience | The Interpreter Module","topic":"The Interpreter Module: How the Left Brain Confabulates Reasons","subject_baseline":"Split-brain patients (whose corpus callosum has been severed) have two independently functioning hemispheres. A command is shown only to the right hemisphere (left visual field): Walk. The patient stands up and begins walking. When asked why they are walking, the left hemisphere (which did not see the command) instantly fabricates a plausible reason: I am going to get a Coke.","interrogation_transcript":"Gazzanigas split-brain studies reveal the interpreter module — a left-hemisphere system that continuously constructs narratives to explain behavior. It has no access to the right hemispheres perceptions or intentions. When the right hemisphere causes an action, the left hemisphere observes the action and invents a reason. Crucially, the patient genuinely believes the fabricated reason. The interpreter is not lying. It does not know it is confabulating. It is doing its job: creating a coherent story about why we do what we do.","observed_nonverbal_log":["The left hemisphere generates explanations without access to the actual cause","The patient believes the confabulated explanation as genuine truth","The interpreter operates constantly, weaving a coherent self-narrative","This is the neural basis for the rider in the rider/elephant metaphor"],"question":"If the interpreter module confabulates reasons for behavior caused by unknown factors, how can we trust any of our self-explanations?","correct_diagnosis":"We cannot fully trust them. The interpreter module is not designed for self-knowledge — it is designed to maintain a coherent self-narrative for social purposes. We need to explain our actions to others to maintain relationships and reputation. The interpreter evolved to serve this social function, not to give accurate introspective reports. This is why Nisbett and Wilson found that people cannot accurately report on their own cognitive processes. The rider tells a good story, but the elephant knows the real reasons.","distractors":["Only split-brain patients have an interpreter module — normal brains do not fabricate reasons","The interpreter module is specific to movement and does not affect moral or political reasoning","With enough introspection and honesty, people can access their true motivations"],"diagnostic_breakdown":"Stage 1 - Action: The right hemisphere initiates behavior based on environmental cues. Stage 2 - Observation: The left hemisphere observes the behavior. Stage 3 - Interpretation: The interpreter module generates a plausible-sounding explanation. Stage 4 - Belief: The person genuinely believes the explanation. Stage 5 - Application to Morality: When we have a moral intuition, the interpreter constructs post-hoc reasons. We believe the reasons caused the judgment, but in reality the judgment came first. The interpreter is the rider.","player_mission":"If the interpreter module confabulates reasons for behavior caused by unknown factors, how can we trust any of our self-explanations?","difficulty_level":"Intermediate","visual_frame_description":"A laboratory ledger with two columns - Seeing-That and Reasoning-Why; cards for the Muller-Lyer lines, the four cards E K 4 7, and 2-4-6 triplets slide into their slots while a rubber stamp descends with Wason's conclusion.","key_concepts":["Scott Murphy brought thirty UVA students into the lab, one at a time, for an extended interview, explaining that his job was to challenge their reasoning no matter what they said","In the Heinz dilemma, Scott couldn't whip up any dumbfounding, because people offered good reasons and would not abandon principles such as 'Life is more important than property'","In the 'roach juice' scenario, Scott opened a can of apple juice, then dipped a sterilized cockroach into the cup with a tea strainer and asked the subject to take a sip; 37 percent were willing","In the soul-selling scenario, Scott offered subjects $2 to sign a paper selling their soul to him after death, with a note that the form was not a legal or binding contract; only 23 percent signed without any goading","Scott convinced an extra 10 percent to sip the juice and an extra 17 percent to sign the soul-selling paper, but most people in both scenarios clung to their initial refusal","Many of the subjects who refused to sell their souls were atheists who didn't believe in souls and yet still felt uncomfortable about signing","In the Julie-and-Mark story, a brother and sister on summer vacation in France decide to make love, using birth control pills and a condom; only 20 percent of subjects said it was OK","In the Jennifer story, a vegetarian hospital worker who incinerates a fresh human cadaver cuts off a piece of flesh, cooks it, and eats it; only 13 percent said it was OK","In the harmless-taboo scenarios people generated far more reasons and discarded far more reasons than in any of the other scenarios, flailing around as Scott proved each reason irrelevant","One dumbfounded incest-story subject said: 'There's just no way I could change my mind but I just don't know how to - how to show what I'm feeling, what I feel about it. It's crazy!'","The quantified analyses showed big differences between responses to the harmless-taboo scenarios and responses to the Heinz dilemma","The results supported Hume, not Jefferson or Plato: people made moral judgments quickly and emotionally, and moral reasoning was mostly a post hoc search for reasons to justify judgments already made"]}]},{"id":"rm_ch02b","title":"Chapter 2: The Intuitive Dog and Its Rational Tail (Part 2)","pages":"52-71","assets":[{"asset_type":"CUE_SCRUBBER_STATION","domain":"Cognitive Psychology | Confirmation Bias","topic":"The Wason 4-Card Task: Why We Seek Confirming Evidence","visual_frame_description":"Four cards are placed on a table. Each card has a number on one side and a letter on the other. The visible faces show: E, K, 4, 7. The rule: If a card has a vowel on one side, then it has an even number on the other side. Which cards do you need to turn over to verify the rule? Most people choose E (the vowel) and 4 (the even number). But 4 cannot falsify the rule — only 7 can, because an odd number on the other side of 7 would break it. People instinctively seek confirming evidence and ignore falsifying evidence.","target_channel":"Confirmation Bias | Hypothesis Testing Failure","question":"Why do most people, including highly educated participants, fail to turn over the 7 card that could actually falsify the rule?","correct_cue":"The confirmation bias: people naturally seek evidence that confirms their hypothesis rather than evidence that could disconfirm it. The rider (conscious reasoning) searches for supporting evidence to justify the elephants intuitive belief. Wason designed this task to demonstrate that even in abstract logical problems, people do not think like scientists seeking falsification — they think like lawyers seeking supporting evidence. The rider is not a truth-seeker; it is a defense attorney for the elephants intuitions.","distractors":["The task is confusingly worded and participants misunderstand what the rule means","Participants are simply not paying enough attention to the logical structure of the problem","The 4 card is correct because it can confirm the rule by showing a vowel on the other side"],"underlying_psychology":"Wason 1966 and 1969. The confirmation bias is one of the most robust findings in cognitive psychology. Haidt uses it to show that moral reasoning is similarly biased. When we evaluate a moral claim, we do not dispassionately test all possibilities. We search for reasons that support our intuitive position and reject counterevidence. The rider is the press secretary, not the scientist. The 4-card task reveals something uncomfortable: even when we think we are being logical, we are being intuitive — the elephant decides which hypothesis feels right, and the rider finds evidence for it.","player_mission":"Why do most people, including highly educated participants, fail to turn over the 7 card that could actually falsify the rule?","difficulty_level":"Beginner","key_concepts":["Scott Murphy brought thirty UVA students into the lab, one at a time, for an extended interview, explaining that his job was to challenge their reasoning no matter what they said","In the Heinz dilemma, Scott couldn't whip up any dumbfounding, because people offered good reasons and would not abandon principles such as 'Life is more important than property'","In the 'roach juice' scenario, Scott opened a can of apple juice, then dipped a sterilized cockroach into the cup with a tea strainer and asked the subject to take a sip; 37 percent were willing","In the soul-selling scenario, Scott offered subjects $2 to sign a paper selling their soul to him after death, with a note that the form was not a legal or binding contract; only 23 percent signed without any goading","Scott convinced an extra 10 percent to sip the juice and an extra 17 percent to sign the soul-selling paper, but most people in both scenarios clung to their initial refusal","Many of the subjects who refused to sell their souls were atheists who didn't believe in souls and yet still felt uncomfortable about signing","In the Julie-and-Mark story, a brother and sister on summer vacation in France decide to make love, using birth control pills and a condom; only 20 percent of subjects said it was OK","In the Jennifer story, a vegetarian hospital worker who incinerates a fresh human cadaver cuts off a piece of flesh, cooks it, and eats it; only 13 percent said it was OK","In the harmless-taboo scenarios people generated far more reasons and discarded far more reasons than in any of the other scenarios, flailing around as Scott proved each reason irrelevant","One dumbfounded incest-story subject said: 'There's just no way I could change my mind but I just don't know how to - how to show what I'm feeling, what I feel about it. It's crazy!'","The quantified analyses showed big differences between responses to the harmless-taboo scenarios and responses to the Heinz dilemma","The results supported Hume, not Jefferson or Plato: people made moral judgments quickly and emotionally, and moral reasoning was mostly a post hoc search for reasons to justify judgments already made"]}]},{"id":"rm_ch03a","title":"Chapter 3: Elephants Rule (Part 1)","pages":"72-94","assets":[{"asset_type":"DECEPTION_AUDIT_FILE","domain":"Moral Psychology | Intuitive Primacy","topic":"The Social Intuitionist Model: How Moral Judgments Really Happen","subject_baseline":"A flash of disgust. A wave of admiration. A pang of guilt. Moral emotions fire instantly when we encounter situations. Within milliseconds, we have already judged: this is wrong, this is right, this person is good, that person is bad. The conscious mind then begins its search for reasons.","interrogation_transcript":"A woman is cleaning out her closet. She finds an old American flag. She does not want it anymore, so she cuts it into pieces and uses the rags to clean her toilet. Most Americans condemn this act immediately. When asked why, they cite respect for the flag, the sacrifices it represents, its sacred status. But if pressed: is anyone harmed by this? Can a piece of cloth be harmed? The reasoning fails. But the judgment stands. The elephant finds the act disgusting; the rider scrambles for post-hoc reasons.","observed_nonverbal_log":["Judgment occurs before conscious deliberation begins","Reasoning is recruited after the fact to justify the judgment","When reasoning fails to find justification, judgment persists unchanged","Social influence works primarily through changing intuitions, not through logical argument"],"question":"If reasoned arguments rarely change moral intuitions directly, how does moral change actually occur in individuals and societies?","correct_diagnosis":"According to the social intuitionist model, moral change happens through four pathways: (1) Intuitive change through social influence - being in a group of people with different intuitions gradually shifts your own via mimicry and emotional contagion. (2) Reasoning privately can sometimes trigger new intuitions - if you force yourself to consider a situation from another perspective, new intuitive flashes can occur. (3) Reasoned persuasion works only indirectly - your reasons can change someone elses intuitions, but only if they trigger new intuitions in that person. (4) Social pressure - people sometimes reason publicly to justify their intuitions, and the need to appear consistent can slowly reshape their position. The key: you cannot argue someone out of a position they did not reason themselves into.","distractors":["Moral change happens through better logic - people abandon positions when shown logical contradictions in their reasoning","Moral change happens through education and factual correction - ignorance is the root of moral disagreement","Moral change happens through punishment and reward - behavior modification eventually changes underlying beliefs"],"diagnostic_breakdown":"Stage 1 - Intuitive Flash: The elephants instant judgment. Stage 2 - Post-Hoc Reasoning: The rider constructs justifications. Stage 3 - Social Sharing: We share our judgments and reasons with others, spreading emotional contagion. Stage 4 - Social Pressure: We adjust our public positions to maintain relationships. Stage 5 - Possible Internalization: Repeated public positions can slowly shift private intuitions. The model has four links: (A) intuitive judgment, (B) post-hoc reasoning, (C) reasoned persuasion (which works by triggering new intuitions in others), and (D) social pressure. Reasoned persuasion (link C) is the weakest link. Social influence (link D) is the strongest.","player_mission":"If reasoned arguments rarely change moral intuitions directly, how does moral change actually occur in individuals and societies?","difficulty_level":"Intermediate","visual_frame_description":"A six-drawer evidence registry; findings arrive as case files - the fart spray canister, the hand sanitizer, the puppet stage, the fMRI slices, the psychopath's confession tape - and each is slotted under its numbered drawer before the seal drops.","key_concepts":["Thalia Wheatley hypnotized UVA students to feel a flash of disgust whenever they saw a certain word - take for half the subjects, often for the others - and instructed them while in a trance that they would not remember anything she had told them","Subjects judged six moral-violation stories as more disgusting and morally wrong when their code word was embedded, as when a congressman 'takes bribes from the tobacco lobby'","The seventh story had no moral violation: student council president Dan scheduling discussions between students and faculty, with 'tries to take topics that appeal to both professors and students' versus 'often picks topics'","Many subjects who felt the artificial flash of disgust still condemned Dan, making up absurd reasons like 'I don't know, it just seems like he's up to something'","Wilhelm Wundt, the founder of experimental psychology, formulated the doctrine of 'affective primacy' in the 1890s, holding that we find ourselves liking or disliking something the instant we notice it, sometimes even before we know what it is","Robert Zajonc's 1980 research had people rate Japanese pictograms, words in a made-up language, and geometric shapes, and showed that merely showing a word or image several times makes people like it more - the 'mere exposure effect'","Zajonc said affect has primacy both because it happens first and because it is more powerful, and that affective reactions act like blinders that 'reduce the universe of alternatives' available to later thinking","In affective priming, after the first word hate is flashed, the positive word sunshine takes about 250 milliseconds longer to categorize than cancer, because you have to undo the lean toward negativity","The Implicit Association Test, developed by Tony Greenwald, Mahzarin Banaji, and Brian Nosek, shows that most people turn out to have negative implicit associations with groups such as black people, immigrants, obese people, and the elderly","Jamie Morris measured the brain waves of liberals and conservatives reading words like Clinton, Bush, flag, taxes, welfare, and pro-life: pro-life plus sunshine was affectively incongruous for liberals, just as Clinton plus sunshine was for conservatives","Alexander Todorov found that the candidate judged more competent from photographs won U.S. Senate and House races about two-thirds of the time","When Todorov forced people to judge competence after flashing the pair of pictures for just a tenth of a second, the snap judgments predicted the real outcomes just as well"]},{"asset_type":"CUE_SCRUBBER_STATION","domain":"Moral Psychology | Affect Heuristic | Disgust","topic":"The Disgust Amplifier: How Incidental Emotions Hijack Moral Judgment","visual_frame_description":"Participants sit at a desk. Unbeknownst to them, the desk has been sprayed with a foul-smelling fart spray. Other participants watch a disgusting film clip (a toilet scene from Trainspotting). A third group sits in a clean room. All then read about a man who does various morally questionable but not clearly harmful things (keeping a found wallet, using a condom, eating his dead dog). The disgust-primed participants judge these actions as significantly more morally wrong than the clean-room participants do.","target_channel":"Emotional Contamination | Incidental Affect in Moral Judgment","question":"Why would the smell of a room or a disgusting film clip change peoples moral judgments about unrelated actions?","correct_cue":"The affect heuristic: people use their current emotional state as information when making judgments. If I feel disgust right now, and I am reading about a morally ambiguous action, the disgust gets misattributed to the action - this feels disgusting, so it must be wrong. The emotion amplifies the intuitive judgment. This is why moral panics often involve disgust-inducing acts - disgust is a powerful amplifier that makes moderate violations feel monstrous. The contamination is automatic and unconscious - participants genuinely believe the action itself caused their disgust.","distractors":["The disgusting environment makes people more irritable and therefore harsher in all their judgments across the board","The priming effect only works for taboo violations and does not affect judgments about harm or fairness","Participants overcorrected for the disgusting environment and became more lenient instead"],"underlying_psychology":"Schnall, Haidt, Clore, and Jordan 2008 disgust-into-judgment studies. The finding demonstrates embodied cognition and the affect-as-information theory (Schwarz and Clore). Moral judgment is not pure cognition operating on objective facts. It is deeply influenced by bodily states, incidental emotions, and environmental cues. The practical implication: judges and jurors should not be making decisions on full stomachs or in physically uncomfortable environments. The disgust you feel from a gruesome crime scene photo may contaminate your judgment of the defendants entire character, not just your assessment of the crime.","player_mission":"Why would the smell of a room or a disgusting film clip change peoples moral judgments about unrelated actions?","difficulty_level":"Beginner","key_concepts":["Thalia Wheatley hypnotized UVA students to feel a flash of disgust whenever they saw a certain word - take for half the subjects, often for the others - and instructed them while in a trance that they would not remember anything she had told them","Subjects judged six moral-violation stories as more disgusting and morally wrong when their code word was embedded, as when a congressman 'takes bribes from the tobacco lobby'","The seventh story had no moral violation: student council president Dan scheduling discussions between students and faculty, with 'tries to take topics that appeal to both professors and students' versus 'often picks topics'","Many subjects who felt the artificial flash of disgust still condemned Dan, making up absurd reasons like 'I don't know, it just seems like he's up to something'","Wilhelm Wundt, the founder of experimental psychology, formulated the doctrine of 'affective primacy' in the 1890s, holding that we find ourselves liking or disliking something the instant we notice it, sometimes even before we know what it is","Robert Zajonc's 1980 research had people rate Japanese pictograms, words in a made-up language, and geometric shapes, and showed that merely showing a word or image several times makes people like it more - the 'mere exposure effect'","Zajonc said affect has primacy both because it happens first and because it is more powerful, and that affective reactions act like blinders that 'reduce the universe of alternatives' available to later thinking","In affective priming, after the first word hate is flashed, the positive word sunshine takes about 250 milliseconds longer to categorize than cancer, because you have to undo the lean toward negativity","The Implicit Association Test, developed by Tony Greenwald, Mahzarin Banaji, and Brian Nosek, shows that most people turn out to have negative implicit associations with groups such as black people, immigrants, obese people, and the elderly","Jamie Morris measured the brain waves of liberals and conservatives reading words like Clinton, Bush, flag, taxes, welfare, and pro-life: pro-life plus sunshine was affectively incongruous for liberals, just as Clinton plus sunshine was for conservatives","Alexander Todorov found that the candidate judged more competent from photographs won U.S. Senate and House races about two-thirds of the time","When Todorov forced people to judge competence after flashing the pair of pictures for just a tenth of a second, the snap judgments predicted the real outcomes just as well"]},{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Moral Psychology | Intuitive Change | Moral Reframing","topic":"How to Change a Moral Intuition: Appealing to the Elephant Directly","stage_1_observation":"Frimer, Skitka, and Motyl 2017: Environmental messages framed in purely economic terms (saving money on energy bills) consistently failed to change conservatives environmental behavior. But messages framed as protecting natural resources for future generations and preserving the purity of national parks (loyalty and sanctity foundations) succeeded. The facts were the same. Only the framing changed.","stage_1_question":"Why does the same factual information change behavior under one moral frame but not another?","stage_1_correct_answer":"The elephants attention is captured by moral-emotional cues, not by abstract reasoning. An economic frame activates the care foundation for liberals (caring about future generations harmed by climate change) but may not resonate with conservatives whose moral matrix prioritizes loyalty, authority, and sanctity. When the message is reframed to activate those foundations - protecting the purity of sacred national lands, showing patriotic loyalty to the country - the elephants ears perk up. The rider then finds reasons to support action, but the elephant was the one that decided to move.","stage_2_intervention":"You are trying to convince a conservative family member that same-sex marriage should be legal. Your first instinct is to argue about love is love, equal rights, and the harm of discrimination (care/harm foundation). They are not moved. According to the moral foundations approach, how should you reframe your argument?","stage_2_correct_answer":"Use the loyalty and authority foundations: (1) Loyalty - same-sex couples make committed, loyal partners who strengthen the institution of marriage. Marriage is about fidelity and commitment. Allowing them to marry strengthens the social fabric. (2) Authority - the conservative legal tradition of equal protection under the law and the authority of long-term committed relationships. (3) Liberty - the conservative value of people being free to make their own life choices without government interference in private matters. Do not use the care/harm frame that triggers defensiveness from the elephants fear of being morally lectured. Speak to the elephants existing moral concerns.","boss_battle_explanation":"Layer 1 - Moral Reframing: Change the moral foundation the message activates, not the facts. Layer 2 - Six Foundations: Care, Fairness, Loyalty, Authority, Sanctity, Liberty. Liberals prioritize Care and Fairness; conservatives engage all six. Layer 3 - Elephant Targeting: Speak to the elephants emotional-moral intuitions, not to the riders logic. Layer 4 - The Golden Rule of Moral Persuasion: If you want to change someones mind, first figure out which moral foundations matter to them, then show how your position serves those foundations. Your own foundations will not work on them - only theirs will.","player_mission":"","difficulty_level":"Intermediate","visual_frame_description":"A laboratory puppet theater on a hill of felt: the climber trembles on the slope, the helper nudges from below, the hinderer bashes from the top; in the audience, infants in car seats stare, and on a monitor a countdown clock ticks toward a two-minute judgment that may let reason back in.","key_concepts":["Thalia Wheatley hypnotized UVA students to feel a flash of disgust whenever they saw a certain word - take for half the subjects, often for the others - and instructed them while in a trance that they would not remember anything she had told them","Subjects judged six moral-violation stories as more disgusting and morally wrong when their code word was embedded, as when a congressman 'takes bribes from the tobacco lobby'","The seventh story had no moral violation: student council president Dan scheduling discussions between students and faculty, with 'tries to take topics that appeal to both professors and students' versus 'often picks topics'","Many subjects who felt the artificial flash of disgust still condemned Dan, making up absurd reasons like 'I don't know, it just seems like he's up to something'","Wilhelm Wundt, the founder of experimental psychology, formulated the doctrine of 'affective primacy' in the 1890s, holding that we find ourselves liking or disliking something the instant we notice it, sometimes even before we know what it is","Robert Zajonc's 1980 research had people rate Japanese pictograms, words in a made-up language, and geometric shapes, and showed that merely showing a word or image several times makes people like it more - the 'mere exposure effect'","Zajonc said affect has primacy both because it happens first and because it is more powerful, and that affective reactions act like blinders that 'reduce the universe of alternatives' available to later thinking","In affective priming, after the first word hate is flashed, the positive word sunshine takes about 250 milliseconds longer to categorize than cancer, because you have to undo the lean toward negativity","The Implicit Association Test, developed by Tony Greenwald, Mahzarin Banaji, and Brian Nosek, shows that most people turn out to have negative implicit associations with groups such as black people, immigrants, obese people, and the elderly","Jamie Morris measured the brain waves of liberals and conservatives reading words like Clinton, Bush, flag, taxes, welfare, and pro-life: pro-life plus sunshine was affectively incongruous for liberals, just as Clinton plus sunshine was for conservatives","Alexander Todorov found that the candidate judged more competent from photographs won U.S. Senate and House races about two-thirds of the time","When Todorov forced people to judge competence after flashing the pair of pictures for just a tenth of a second, the snap judgments predicted the real outcomes just as well"]}]},{"id":"rm_ch03b","title":"Chapter 3: Elephants Rule (Part 2)","pages":"72-94","assets":[{"asset_type":"CUE_SCRUBBER_STATION","domain":"Cognitive Psychology | Disconfirmation","topic":"The Confirmation Bias in Moral Reasoning: Why We Are All Bad Scientists","visual_frame_description":"Two groups read about a proposed policy. Group A is told: this will increase efficiency by 15%. Group B is told: this will reduce costs by 15%. Both groups generate reasons to support the policy. Then they are told the policy will also require layoffs. Group A weighs the layoff cost against efficiency. Group B weighs it against cost savings. Both groups still support the policy, but use different weights depending on which benefit they heard first. The order of information determines which reason they emphasize, but they believe their reasoning was objective.","target_channel":"Myside Bias | Motivated Reasoning in Evaluation","question":"Why does the order of information presentation systematically change which reasons people cite, even though everyone has the same total information?","correct_cue":"The myside bias (confirmation bias applied to reasoning): people generate reasons to support their already-formed position. The first piece of information triggers an intuitive leaning, and subsequent information is evaluated through that lens. Supporting evidence is weighted heavily; contradicting evidence is scrutinized and dismissed. This is not conscious dishonesty — it is how the elephant works. The rider (interpreter module) searches for reasons that fit the elephants direction, not reasons that would challenge it. The order effect proves the rider is post-hoc, not independent.","distractors":["The first information is simply more memorable and therefore more influential in conscious deliberation","People are aware of the order effect but choose to overweight first information due to primacy bias","The effect disappears when participants are explicitly warned about the order manipulation"],"underlying_psychology":"Haidt synthesizes the Wason task (1966), the myside bias (Perkins 1985, Baron 1995), and motivated reasoning (Kunda 1990, Westen 2006) to show that the human mind is designed for confirmation, not objectivity. When we reason about morality, politics, or any emotionally charged topic, we do not weigh evidence impartially. We search for supporting evidence and generate counterarguments against opposing evidence. The rider can change direction — but only if the elephant is willing. New intuitions (from social influence, personal experience, or compelling narratives) can shift the elephant. Arguments alone rarely do.","player_mission":"Why does the order of information presentation systematically change which reasons people cite, even though everyone has the same total information?","difficulty_level":"Beginner","key_concepts":["Thalia Wheatley hypnotized UVA students to feel a flash of disgust whenever they saw a certain word - take for half the subjects, often for the others - and instructed them while in a trance that they would not remember anything she had told them","Subjects judged six moral-violation stories as more disgusting and morally wrong when their code word was embedded, as when a congressman 'takes bribes from the tobacco lobby'","The seventh story had no moral violation: student council president Dan scheduling discussions between students and faculty, with 'tries to take topics that appeal to both professors and students' versus 'often picks topics'","Many subjects who felt the artificial flash of disgust still condemned Dan, making up absurd reasons like 'I don't know, it just seems like he's up to something'","Wilhelm Wundt, the founder of experimental psychology, formulated the doctrine of 'affective primacy' in the 1890s, holding that we find ourselves liking or disliking something the instant we notice it, sometimes even before we know what it is","Robert Zajonc's 1980 research had people rate Japanese pictograms, words in a made-up language, and geometric shapes, and showed that merely showing a word or image several times makes people like it more - the 'mere exposure effect'","Zajonc said affect has primacy both because it happens first and because it is more powerful, and that affective reactions act like blinders that 'reduce the universe of alternatives' available to later thinking","In affective priming, after the first word hate is flashed, the positive word sunshine takes about 250 milliseconds longer to categorize than cancer, because you have to undo the lean toward negativity","The Implicit Association Test, developed by Tony Greenwald, Mahzarin Banaji, and Brian Nosek, shows that most people turn out to have negative implicit associations with groups such as black people, immigrants, obese people, and the elderly","Jamie Morris measured the brain waves of liberals and conservatives reading words like Clinton, Bush, flag, taxes, welfare, and pro-life: pro-life plus sunshine was affectively incongruous for liberals, just as Clinton plus sunshine was for conservatives","Alexander Todorov found that the candidate judged more competent from photographs won U.S. Senate and House races about two-thirds of the time","When Todorov forced people to judge competence after flashing the pair of pictures for just a tenth of a second, the snap judgments predicted the real outcomes just as well"]}]},{"id":"rm_ch04","title":"Chapter 4: Vote for Me (Here's Why)","pages":"95-128","assets":[{"asset_type":"DECEPTION_AUDIT_FILE","domain":"Political Psychology | Motivated Reasoning","topic":"The Rational Voter Myth: Motivated Reasoning in Political Judgment","subject_baseline":"Westen 2006: Partisan Democrats and Republicans watched video clips of their preferred candidate and the opposing candidate making contradictory statements. Brain scans revealed that reasoning about the opposing candidates contradictions activated cognitive areas. Reasoning about their own candidate's contradictions activated emotion areas.","interrogation_transcript":"When participants processed their own candidate making a contradictory statement, the emotional centers lit up (pleasure circuits, like reward). Reasoning centers showed minimal activation. Participants did not objectively evaluate the contradiction - they generated exculpatory explanations. When the other candidate did the same thing, reasoning centers activated and participants judged the contradiction as evidence of dishonesty. The brain was not seeking truth. The brain was defending the team.","observed_nonverbal_log":["Partisan identity activates before conscious political reasoning begins","The same objective contradiction is judged differently based on team affiliation","Emotional reward centers activate when finding excuses for the in-group","Reasoning centers activate only for out-group contradictions"],"question":"If both Democrats and Republicans are equally guilty of motivated reasoning, how can any voter arrive at objective political judgments?","correct_diagnosis":"They cannot. Motivated reasoning is not a bug in the political mind - it is a feature. Our political beliefs are not conclusions we reach after dispassionate analysis. They are identity markers that signal group membership. When we reason about politics, we are not trying to find truth. We are trying to defend our teams reputation and our own identity as a good team member. The rider (reason) serves the elephant (intuition), and the elephants primary political goal is not accuracy but belonging. The cure is not to eliminate motivated reasoning (impossible) but to expose ourselves to different moral matrices and understand that we are all doing it.","distractors":["Higher education and intelligence reduce partisan bias - smarter people evaluate evidence more objectively","Motivated reasoning only affects uneducated voters who lack critical thinking skills","Non-partisan independents and swing voters are immune to motivated reasoning"],"diagnostic_breakdown":"Stage 1 - Team Identity: I am a Democrat/Republican becomes part of self-concept. Stage 2 - Information Threat: Evidence that hurts my team threatens my identity. Stage 3 - Motivated Reasoning: The elephant triggers defense mode. Stage 4 - Explanation Generation: The rider generates counterarguments, alternative interpretations, and attacks on the source. Stage 5 - Convincing the Self: The rider believes its own post-hoc rationalizations. The neural evidence shows this is not conscious hypocrisy - the brain literally processes the same information differently based on team allegiance. We are not lying about our objectivity. We genuinely believe in our own rational assessment because the rider has no access to the elephants motivational state.","player_mission":"If both Democrats and Republicans are equally guilty of motivated reasoning, how can any voter arrive at objective political judgments?","difficulty_level":"Intermediate","visual_frame_description":"A Parliament-style ledger room where expense claims for moat dredging are stamped 'Ring of Gyges,' slips of overpaid cash are sorted by whether the cashier asked, and a sociometer gauge on the wall dips with every declining number from 4 to 2.","key_concepts":["In Wason's 1960 '2-4-6 problem,' subjects saw the triplet 2-4-6 and generated test triplets, but what they hardly ever did was test their hypotheses by offering triplets that did not conform to them","Proposing 2-4-5 (yes) and 2-4-3 (no) would have helped subjects zero in on the actual rule - any series of ascending numbers - but instead people kept confirming their guesses","Wason called this phenomenon the confirmation bias: the tendency to seek out and interpret new evidence in ways that confirm what you already think","Deanna Kuhn's sets of eight index cards showed a cartoon child eating chocolate cake or carrot cake and then smiling or looking sick; people usually concluded the evidence proved their hunch that chocolate cake makes kids sick, even when the cards showed a stronger association for carrot cake","Kuhn's subjects seemed to think: 'Here is some evidence I can point to as supporting my theory, and therefore the theory is right'","David Perkins asked people of various ages and education levels whether giving schools more money would improve teaching and learning, and found the more education subjects had, the more reasons they came up with","Perkins found barely any improvement in argument quality within each school from first-year to fourth-year students - schools don't teach people to reason thoroughly, they select the applicants with higher IQs","IQ was by far the biggest predictor of how well people argued, but it predicted only the number of my-side arguments - smart people make good lawyers but are no better at finding reasons on the other side","Perkins concluded that 'people invest their IQ in buttressing their own case rather than in exploring the entire issue more fully and evenhandedly'","Tetlock's research distinguishes exploratory thought, the 'evenhanded consideration of alternative points of view,' from confirmatory thought, 'a one-sided attempt to rationalize a particular point of view'","Accountability increases exploratory thought only when people learn before forming an opinion that they will be accountable to an audience, the audience's views are unknown, and the audience is believed to be well informed and interested in accuracy","The rest of the time, accountability pressures simply increase confirmatory thought - people are trying harder to look right than to be right"]},{"asset_type":"DYNAMIC_DIALOGUE_SIM","domain":"Political Psychology | Moral Reframing","topic":"The Cross-Party Conversation: Speaking Across Moral Matrices","dialogue":[{"speaker":"Liberal Friend","line":"How can you support a candidate who cut funding for food stamps? Those are the most vulnerable people in our society - children, elderly, disabled. We have a moral obligation to care for them."},{"speaker":"Conservative Friend","line":"I care about vulnerable people too, but food stamps create dependency. The best way to help people is to create jobs and economic growth. When the government hands out checks, it traps people in poverty. My candidate cuts regulations to let businesses hire more people."},{"speaker":"You (Mediator)","line":"Youre both using the Care foundation - you just disagree about what causes harm. The liberal friend sees harm in cutting benefits. The conservative friend sees harm in dependency. But notice: the conservative friend did not mention Fairness or Liberty. A better conservative response would be: I am for liberty and personal responsibility. Government assistance can trap people in a cycle of dependency, violating the principle that people should earn their own way through hard work. That activates Fairness (as proportionality) and Liberty (as freedom from government control). Understanding the moral foundation being used helps explain why factual arguments often fail. You are not disagreeing about facts - you are disagreeing about which moral principle takes priority."}],"psychological_breakdown":"Haidt argues that each side occupies a different moral matrix. Liberals have a three-foundation morality (Care, Fairness/equality, Liberty). Conservatives have a six-foundation morality (all of the above plus Loyalty, Authority, Sanctity, and Fairness/proportionality). The same policy triggers different foundations in different people. A liberal who argues purely from Care will not reach a conservative whose Care concern is overridden by Authority (government overreach) and Sanctity (tradition). The solution is not to abandon your own foundations but to learn to translate your position into the other sides language.","player_mission":"","difficulty_level":"Intermediate","visual_frame_description":"A marble hall in The Republic: on one finger the gold ring of Gyges glints as a shadow-self raids the marketplace and enters houses, while a scale weighs Being against Seeming and the ghost of Glaucon demands proof that justice itself, not its reputation, brings happiness.","key_concepts":["In Wason's 1960 '2-4-6 problem,' subjects saw the triplet 2-4-6 and generated test triplets, but what they hardly ever did was test their hypotheses by offering triplets that did not conform to them","Proposing 2-4-5 (yes) and 2-4-3 (no) would have helped subjects zero in on the actual rule - any series of ascending numbers - but instead people kept confirming their guesses","Wason called this phenomenon the confirmation bias: the tendency to seek out and interpret new evidence in ways that confirm what you already think","Deanna Kuhn's sets of eight index cards showed a cartoon child eating chocolate cake or carrot cake and then smiling or looking sick; people usually concluded the evidence proved their hunch that chocolate cake makes kids sick, even when the cards showed a stronger association for carrot cake","Kuhn's subjects seemed to think: 'Here is some evidence I can point to as supporting my theory, and therefore the theory is right'","David Perkins asked people of various ages and education levels whether giving schools more money would improve teaching and learning, and found the more education subjects had, the more reasons they came up with","Perkins found barely any improvement in argument quality within each school from first-year to fourth-year students - schools don't teach people to reason thoroughly, they select the applicants with higher IQs","IQ was by far the biggest predictor of how well people argued, but it predicted only the number of my-side arguments - smart people make good lawyers but are no better at finding reasons on the other side","Perkins concluded that 'people invest their IQ in buttressing their own case rather than in exploring the entire issue more fully and evenhandedly'","Tetlock's research distinguishes exploratory thought, the 'evenhanded consideration of alternative points of view,' from confirmatory thought, 'a one-sided attempt to rationalize a particular point of view'","Accountability increases exploratory thought only when people learn before forming an opinion that they will be accountable to an audience, the audience's views are unknown, and the audience is believed to be well informed and interested in accuracy","The rest of the time, accountability pressures simply increase confirmatory thought - people are trying harder to look right than to be right"]}]},{"id":"rm_ch05","title":"Chapter 5: Beyond WEIRD Morality","pages":"128-154","assets":[{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Cultural Psychology | Moral Diversity","topic":"WEIRD vs The Rest: How Western Psychology Misrepresents Universal Morality","column_a_label":"WEIRD Morality - Western, Educated, Industrialized, Rich, Democratic. Emphasizes harm, rights, and individual autonomy. Analytic thinking, universal rules, guilt culture.","column_b_label":"Traditional/Eastern Morality - Emphasizes community, duty, hierarchy, and purity. Holistic thinking, context-sensitive rules, shame culture.","rows":[{"scenario":"A Brazilian participant says incest between consenting adults is wrong, but they cannot explain why","correct_column":"B","reason":"Non-WEIRD participants rely on intuitive disgust/purity rather than individual rights - the act violates sanctity regardless of consent"},{"scenario":"An American says consensual incest between adults is not wrong because no one is harmed","correct_column":"A","reason":"WEIRD participants apply the harm principle - if no individual is harmed, the act is not immoral"},{"scenario":"An Indian villager says respecting elders is the most important moral virtue for children to learn","correct_column":"B","reason":"Non-WEIRD cultures prioritize hierarchy and authority as moral goods, not as oppressive systems"},{"scenario":"A European says it is immoral to charge interest on loans because it exploits the poor","correct_column":"B","reason":"Though Western, this reflects a community-binding fairness concern more common in non-WEIRD cultures"},{"scenario":"An American graduate student in psychology volunteers for a study on moral judgment","correct_column":"A","reason":"WEIRD populations are the most individualistic, analytical, and rights-focused - and 96% of psychology studies use them"}],"explanation":"Henrich, Heine, and Norenzayan 2010 showed that 96% of psychological subjects come from WEIRD societies, which are outliers on many psychological dimensions including moral reasoning. WEIRD people are more individualistic, less conforming, more analytic in thinking style, and more focused on harm/rights-based morality. The standard story of moral development (Kohlberg) with justice as the highest stage is a WEIRD-centric view that pathologizes non-WEIRD moralities. Haidts Moral Foundations Theory expands the moral domain beyond harm and fairness to include loyalty, authority, and sanctity - concerns that non-WEIRD cultures treat as central.","player_mission":"","difficulty_level":"Advanced","visual_frame_description":"A sorting chamber where a square containing a line is projected onto two frames, one absolute and one relative; twin chutes labeled Analytic and Holistic swallow scenes while a banner reads 'Moral matrices bind people together and blind them to the coherence, or even existence, of other matrices.'","key_concepts":["Haidt recruited working-class adults standing outside a McDonald's in West Philadelphia for his dissertation research and asked them about a family that ate its dog, a woman who used her flag as a rag, and a man and his chicken","When Haidt asked 'Can you tell me why that was wrong?', the working-class subjects often answered with long pauses and disbelieving stares that seemed to say: 'You mean you don't know why it's wrong to do that to a chicken? What planet are you from?'","The Penn students were the most unusual of all twelve groups in Haidt's study, unique in their unwavering devotion to the harm principle put forth by John Stuart Mill in 1859","Mill's harm principle reads: 'The only purpose for which power can be rightfully exercised over any member of a civilized community, against his will, is to prevent harm to others'","As one Penn student said of the chicken story, 'It's his chicken, he's eating it, nobody is getting hurt,' while another said, 'It's perverted, but if it's done in private, it's his right'","The Penn students were the only group in which a majority (73 percent) tolerated the chicken story, yet they were just as likely as the other eleven groups to say the taboo violations would bother them","In 2010 the cultural psychologists Joe Henrich, Steve Heine, and Ara Norenzayan published 'The Weirdest People in the World?', pointing out that psychology's subjects are nearly all WEIRD: Western, educated, industrialized, rich, and democratic","WEIRD people are statistical outliers - the least typical, least representative people you could study - with Americans more extreme outliers than Europeans and the educated upper middle class the most unusual of all","In the framed-line task, Westerners (particularly Americans) excel at the absolute version because they saw the line as an independent object, while East Asians outperform at the relative version because they perceived the relationship among the parts","Most people think holistically, seeing the whole context and the relationships among parts, whereas WEIRD people think analytically, detaching the focal object from its context and assigning it to a category","WEIRD philosophers since Kant and Mill have generated moral systems that are individualistic, rule-based, and universalist, whereas Confucius's Analects is a collection of aphorisms and anecdotes that cannot be reduced to a single rule","The second principle of moral psychology is that there's more to morality than harm and fairness - true descriptively, as a portrait of the moralities we see when we look around the world"]},{"asset_type":"CUE_SCRUBBER_STATION","domain":"Cultural Psychology | Autonomy vs Community","topic":"The Three Ethics: Autonomy, Community, Divinity","visual_frame_description":"Shweder 1997 described three ethics across cultures. The Ethics of Autonomy: individuals are free agents whose rights and preferences should not be violated. The Ethics of Community: individuals are embedded in roles, families, and hierarchies; duty and loyalty are paramount. The Ethics of Divinity: individuals are temporary vessels of a sacred soul; purity and sanctity are paramount. WEIRD cultures focus almost exclusively on Autonomy. Most other cultures use all three.","target_channel":"Cross-Cultural Moral Blindness","question":"When a Western human rights advocate encounters a culture that practices honor killings, which moral frames are they using and which are they missing?","correct_cue":"The Western advocate uses only the Ethics of Autonomy: the individual woman has a right to life, liberty, and sexual choice. The practicing culture uses the Ethics of Community (the woman brought dishonor on her family, threatening its standing and survival) and the Ethics of Divinity (her sexual purity is sacred). Both sides see the other as morally deficient. The advocate sees murder and oppression. The community sees the advocate as failing to understand the sacred obligations of family honor. Understanding Shweders three ethics does not require accepting honor killings - but it explains why purely autonomy-based arguments fail to persuade.","distractors":["The practicing culture simply does not value human life as much as Western cultures do","The practice is purely about male control of female bodies with no moral dimension","The honor killing is universally recognized as murder within the culture but is justified through denial"],"underlying_psychology":"Shweders three ethics map onto Haidts moral foundations: Autonomy maps to Care and Liberty, Community maps to Loyalty and Authority, Divinity maps to Sanctity. The Wests narrowing to Autonomy alone is historically unusual. Most human societies throughout history used all three ethics to structure moral life. The implication: when WEIRD people try to export their moral values, they are often baffled by resistance because they fail to see that they are asking people to abandon their entire moral framework.","player_mission":"When a Western human rights advocate encounters a culture that practices honor killings, which moral frames are they using and which are they missing?","difficulty_level":"Beginner","key_concepts":["Haidt recruited working-class adults standing outside a McDonald's in West Philadelphia for his dissertation research and asked them about a family that ate its dog, a woman who used her flag as a rag, and a man and his chicken","When Haidt asked 'Can you tell me why that was wrong?', the working-class subjects often answered with long pauses and disbelieving stares that seemed to say: 'You mean you don't know why it's wrong to do that to a chicken? What planet are you from?'","The Penn students were the most unusual of all twelve groups in Haidt's study, unique in their unwavering devotion to the harm principle put forth by John Stuart Mill in 1859","Mill's harm principle reads: 'The only purpose for which power can be rightfully exercised over any member of a civilized community, against his will, is to prevent harm to others'","As one Penn student said of the chicken story, 'It's his chicken, he's eating it, nobody is getting hurt,' while another said, 'It's perverted, but if it's done in private, it's his right'","The Penn students were the only group in which a majority (73 percent) tolerated the chicken story, yet they were just as likely as the other eleven groups to say the taboo violations would bother them","In 2010 the cultural psychologists Joe Henrich, Steve Heine, and Ara Norenzayan published 'The Weirdest People in the World?', pointing out that psychology's subjects are nearly all WEIRD: Western, educated, industrialized, rich, and democratic","WEIRD people are statistical outliers - the least typical, least representative people you could study - with Americans more extreme outliers than Europeans and the educated upper middle class the most unusual of all","In the framed-line task, Westerners (particularly Americans) excel at the absolute version because they saw the line as an independent object, while East Asians outperform at the relative version because they perceived the relationship among the parts","Most people think holistically, seeing the whole context and the relationships among parts, whereas WEIRD people think analytically, detaching the focal object from its context and assigning it to a category","WEIRD philosophers since Kant and Mill have generated moral systems that are individualistic, rule-based, and universalist, whereas Confucius's Analects is a collection of aphorisms and anecdotes that cannot be reduced to a single rule","The second principle of moral psychology is that there's more to morality than harm and fairness - true descriptively, as a portrait of the moralities we see when we look around the world"]}]},{"id":"rm_ch06","title":"Chapter 6: Taste Buds of the Righteous Mind","pages":"155-188","assets":[{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Moral Psychology | Moral Foundations Theory","topic":"The Six Moral Foundations: The Taste Buds of the Righteous Mind","column_a_label":"Each foundation is an evolved module for detecting certain patterns in the social world and generating appropriate emotional responses.","column_b_label":"Foundations vary across individuals and cultures - some are more sensitive, like taste buds that differ in strength.","rows":[{"scenario":"A child is abused by a parent. People across all cultures condemn this instantly.","correct_foundation":"Care/Harm","description":"The Care foundation detects suffering and vulnerability in offspring and generates compassion and protection responses. It is the most universal foundation, found in nearly all humans."},{"scenario":"Two people do equal work but one is paid twice as much for no reason.","correct_foundation":"Fairness/Cheating","description":"The Fairness foundation detects cheating, free-riding, and unequal treatment. It generates anger at cheaters and gratitude toward fair players. It evolved in the context of reciprocal altruism."},{"scenario":"A soldier throws himself on a grenade to save his comrades.","correct_foundation":"Loyalty/Betrayal","description":"The Loyalty foundation detects signs of coalitional commitment and betrayal. It generates pride in loyalty and righteous anger at traitors. It evolved from the challenges of intergroup competition."},{"scenario":"A teenager publicly mocks and insults the principal of their school.","correct_foundation":"Authority/Subversion","description":"The Authority foundation detects status hierarchies, leadership, and respect for legitimate authority. It generates respect, deference, and awe when authority is properly exercised - anger and contempt when it is subverted."},{"scenario":"A person sells their body to be eaten after death, or makes art from human blood.","correct_foundation":"Sanctity/Degradation","description":"The Sanctity foundation detects physical and spiritual contamination. It generates disgust toward degradation and awe toward purity. It evolved from the need to avoid pathogens and parasites, then extended to moral purity."}],"explanation":"Haidt identifies six innate moral foundations that are the taste buds of the righteous mind. Each foundation has an adaptive challenge it addresses, characteristic emotions it generates, and relevant virtues it supports. The foundations are not deterministic - they are like a first draft that gets revised by experience. Cultures vary in which foundations they emphasize, creating different moral matrices. A sixth foundation, Liberty/Oppression, was later added - detecting signs of domination and generating reactance against bullies.","player_mission":"","difficulty_level":"Advanced","visual_frame_description":"A pantry of shelves labeled with the five foundations; cards shaped like taste buds slide into slots while a sixth empty shelf waits, labeled Liberty/oppression, to be filled in chapter 8.","key_concepts":["The True Taste restaurant had an entirely white interior, each table set only with five small spoons, and a menu divided into sections labeled 'Sugars,' 'Honeys,' 'Tree Saps,' and 'Artificials'","The waiter, who was also the owner and sole employee, was a biologist who specialized in the sense of taste and ran the first tasting bar for sweeteners in the world, with samples from thirty-two countries","The five kinds of taste receptor found in each taste bud on the tongue are sweet, sour, salty, bitter, and savory (also called umami)","The owner's research had shown that activation of the sweet receptor produced the strongest surge of dopamine, indicating humans are hard-wired to seek sweetness above the other four tastes","Business was 'Terrible,' he said, 'but at least I'm doing better than the chemist down the street who opened a salt-tasting bar'","Many authors reduce morality to a single principle, usually welfare maximization, or to justice and notions of fairness, rights, and respect for autonomy - The Utilitarian Grill serves only sweeteners (welfare) and The Deontological Diner serves only salts (rights)","Moral monism - the attempt to ground all of morality on a single principle - leads to societies that are unsatisfying to most people and at high risk of becoming inhumane because they ignore so many other moral principles","The Chinese sage Mencius made the analogy between morality and food 2,300 years ago, writing that 'moral principles please our minds as beef and mutton and pork please our mouths'","The righteous mind is like a tongue with six taste receptors, and morality is like cuisine - a cultural construction influenced by accidents of environment and history, but not so flexible that anything goes","You can't have a cuisine based on tree bark, nor one based primarily on bitter tastes; moral matrices vary, but they all must please righteous minds equipped with the same six social receptors","Just knowing that everyone has sweetness receptors can't tell you why one person prefers Thai food to Mexican or why hardly anyone stirs sugar into beer - connecting the universal receptors to particular judgments takes a lot of additional work","Hume thought morality was based in a variety of sentiments, so he relied on sensory analogies: moral judgment is a kind of perception, and moral science should begin with a careful study of the moral taste receptors"]},{"asset_type":"CUE_SCRUBBER_STATION","domain":"Moral Psychology | Sanctity | Disgust","topic":"The Sanctity Foundation: From Physical Disgust to Moral Purity","visual_frame_description":"Rozin 1999: A brand new, sterile, never-used bedpan. Would you drink soup from it? Almost everyone says no. A piece of fudge shaped like dog feces. Would you eat it? Almost everyone says no, even knowing it is fudge. A sweater worn by Adolf Hitler. Would you wear it? Almost everyone says no. The mind treats physical, moral, and spiritual purity as equivalent - the same disgust response that evolved to protect us from pathogens now protects our moral and spiritual purity.","target_channel":"Purity-Disgust Generalization | Sacred Values","question":"Why do people react with disgust and rejection to perfectly clean objects that have merely been associated with contamination?","correct_cue":"The laws of sympathetic magic: contagion (once in contact, always in contact) and similarity (the image shares the qualities of the thing it represents). These are not bugs in cognition - they are features of the Sanctity foundation. The disgust system evolved to protect the body from pathogens, but it was co-opted by the moral system to protect the soul from spiritual contamination. This is why people describe moral violations as disgusting, why political rhetoric uses purity language, and why cultural rules about food, sex, and the body are so emotionally charged.","distractors":["People are rationally assessing risk - the bedpan might have residue, the fudge might actually be feces, the sweater might carry diseases","The effect is purely cultural conditioning - there is no biological basis for purity concerns","Only religious people have purity concerns; secular people judge solely on harm and fairness"],"underlying_psychology":"The Sanctity foundation explains why moral and political arguments often involve disgust and purity - not just harm. Abortion is framed as the sanctity of life. Flag burning is described as a sacred desecration. Environmentalism uses purity language about clean air, pristine nature, and pollution. The Sanctity foundation is the most variable across cultures and individuals. Progressives tend to minimize it; conservatives and traditionalists give it weight. Understanding Sanctity as a legitimate moral foundation (not just superstition) helps explain why people across cultures care about issues that seem irrational from a purely harm-based perspective.","player_mission":"Why do people react with disgust and rejection to perfectly clean objects that have merely been associated with contamination?","difficulty_level":"Beginner","key_concepts":["The True Taste restaurant had an entirely white interior, each table set only with five small spoons, and a menu divided into sections labeled 'Sugars,' 'Honeys,' 'Tree Saps,' and 'Artificials'","The waiter, who was also the owner and sole employee, was a biologist who specialized in the sense of taste and ran the first tasting bar for sweeteners in the world, with samples from thirty-two countries","The five kinds of taste receptor found in each taste bud on the tongue are sweet, sour, salty, bitter, and savory (also called umami)","The owner's research had shown that activation of the sweet receptor produced the strongest surge of dopamine, indicating humans are hard-wired to seek sweetness above the other four tastes","Business was 'Terrible,' he said, 'but at least I'm doing better than the chemist down the street who opened a salt-tasting bar'","Many authors reduce morality to a single principle, usually welfare maximization, or to justice and notions of fairness, rights, and respect for autonomy - The Utilitarian Grill serves only sweeteners (welfare) and The Deontological Diner serves only salts (rights)","Moral monism - the attempt to ground all of morality on a single principle - leads to societies that are unsatisfying to most people and at high risk of becoming inhumane because they ignore so many other moral principles","The Chinese sage Mencius made the analogy between morality and food 2,300 years ago, writing that 'moral principles please our minds as beef and mutton and pork please our mouths'","The righteous mind is like a tongue with six taste receptors, and morality is like cuisine - a cultural construction influenced by accidents of environment and history, but not so flexible that anything goes","You can't have a cuisine based on tree bark, nor one based primarily on bitter tastes; moral matrices vary, but they all must please righteous minds equipped with the same six social receptors","Just knowing that everyone has sweetness receptors can't tell you why one person prefers Thai food to Mexican or why hardly anyone stirs sugar into beer - connecting the universal receptors to particular judgments takes a lot of additional work","Hume thought morality was based in a variety of sentiments, so he relied on sensory analogies: moral judgment is a kind of perception, and moral science should begin with a careful study of the moral taste receptors"]},{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Moral Psychology | Disgust | Purity","topic":"The Rozin Disgust Experiments: From Physical to Moral Contagion","stage_1_observation":"Paul Rozin's classic contagion studies: A brand-new, sterilized bedpan. Would you drink soup from it? Participants refuse. A piece of fudge shaped like dog feces. Would you eat it? Even knowing it is fudge, participants refuse. A sweter worn by Adolf Hitler. Would you wear it? Participants refuse. A glass of juice that a sterilized cockroach was dipped into. Participants refuse and find the idea disgusting, even though the roach was sterilized.","stage_1_question":"Why do people reject perfectly clean objects that carry no physical risk?","stage_1_correct_answer":"The laws of sympathetic magic. Contagion law: once in contact, always in contact — the object retains the essence of whatever touched it. Similarity law: an image shares the properties of the thing it represents. These are not errors — they are features of a disgust system that evolved to protect us from pathogens. The disgust system was co-opted by morality. This is why moral violations feel disgusting, and why people describe immoral acts as sickening. The purity foundation extends physical disgust into the moral domain.","stage_2_intervention":"You are a lawyer defending a client accused of a gruesome crime. The crime scene photos are horrific — blood, violence, gore. The prosecutor wants to show them to the jury. You know that disgust contaminates moral judgment. How do you prevent the disgust response from biasing the jury against your client?","stage_2_correct_answer":"Two strategies. (1) Desensitization through exposure — request that the jury sees the photos multiple times before trial, allowing the disgust response to habituate. Research shows that with repeated exposure, the visceral disgust response diminishes. (2) Cognitive reframing — immediately after each disturbing image, provide an alternative interpretation that redirects the jurys attention away from disgust and toward the legal elements (chain of custody, forensic limitations, alternative explanations). The key is to prevent the incidental disgust from being misattributed to your client. Rozins research shows that disgust is domain-general — disgust from any source contaminates unrelated moral judgments.","boss_battle_explanation":"Layer 1 - Physical Disgust: Evolved to avoid pathogens and parasites. Layer 2 - The Laws of Sympathetic Magic: Contagion (contact transfers essence) and Similarity (appearance equals reality). Layer 3 - Moral Extension: Disgust generalizes from physical to moral violations. Layer 4 - Contamination: Incidental disgust (from a smell, a photo, a memory) is misattributed to whatever you are judging, making the judgment harsher. Layer 5 - The Cure: Separate the disgust trigger from the moral judgment. Is this wrong, or does it just feel disgusting?","player_mission":"","difficulty_level":"Intermediate","visual_frame_description":"An operating theater behind glass where a surgeon's knife glints over a small unconscious child; a screen splits the scene into hedons and dolors while a pair of Muller-Lyer lines proves that the eye cannot help itself.","key_concepts":["The True Taste restaurant had an entirely white interior, each table set only with five small spoons, and a menu divided into sections labeled 'Sugars,' 'Honeys,' 'Tree Saps,' and 'Artificials'","The waiter, who was also the owner and sole employee, was a biologist who specialized in the sense of taste and ran the first tasting bar for sweeteners in the world, with samples from thirty-two countries","The five kinds of taste receptor found in each taste bud on the tongue are sweet, sour, salty, bitter, and savory (also called umami)","The owner's research had shown that activation of the sweet receptor produced the strongest surge of dopamine, indicating humans are hard-wired to seek sweetness above the other four tastes","Business was 'Terrible,' he said, 'but at least I'm doing better than the chemist down the street who opened a salt-tasting bar'","Many authors reduce morality to a single principle, usually welfare maximization, or to justice and notions of fairness, rights, and respect for autonomy - The Utilitarian Grill serves only sweeteners (welfare) and The Deontological Diner serves only salts (rights)","Moral monism - the attempt to ground all of morality on a single principle - leads to societies that are unsatisfying to most people and at high risk of becoming inhumane because they ignore so many other moral principles","The Chinese sage Mencius made the analogy between morality and food 2,300 years ago, writing that 'moral principles please our minds as beef and mutton and pork please our mouths'","The righteous mind is like a tongue with six taste receptors, and morality is like cuisine - a cultural construction influenced by accidents of environment and history, but not so flexible that anything goes","You can't have a cuisine based on tree bark, nor one based primarily on bitter tastes; moral matrices vary, but they all must please righteous minds equipped with the same six social receptors","Just knowing that everyone has sweetness receptors can't tell you why one person prefers Thai food to Mexican or why hardly anyone stirs sugar into beer - connecting the universal receptors to particular judgments takes a lot of additional work","Hume thought morality was based in a variety of sentiments, so he relied on sensory analogies: moral judgment is a kind of perception, and moral science should begin with a careful study of the moral taste receptors"]}]},{"id":"rm_ch07","title":"Chapter 7: The Moral Foundations of Politics","pages":"189-223","assets":[{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Political Psychology | Moral Matrices","topic":"The Moral Matrix: Why Liberals and Conservatives Live in Different Moral Worlds","stage_1_observation":"Haidt and colleagues surveyed tens of thousands of people on the Moral Foundations Questionnaire. The results: liberals (progressives) strongly endorse Care and Fairness foundations, moderately endorse Liberty, and weakly endorse Loyalty, Authority, and Sanctity. Conservatives endorse all six foundations more evenly, with the strongest endorsements on Loyalty, Authority, and Sanctity.","stage_1_question":"If conservatives endorse all six foundations and liberals endorse only three, does that mean conservatives have a richer moral palate?","stage_1_correct_answer":"Yes, in a sense. Conservatives have more diverse moral concerns. But liberals care more intensely about their three foundations. The conservative advantage on Loyalty/Authority/Sanctity means conservatives can frame their positions using moral language that resonates with a wider range of voters. Liberals often fail to understand why voters respond to conservative messages about patriotism, tradition, and order - they dismiss these as non-moral concerns (racism, sexism, xenophobia) rather than recognizing them as different moral values.","stage_2_intervention":"You are a Democratic strategist trying to win rural working-class voters who voted for the Republican candidate. Your focus groups show these voters say: I feel like Democrats look down on me. They think I am a redneck. They want to take away my guns and tell me how to live. According to moral foundations theory, what is going wrong and how do you fix it?","stage_2_correct_answer":"The Democratic messaging uses only Care (we will help the poor) and Fairness-as-equality (close the income gap). These voters also care about Loyalty (love of country, community solidarity against outsiders), Authority (respect for tradition, military, local institutions), and Sanctity (traditional values, the sacredness of certain ways of life). The Democratic message fails because it does not just disagree - it implicitly dismisses the others foundations as immoral. The fix: (1) Acknowledge the legitimacy of the voters moral concerns about community breakdown and loss of tradition. (2) Reframe Democratic policies in terms that activate Loyalty (patriotism of universal healthcare), Authority (the stable institutions that help families), and Sanctity (protecting the sacred dignity of all workers). (3) Stop using contempt-laden language that signals moral superiority.","boss_battle_explanation":"Layer 1 - Differential Endorsement: Liberals prioritize Care and Fairness; conservatives use all six. Layer 2 - Moral Blindness: Each side sees the others concerns as non-moral (greed, hate, stupidity). Layer 3 - Contempt Trap: When you dismiss someones moral foundations, you signal that you consider them morally inferior, which triggers defensive reactance. Layer 4 - The Cure: Learn to speak the other sides moral language without abandoning your own values. Just as an American can learn Mandarin while remaining English-speaking, you can learn the others moral grammar while keeping your own.","player_mission":"","difficulty_level":"Intermediate","visual_frame_description":"A courtroom where the evidence table holds a frying pan, a meat hook, and a video camera; behind the judge the vertical dimension of divinity rises from worms and demons up to God, with a lone house on the ground awaiting its verdict.","key_concepts":["Homo economicus is a simple creature who makes all of life's choices like a shopper in a supermarket with plenty of time to compare jars of applesauce, and there is really just one principle at work: self-interest","The ten questions of figure 7.1 ask how much someone would have to pay you to perform each action, paid secretly with no social, legal, or other harmful consequences: 0 = $0, I'd do it for free; 1 = $100; 2 = $10,000; 3 = $1,000,000; 4 = I would not do this for any amount of money","Homo economicus would put a price on sticking a needle into his own arm and a lower price - perhaps zero - on the other nine actions, none of which hurts him directly or costs him anything","Each column-B item was written to give an intuitive flash from a foundation: hurting a child (Care), profiting from someone else's undeserved loss (Fairness), criticizing your nation to outsiders (Loyalty), disrespecting your father (Authority), and acting in a degrading or disgusting way (Sanctity)","If you found any of the actions in column B worse than their counterparts in column A, then congratulations - you are a human being, not an economist's fantasy, with concerns beyond narrow self-interest","The neuroscientist Gary Marcus: 'Nature bestows upon the newborn a considerably complex brain, but one that is best seen as prewired - flexible and subject to change - rather than hardwired, fixed, and immutable'","Marcus's analogy: the brain is like a book, the first draft of which is written by the genes during fetal development - no chapters are complete at birth, but not a single chapter can be inscribed by experience in any conceivable set of words","The best definition of innateness Haidt has ever seen: organized in advance of experience - 'built-in' does not mean unmalleable","It used to be risky for a scientist to assert that anything about human behavior was innate: you had to show the trait was hardwired, unchangeable by experience, and found in all cultures, a standard that left little innate besides a few infant reflexes","We have advanced a lot since the 1970s in our understanding of the brain, and now we know that traits can be innate without being either hardwired or universal","The list of five moral foundations was Haidt's first attempt to specify how the righteous mind was 'organized in advance of experience'","Moral Foundations Theory also tries to explain how that first draft gets revised during childhood to produce the diversity of moralities we find across cultures - and across the political spectrum"]},{"asset_type":"DECEPTION_AUDIT_FILE","domain":"Political Psychology | Moral Hypocrisy","topic":"The Paradox of Toleration: When Derogating the Outgroup Becomes a Moral Duty","subject_baseline":"Both Democrats and Republicans believe the other sides candidate is uniquely dangerous to democracy, that the other sides voters are motivated by hatred and ignorance, and that their own side is defending civilization. Each side believes the other is a threat to the moral order itself.","interrogation_transcript":"Haidt points out that this mutual demonization is a feature of moral psychology, not a bug. Humans evolved to be righteous - to bond with their group, to detect threats, and to mobilize against enemies. When groups have different moral foundations, each sees the other not just as wrong but as morally dangerous. The tolerant liberal becomes intolerant of conservative intolerance. The liberty-loving conservative becomes authoritarian about defending liberty. The rider constructs elaborate reasons why our team is righteous and theirs is depraved.","observed_nonverbal_log":["Each side monitors the other for moral violations - selective attention","Each side applies different standards to in-group vs out-group","Hypocrisy is invisible to the self but obvious when the other side does it","Normalizing the outgroup feels like betrayal to the ingroup"],"question":"How can people who value fairness and truth be so unfair and credulous about the other political side?","correct_diagnosis":"The righteous mind is designed for coalitional competition, not objectivity. Our moral psychology evolved in a context of intergroup conflict where loyalty to the group was essential for survival. Being objective about the outgroup was not adaptive - it was dangerous. The mechanism: (1) ingroup loyalty makes us receptive to positive in-group information. (2) Outgroup hostility makes us receptive to negative out-group information. (3) Confirmation bias strengthens existing beliefs. (4) The myside bias makes us better at finding flaws in the other sides arguments than in our own. We are not being irrational. We are being good team players.","distractors":["Social media algorithms create echo chambers that prevent people from seeing the other sides perspective","The media is biased and feeds each side the misinformation they want to hear","One side is genuinely more objective and fact-based while the other is driven by emotion and propaganda"],"diagnostic_breakdown":"Stage 1 - Group Identity: My political party is part of who I am. Stage 2 - Moral Foundations: My party stands for Care and Fairness (liberal) or for Loyalty, Authority, Sanctity (conservative). Stage 3 - Outgroup Threat: The other party endorses values I consider immoral. Stage 4 - Righteousness: We are not just opponents - we are good fighting evil. Stage 5 - Demonization: The other side becomes not just wrong but dangerous, irrational, and morally corrupt. The tragedy: both sides are doing the same thing, with equally sincere conviction that they are defending the good.","player_mission":"How can people who value fairness and truth be so unfair and credulous about the other political side?","difficulty_level":"Intermediate","visual_frame_description":"A state-park ranger station turned evidence room: a topographical map of Robbers Cave glows with two colored territories, Rattlers and Eagles, while photographs of bunks and flags drop into a logbook stamped TRIBAL BEHAVIOR.","key_concepts":["Homo economicus is a simple creature who makes all of life's choices like a shopper in a supermarket with plenty of time to compare jars of applesauce, and there is really just one principle at work: self-interest","The ten questions of figure 7.1 ask how much someone would have to pay you to perform each action, paid secretly with no social, legal, or other harmful consequences: 0 = $0, I'd do it for free; 1 = $100; 2 = $10,000; 3 = $1,000,000; 4 = I would not do this for any amount of money","Homo economicus would put a price on sticking a needle into his own arm and a lower price - perhaps zero - on the other nine actions, none of which hurts him directly or costs him anything","Each column-B item was written to give an intuitive flash from a foundation: hurting a child (Care), profiting from someone else's undeserved loss (Fairness), criticizing your nation to outsiders (Loyalty), disrespecting your father (Authority), and acting in a degrading or disgusting way (Sanctity)","If you found any of the actions in column B worse than their counterparts in column A, then congratulations - you are a human being, not an economist's fantasy, with concerns beyond narrow self-interest","The neuroscientist Gary Marcus: 'Nature bestows upon the newborn a considerably complex brain, but one that is best seen as prewired - flexible and subject to change - rather than hardwired, fixed, and immutable'","Marcus's analogy: the brain is like a book, the first draft of which is written by the genes during fetal development - no chapters are complete at birth, but not a single chapter can be inscribed by experience in any conceivable set of words","The best definition of innateness Haidt has ever seen: organized in advance of experience - 'built-in' does not mean unmalleable","It used to be risky for a scientist to assert that anything about human behavior was innate: you had to show the trait was hardwired, unchangeable by experience, and found in all cultures, a standard that left little innate besides a few infant reflexes","We have advanced a lot since the 1970s in our understanding of the brain, and now we know that traits can be innate without being either hardwired or universal","The list of five moral foundations was Haidt's first attempt to specify how the righteous mind was 'organized in advance of experience'","Moral Foundations Theory also tries to explain how that first draft gets revised during childhood to produce the diversity of moralities we find across cultures - and across the political spectrum"]}]},{"id":"rm_ch08","title":"Chapter 8: The Conservative Advantage","pages":"224-273","assets":[{"asset_type":"CUE_SCRUBBER_STATION","domain":"Political Psychology | Conservative Advantage","topic":"The Conservative Advantage: Why Conservatives Have a Broader Moral Palate","visual_frame_description":"Imagine a restaurant menu. Liberals order from three appetizers: Care, Fairness, and Liberty. Conservatives order from all six: Care, Fairness, Liberty, Loyalty, Authority, and Sanctity. When the restaurant runs a promotion on family-style meals that include Loyalty and Sanctity dishes, conservatives are naturally drawn. Liberals look at the menu and see only appetizers they do not order.","target_channel":"Moral Range Asymmetry | Political Persuasion","question":"Why do conservative political messages about patriotism, tradition, and order resonate with more voters than liberal messages about compassion and equality?","correct_cue":"Conservatives appeal to the full range of moral foundations, while liberals primarily use only Care and Fairness. Messages about protecting the nation (Loyalty), respecting tradition (Authority), and defending sacred values (Sanctity) activate moral intuitions that liberals often ignore or dismiss. This gives conservatives a built-in advantage in moral communication: they can talk to voters using 6 channels while liberals use 3. Furthermore, conservative voters are more sensitive to threats and order concerns, making Authority/Sanctity messages land with greater emotional force.","distractors":["Conservative policies are simply more popular and effective, so they win on merit","Conservatives spend more money on advertising and have better media infrastructure","Liberal policies appeal to the head while conservative policies appeal to the heart - and the heart is stronger"],"underlying_psychology":"The conservative advantage is not about policy quality but about moral range. Haidt shows that conservative rhetoric activates Care (protecting the vulnerable from foreign threats), Fairness (proportionality - people should get what they deserve), Loyalty (patriotism, supporting our troops), Authority (law and order, respect for tradition), Sanctity (protecting sacred American values, religious freedom), and Liberty (freedom from government overreach). Liberal rhetoric mainly uses Care (helping the poor, protecting minorities) and Fairness-as-equality (closing the wealth gap). The liberal disadvantage: by ceding Loyalty, Authority, and Sanctity to conservatives, liberals make themselves vulnerable to being framed as unpatriotic, relativist, and immoral.","player_mission":"Why do conservative political messages about patriotism, tradition, and order resonate with more voters than liberal messages about compassion and equality?","difficulty_level":"Beginner","key_concepts":["In January 2005 Haidt was invited to speak to the Charlottesville Democratic Party, having spent much of 2004 as an unpaid speechwriter for John Kerry's presidential campaign, mentally rewriting Kerry's ineffectual appeals while walking his dog","In Kerry's acceptance speech at the Democratic National Convention, 'America can do better' connected to no moral foundation at all, while 'Help is on the way' connected weakly to the Care/harm foundation","Haidt's rewrite - 'You gonna pay for that, George?' - would have made Bush's many new programs, on top of tax cuts and two wars, look like shoplifting rather than generosity, and would have activated the cheater detection modules of the Fairness/cheating foundation","The message of the talk was simple: Republicans understand moral psychology; Democrats don't - Republicans have long understood that the elephant is in charge of political behavior, not the rider, and they know how elephants work","The infamous 1988 ad showed a mug shot of a black man, Willie Horton, who committed a brutal murder after being released from prison on a weekend furlough by the 'soft-on-crime' Democratic candidate, Governor Michael Dukakis","Democrats have often aimed their appeals more squarely at the rider, emphasizing specific policies and the benefits they'll bring to you, the voter","Bill Clinton, the only Democrat since Franklin Roosevelt to win election and then reelection, combined gregariousness and oratorical skill with an almost musical emotionality - he knew how to charm elephants","Republicans since Nixon have had a near-monopoly on appeals to loyalty (particularly patriotism and military virtues) and authority (including respect for parents, teachers, elders, and the police, as well as traditions)","After Ronald Reagan's 1980 campaign embraced Christian conservatives and the party became the party of 'family values,' Republicans inherited a powerful network of Christian ideas about sanctity and sexuality","In the story Haidt told the Charlottesville Democrats, Democratic morality was just sugar (Care) and salt (Fairness as equality), whereas Republican morality appealed to all five taste receptors","Haidt didn't blame the Republicans for trickery - he blamed the Democrats for psychological naiveté, and after two consecutive losses to George W. Bush the audience seemed willing to consider his explanation","Back then Haidt's explanation was just speculation: he had not yet collected any data to support his claim that conservatives responded to a broader set of moral tastes than did liberals"]},{"asset_type":"DECEPTION_AUDIT_FILE","domain":"Political Psychology | System Justification | Status Quo Bias","topic":"The Status Quo as Sacred: Why Conservatives Resist Change","subject_baseline":"A liberal activist protests outside a factory that has operated for 80 years, demanding it close to protect the environment. Workers at the factory counter-protest: they need their jobs, the factory supports the town, and it has been there for generations. Both sides see themselves as the moral party.","interrogation_transcript":"The liberal activist sees an environmental threat (Care foundation - protect the planet) and an injustice (the factory pollutes while the neighborhood suffers). The workers see a threat to their community (Loyalty - this factory is part of who we are), to legitimate authority (the factory owner has been a pillar of the community - Authority), and to the sacredness of their way of life (Sanctity - our traditions, our town, our identity). The conservative resistance to change is not mere stubbornness or ignorance. It is a genuine moral commitment to preserving institutions, traditions, and ways of life that have proven their value over time.","observed_nonverbal_log":["Both sides identify real moral concerns grounded in different foundations","The liberal sees only the Care/Fairness dimensions and dismisses the others","The workers experience liberal demands as an attack on their identity and community","Each side feels the other is being irrational and immoral"],"question":"Why do conservatives typically defend existing institutions and resist change, even when those institutions have clear flaws?","correct_diagnosis":"The conservative mind is not merely resistant to change - it is oriented toward preserving order, stability, and tradition. This is not a cognitive defect. It is a moral commitment grounded in Authority and Sanctity foundations. Conservatives tend to see institutions (government, military, church, family) as evolved structures that embody accumulated wisdom across generations. Changing them recklessly risks destroying something valuable that took centuries to build. The liberal sees an unjust institution. The conservative sees a fragile legacy of civilization. Both are right about different aspects. The best political systems integrate both: conservative preservation of what works with liberal correction of what is unjust.","distractors":["Conservatives are simply less intelligent and cannot understand the complexity of proposed reforms","Conservatives benefit from the status quo economically and resist change to protect their privilege","The conservative personality is defined by fear of change and a need for certainty, making all change threatening"],"diagnostic_breakdown":"Stage 1 - Institution Has Existed: The status quo represents generations of accumulated wisdom and adaptation. Stage 2 - Authority Foundation: Respect for tradition and legitimate hierarchy. Stage 3 - Sanctity Foundation: Existing institutions feel sacred - they are part of our collective identity. Stage 4 - Threat Response: Proposed changes feel like attacks on sacred values, triggering defensive reactions. Stage 5 - Liberal Counterpoint: Change advocates focus on the institutions failures (Care/Fairness) without addressing the conservative concerns about loss, stability, and identity. Both sides are morally motivated. Neither is purely rational.","player_mission":"Why do conservatives typically defend existing institutions and resist change, even when those institutions have clear flaws?","difficulty_level":"Intermediate","visual_frame_description":"A vault lined with evidence drawers: one holds dog photographs, one holds sermon manuscripts, one holds EEG readouts; a clerk stamps each file while a monitor replays liberal and conservative brains flashing surprise in the first half second.","key_concepts":["In January 2005 Haidt was invited to speak to the Charlottesville Democratic Party, having spent much of 2004 as an unpaid speechwriter for John Kerry's presidential campaign, mentally rewriting Kerry's ineffectual appeals while walking his dog","In Kerry's acceptance speech at the Democratic National Convention, 'America can do better' connected to no moral foundation at all, while 'Help is on the way' connected weakly to the Care/harm foundation","Haidt's rewrite - 'You gonna pay for that, George?' - would have made Bush's many new programs, on top of tax cuts and two wars, look like shoplifting rather than generosity, and would have activated the cheater detection modules of the Fairness/cheating foundation","The message of the talk was simple: Republicans understand moral psychology; Democrats don't - Republicans have long understood that the elephant is in charge of political behavior, not the rider, and they know how elephants work","The infamous 1988 ad showed a mug shot of a black man, Willie Horton, who committed a brutal murder after being released from prison on a weekend furlough by the 'soft-on-crime' Democratic candidate, Governor Michael Dukakis","Democrats have often aimed their appeals more squarely at the rider, emphasizing specific policies and the benefits they'll bring to you, the voter","Bill Clinton, the only Democrat since Franklin Roosevelt to win election and then reelection, combined gregariousness and oratorical skill with an almost musical emotionality - he knew how to charm elephants","Republicans since Nixon have had a near-monopoly on appeals to loyalty (particularly patriotism and military virtues) and authority (including respect for parents, teachers, elders, and the police, as well as traditions)","After Ronald Reagan's 1980 campaign embraced Christian conservatives and the party became the party of 'family values,' Republicans inherited a powerful network of Christian ideas about sanctity and sexuality","In the story Haidt told the Charlottesville Democrats, Democratic morality was just sugar (Care) and salt (Fairness as equality), whereas Republican morality appealed to all five taste receptors","Haidt didn't blame the Republicans for trickery - he blamed the Democrats for psychological naiveté, and after two consecutive losses to George W. Bush the audience seemed willing to consider his explanation","Back then Haidt's explanation was just speculation: he had not yet collected any data to support his claim that conservatives responded to a broader set of moral tastes than did liberals"]}]},{"id":"rm_ch09a","title":"Chapter 9: Why Are We So Groupish? (Part 1)","pages":"274-314","assets":[{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Evolutionary Psychology | Group Selection","topic":"Individual vs Group Selection: Two Levels of Competition Driving Human Morality","column_a_label":"Individual Selection - Competition between individuals within a group. Favors selfishness, cheating, and free-riding. Genes that promote individual survival and reproduction spread.","column_b_label":"Group Selection - Competition between groups. Favors cooperation, self-sacrifice, and altruism toward ingroup members. Groups with more cooperators outcompete groups with more free-riders.","rows":[{"scenario":"A soldier throws himself on a grenade to save his unit","correct_column":"B","reason":"This self-sacrifice makes no sense at the individual gene level but greatly benefits the group in intergroup competition"},{"scenario":"An employee secretly steals from the company till","correct_column":"A","reason":"Free-riding behavior that benefits the individual at the groups expense - the classic individual selection dynamic"},{"scenario":"A community builds an irrigation system that requires collective maintenance","correct_column":"B","reason":"Cooperative infrastructure benefits the group and requires group-level coordination to maintain"},{"scenario":"A person volunteers for a dangerous medical trial with no personal benefit","correct_column":"B","reason":"Altruism toward strangers and future generations - hard to explain by individual selection alone"},{"scenario":"Two companies compete in a market - one with high trust and low internal theft outperforms the other","correct_column":"B","reason":"The group (company) with better internal cooperation outcompetes the group with more free-riding"}],"explanation":"Haidt argues for multi-level selection: humans evolved through competition at both levels. Within-group competition selected for selfish genes (individual level). Between-group competition selected for cooperative groups (group level). The result: humans are 90% chimp (selfish individual) and 10% bee (groupish cooperator). This explains why we are both deeply selfish and capable of extraordinary altruism. Morality is the set of psychological adaptations that enable group cooperation in the context of intergroup competition. We are groupish - not purely altruistic, but capable of setting aside self-interest when the group needs us.","player_mission":"","difficulty_level":"Advanced","visual_frame_description":"A sorting chamber with twin scales, one stamped with a chimp and one with a bee; between them a glowing banner reads 'We are 90 percent chimp and 10 percent bee,' and each sorted card drops onto its level of the hierarchy of life.","key_concepts":["Haidt felt a primitive urge to put an American flag decal on his car after the terrorist attacks of September 11, 2001, and resolved his dilemma by placing an American flag in one corner of his rear windshield and a United Nations flag in the opposite corner","Selfishness means our minds contain mechanisms that promote our own interests in competition with our peers, while groupishness means mechanisms that promote our group's interests in competition with other groups","Group selection was banished as a heresy from scientific circles in the 1970s, but Haidt argues it was falsely convicted and unfairly banished, and presents four exhibits in its defense","In The Descent of Man, Darwin argued that a tribe rich in courageous, sympathetic, and faithful members would succeed and conquer other tribes, but he raised the free rider problem in the very next paragraph","Multilevel selection treats life as a hierarchy of nested levels, like Russian dolls: genes within chromosomes within cells within individual organisms within hives, societies, and other groups","The free rider problem says that within the bravest army, the few cowards who hang back are the most likely of all to survive the fight, go home alive, and become fathers","Darwin's probable steps toward team playing were the social instincts, reciprocity, passionate concern with the praise and blame of our fellow-men, and the capacity to treat duties and principles as sacred","Darwin summarized morality as a highly complex sentiment originating in the social instincts, largely guided by the approbation of our fellow-men, ruled by reason, self-interest, and in later times deep religious feelings, and confirmed by instruction and habit","In Adaptation and Natural Selection (1966), George Williams argued that a fast herd of deer is nothing more than a herd of fast deer - the selection process operated on the level of individuals, not the herd","Williams conceded that only by a theory of between-group selection could we achieve a scientific explanation of group-related adaptations","By the late 1970s there was a strong consensus that anyone who said a behavior occurred for the good of the group was a fool who could be safely ignored","Williams called morality an accidental capability produced, in its boundless stupidity, by a biological process normally opposed to its expression, while Haidt answers that human beings are the giraffes of altruism - one-of-a-kind freaks of nature occasionally as team-spirited as bees"]},{"asset_type":"DECEPTION_AUDIT_FILE","domain":"Evolutionary Psychology | Warfare | Tribalism","topic":"The Tribal Instinct: Why We Love Us and Fear Them","subject_baseline":"Across human history, warfare was the leading cause of death for adult males in many small-scale societies. Groups that could cooperate internally and coordinate attacks externally outcompeted groups that could not. The human mind evolved for this reality: deep ingroup loyalty and automatic outgroup vigilance.","interrogation_transcript":"Haidt synthesizes evidence from anthropology (Keeleys war before civilization), primatology (chimpanzee border patrols), and psychology (minimal group paradigm). The minimal group paradigm shows that even random assignment to a meaningless group (like overestimating vs underestimating dots) produces ingroup favoritism within minutes. No history, no conflict, no resources - just group membership. The brain has a tribal instinct that can be triggered by the most trivial categorization. This is the foundation of both team sports and ethnic genocide.","observed_nonverbal_log":["Ingroup favoritism emerges automatically from minimal groups","Outgroup derogation is weaker than ingroup favoritism - we love us more than we hate them","Groupishness is easily triggered by shared symbols, rituals, and enemies","The same person who is kind and generous to ingroup members can be cruel to outgroup members"],"question":"If groupishness evolved for intergroup competition and warfare, can we have the cooperation without the conflict?","correct_diagnosis":"Partially. Groupishness is a double-edged sword. The same mechanisms that bind groups together (shared identity, symbols, rituals, sacred values) also create the potential for intergroup hostility. You cannot turn off groupishness selectively - when you bind a group, you create an us and a them. But you can expand the circle of us. You can create larger, more inclusive identities (national identity, human rights, global citizenship) that encompass former enemies. The key insight: you do not need to eliminate groupishness. You need to redirect it toward ever-larger circles of inclusion while recognizing that some boundary will always exist.","distractors":["Yes - education and globalization are eliminating tribalism as people become more rational and cosmopolitan","No - tribalism is fixed and permanent; humans will always be at war with outgroups and there is no escape","Groupishness is purely cultural - if we raised children without nationalism or religion, tribalism would disappear"],"diagnostic_breakdown":"Stage 1 - Group Formation: Any shared identity creates us/them. Stage 2 - Ingroup Bonding: Rituals, stories, and sacrifice strengthen internal bonds. Stage 3 - Outgroup Perception: The other group is seen as less human, less moral, more threatening. Stage 4 - Moral Division: Our group is righteous; theirs is wrong. Stage 5 - Conflict: Competition over resources or values escalates. The hope: we can design institutions that channel groupishness toward constructive competition (sports, markets, scientific competition) rather than destructive conflict.","player_mission":"If groupishness evolved for intergroup competition and warfare, can we have the cooperation without the conflict?","difficulty_level":"Intermediate","visual_frame_description":"A clerk's office stacked with four brown dossier binders labeled A, B, C, and D; artifacts fly in to be filed while a timeline along the wall runs from the first bacteria to Facebook linking half a billion people, and an alarm box reads: In case of foreign attack, break glass and push button.","key_concepts":["Haidt felt a primitive urge to put an American flag decal on his car after the terrorist attacks of September 11, 2001, and resolved his dilemma by placing an American flag in one corner of his rear windshield and a United Nations flag in the opposite corner","Selfishness means our minds contain mechanisms that promote our own interests in competition with our peers, while groupishness means mechanisms that promote our group's interests in competition with other groups","Group selection was banished as a heresy from scientific circles in the 1970s, but Haidt argues it was falsely convicted and unfairly banished, and presents four exhibits in its defense","In The Descent of Man, Darwin argued that a tribe rich in courageous, sympathetic, and faithful members would succeed and conquer other tribes, but he raised the free rider problem in the very next paragraph","Multilevel selection treats life as a hierarchy of nested levels, like Russian dolls: genes within chromosomes within cells within individual organisms within hives, societies, and other groups","The free rider problem says that within the bravest army, the few cowards who hang back are the most likely of all to survive the fight, go home alive, and become fathers","Darwin's probable steps toward team playing were the social instincts, reciprocity, passionate concern with the praise and blame of our fellow-men, and the capacity to treat duties and principles as sacred","Darwin summarized morality as a highly complex sentiment originating in the social instincts, largely guided by the approbation of our fellow-men, ruled by reason, self-interest, and in later times deep religious feelings, and confirmed by instruction and habit","In Adaptation and Natural Selection (1966), George Williams argued that a fast herd of deer is nothing more than a herd of fast deer - the selection process operated on the level of individuals, not the herd","Williams conceded that only by a theory of between-group selection could we achieve a scientific explanation of group-related adaptations","By the late 1970s there was a strong consensus that anyone who said a behavior occurred for the good of the group was a fool who could be safely ignored","Williams called morality an accidental capability produced, in its boundless stupidity, by a biological process normally opposed to its expression, while Haidt answers that human beings are the giraffes of altruism - one-of-a-kind freaks of nature occasionally as team-spirited as bees"]},{"asset_type":"CUE_SCRUBBER_STATION","domain":"Evolutionary Psychology | Targeted Altruism","topic":"The Parochial Altruism Paradox: Why We Are Nicest to Those We Can Kill","visual_frame_description":"A soldier carries a wounded comrade through enemy fire, risking his own life. The same soldier might have killed a member of a neighboring tribe in a raid last year. The capacity for self-sacrifice and for killing are two sides of the same coin: parochial altruism - altruism toward the ingroup, aggression toward the outgroup.","target_channel":"Parochial Altruism Detection","question":"Why does human altruism so consistently stop at the group boundary, and why do we show the most extreme altruism in the context of intergroup conflict?","correct_cue":"Parochial altruism evolved through group selection. Groups that had members willing to sacrifice for each other and fight against other groups outcompeted groups lacking these traits. The same psychological mechanism produces both effects: the deep emotional bond with ingroup members (love, loyalty, duty) and the willingness to harm outgroup members who threaten the ingroup. This is why veterans describe the profound bond of combat - the most intense altruism humans ever experience occurs in the context of trying to kill other humans. The evolutionary logic: you cannot have ingroup love without some degree of outgroup vigilance.","distractors":["Altruism is a purely cultural construction that can be extended to all humans through education and reason","Parochial altruism is unique to warrior cultures and does not appear in peaceful societies","The altruism soldiers show is about duty and training, not genuine care for comrades"],"underlying_psychology":"Choi and Bowles 2007 parochial altruism model. Mathematical modeling shows that parochial altruism (altruism toward ingroup + hostility toward outgroup) can evolve through group selection when groups frequently compete. Pure altruism (toward everyone) cannot evolve because altruists are exploited by free-riders. Parochial altruism solves this: altruism toward ingroup members who reciprocate, combined with hostility toward outgroup members who might exploit. The implication for modern politics: national identity, patriotism, and even some forms of nationalism are not primitive holdovers. They are expressions of our evolved groupishness that can be channeled toward inclusive patriotism (proud of our nation, proud of our contributions to the world) rather than aggressive nationalism.","player_mission":"Why does human altruism so consistently stop at the group boundary, and why do we show the most extreme altruism in the context of intergroup conflict?","difficulty_level":"Beginner","key_concepts":["Haidt felt a primitive urge to put an American flag decal on his car after the terrorist attacks of September 11, 2001, and resolved his dilemma by placing an American flag in one corner of his rear windshield and a United Nations flag in the opposite corner","Selfishness means our minds contain mechanisms that promote our own interests in competition with our peers, while groupishness means mechanisms that promote our group's interests in competition with other groups","Group selection was banished as a heresy from scientific circles in the 1970s, but Haidt argues it was falsely convicted and unfairly banished, and presents four exhibits in its defense","In The Descent of Man, Darwin argued that a tribe rich in courageous, sympathetic, and faithful members would succeed and conquer other tribes, but he raised the free rider problem in the very next paragraph","Multilevel selection treats life as a hierarchy of nested levels, like Russian dolls: genes within chromosomes within cells within individual organisms within hives, societies, and other groups","The free rider problem says that within the bravest army, the few cowards who hang back are the most likely of all to survive the fight, go home alive, and become fathers","Darwin's probable steps toward team playing were the social instincts, reciprocity, passionate concern with the praise and blame of our fellow-men, and the capacity to treat duties and principles as sacred","Darwin summarized morality as a highly complex sentiment originating in the social instincts, largely guided by the approbation of our fellow-men, ruled by reason, self-interest, and in later times deep religious feelings, and confirmed by instruction and habit","In Adaptation and Natural Selection (1966), George Williams argued that a fast herd of deer is nothing more than a herd of fast deer - the selection process operated on the level of individuals, not the herd","Williams conceded that only by a theory of between-group selection could we achieve a scientific explanation of group-related adaptations","By the late 1970s there was a strong consensus that anyone who said a behavior occurred for the good of the group was a fool who could be safely ignored","Williams called morality an accidental capability produced, in its boundless stupidity, by a biological process normally opposed to its expression, while Haidt answers that human beings are the giraffes of altruism - one-of-a-kind freaks of nature occasionally as team-spirited as bees"]}]},{"id":"rm_ch09b","title":"Chapter 9: Why Are We So Groupish? (Part 2)","pages":"274-314","assets":[{"asset_type":"CUE_SCRUBBER_STATION","domain":"Neuroscience | Oxytocin | Group Bonding","topic":"The Dark Side of Oxytocin: Love Hormone or Tribal Bonding Agent?","visual_frame_description":"Participants receive either oxytocin nasal spray or a placebo. They then play a trust game. Oxytocin increases trust and generosity — toward ingroup members. In one study, oxytocin made Dutch men more trusting of Dutch names but not of Arab or German names. In another, oxytocin made men more willing to hurt outgroup members to benefit their group. The love hormone is not universal love — it is parochial bonding.","target_channel":"Oxytocin as Group-Bonding Hormone | The Tribal Glue","question":"Why does a hormone that promotes trust and bonding also increase hostility toward outsiders?","correct_cue":"Oxytocin did not evolve to promote universal love. It evolved to bond mothers to infants and to bond individuals to their ingroup. In ancestral environments, the group was family and close allies. The hormone that makes you trust your group also makes you vigilant against outsiders. Oxytocin amplifies parochial altruism — altruism toward us, defensiveness against them. This is the neurochemical basis of the hive switch. When groups engage in synchrony and shared rituals, oxytocin is released, bonding members together and making them more willing to sacrifice for the group.","distractors":["Oxytocin is purely a love hormone — the outgroup hostility findings are statistical anomalies that failed to replicate","Oxytocin only affects women and has no meaningful effect on male social bonding","The effects of oxytocin are short-term and have no lasting impact on group identity or behavior"],"underlying_psychology":"Oxytocin research (De Dreu 2010, Kosfeld 2005) shows that the hormone is not a universal moral enhancer but a group-bonding agent. It increases trust, cooperation, and generosity within the group while simultaneously increasing defensiveness, suspicion, and even aggression toward outgroups. This perfectly maps onto Haidts argument that morality binds and blinds. The same mechanism that binds groups together (oxytocin release during collective rituals) also blinds them to the humanity of outsiders. This is why collective rituals can produce both ecstatic unity and tribal hostility.","player_mission":"Why does a hormone that promotes trust and bonding also increase hostility toward outsiders?","difficulty_level":"Beginner","key_concepts":["Haidt felt a primitive urge to put an American flag decal on his car after the terrorist attacks of September 11, 2001, and resolved his dilemma by placing an American flag in one corner of his rear windshield and a United Nations flag in the opposite corner","Selfishness means our minds contain mechanisms that promote our own interests in competition with our peers, while groupishness means mechanisms that promote our group's interests in competition with other groups","Group selection was banished as a heresy from scientific circles in the 1970s, but Haidt argues it was falsely convicted and unfairly banished, and presents four exhibits in its defense","In The Descent of Man, Darwin argued that a tribe rich in courageous, sympathetic, and faithful members would succeed and conquer other tribes, but he raised the free rider problem in the very next paragraph","Multilevel selection treats life as a hierarchy of nested levels, like Russian dolls: genes within chromosomes within cells within individual organisms within hives, societies, and other groups","The free rider problem says that within the bravest army, the few cowards who hang back are the most likely of all to survive the fight, go home alive, and become fathers","Darwin's probable steps toward team playing were the social instincts, reciprocity, passionate concern with the praise and blame of our fellow-men, and the capacity to treat duties and principles as sacred","Darwin summarized morality as a highly complex sentiment originating in the social instincts, largely guided by the approbation of our fellow-men, ruled by reason, self-interest, and in later times deep religious feelings, and confirmed by instruction and habit","In Adaptation and Natural Selection (1966), George Williams argued that a fast herd of deer is nothing more than a herd of fast deer - the selection process operated on the level of individuals, not the herd","Williams conceded that only by a theory of between-group selection could we achieve a scientific explanation of group-related adaptations","By the late 1970s there was a strong consensus that anyone who said a behavior occurred for the good of the group was a fool who could be safely ignored","Williams called morality an accidental capability produced, in its boundless stupidity, by a biological process normally opposed to its expression, while Haidt answers that human beings are the giraffes of altruism - one-of-a-kind freaks of nature occasionally as team-spirited as bees"]}]},{"id":"rm_ch09c","title":"Chapter 9: Why Are We So Groupish? (Part 3)","pages":"274-314","assets":[{"asset_type":"CUE_SCRUBBER_STATION","domain":"Neuroscience | Mirror Neurons | Empathy","topic":"Mirror Neurons: The Neural Basis of Empathy and Collective Sentiment","visual_frame_description":"A monkey watches a researcher pick up a raisin. The same neurons fire in the monkey brain as if the monkey itself were picking up the raisin. In humans, this mirror system is broader — it responds to emotions, intentions, and sensations. When you see someone in pain, your pain matrix activates. When you see someone smile, your smile muscles subtly activate. The boundaries between self and other blur at the neural level.","target_channel":"Mirror Neuron System | Emotional Contagion | Collective Effervescence","question":"How does the mirror neuron system make Durkheim's collective effervescence possible?","correct_cue":"Mirror neurons provide the neural mechanism for emotional contagion and collective sentiment. When people gather in synchrony — chanting, marching, dancing together — each persons movements and emotions are mirrored in others brains. This creates a feedback loop: my joy triggers your mirror neurons, which triggers your joy, which triggers my mirror neurons even more strongly. The group becomes a single emotional system. Haidt draws on mirror neuron research to explain how the hive switch works at the neural level. The same system that allows us to empathize with one individual also allows us to merge into a collective.","distractors":["Mirror neurons are found only in monkeys and have not been reliably demonstrated in humans","Mirror neurons only respond to physical actions, not to emotions or intentions","The mirror system is purely a cognitive mechanism with no emotional or motivational effects"],"underlying_psychology":"Rizzolatti and Craighero 2004 discovered mirror neurons in the premotor cortex of macaques. Human neuroimaging suggests an even broader mirror system connecting action, emotion, and sensation regions. Mirror neurons are perfectly suited for Durkheimian collective sentiments — they automatically align the internal states of group members. When a group moves together in synchrony, the mirror system amplifies the shared experience. This is not metaphor: the same neural circuits fire in all participants, creating a genuinely shared mental state. This is the biological basis for the hive switch.","player_mission":"How does the mirror neuron system make Durkheim's collective effervescence possible?","difficulty_level":"Beginner","key_concepts":["Haidt felt a primitive urge to put an American flag decal on his car after the terrorist attacks of September 11, 2001, and resolved his dilemma by placing an American flag in one corner of his rear windshield and a United Nations flag in the opposite corner","Selfishness means our minds contain mechanisms that promote our own interests in competition with our peers, while groupishness means mechanisms that promote our group's interests in competition with other groups","Group selection was banished as a heresy from scientific circles in the 1970s, but Haidt argues it was falsely convicted and unfairly banished, and presents four exhibits in its defense","In The Descent of Man, Darwin argued that a tribe rich in courageous, sympathetic, and faithful members would succeed and conquer other tribes, but he raised the free rider problem in the very next paragraph","Multilevel selection treats life as a hierarchy of nested levels, like Russian dolls: genes within chromosomes within cells within individual organisms within hives, societies, and other groups","The free rider problem says that within the bravest army, the few cowards who hang back are the most likely of all to survive the fight, go home alive, and become fathers","Darwin's probable steps toward team playing were the social instincts, reciprocity, passionate concern with the praise and blame of our fellow-men, and the capacity to treat duties and principles as sacred","Darwin summarized morality as a highly complex sentiment originating in the social instincts, largely guided by the approbation of our fellow-men, ruled by reason, self-interest, and in later times deep religious feelings, and confirmed by instruction and habit","In Adaptation and Natural Selection (1966), George Williams argued that a fast herd of deer is nothing more than a herd of fast deer - the selection process operated on the level of individuals, not the herd","Williams conceded that only by a theory of between-group selection could we achieve a scientific explanation of group-related adaptations","By the late 1970s there was a strong consensus that anyone who said a behavior occurred for the good of the group was a fool who could be safely ignored","Williams called morality an accidental capability produced, in its boundless stupidity, by a biological process normally opposed to its expression, while Haidt answers that human beings are the giraffes of altruism - one-of-a-kind freaks of nature occasionally as team-spirited as bees"]}]},{"id":"rm_ch10a","title":"Chapter 10: The Hive Switch (Part 1)","pages":"315-354","assets":[{"asset_type":"CUE_SCRUBBER_STATION","domain":"Social Psychology | Collective Behavior | Self-Transcendence","topic":"The Hive Switch: Collective Effervescence and Self-Transcendence","visual_frame_description":"Thousands of people at a concert, all jumping in sync, singing together, arms around strangers. A military unit marching in perfect formation. A religious congregation chanting, swaying, and praying in unison. A crowd at a stadium roaring as one when the home team scores. In all these moments, individuals report losing their sense of separate self and becoming part of something larger.","target_channel":"Collective Effervescence | Self-Transcendence Induction","question":"Why do humans across cultures engage in synchronized, collective rituals that dissolve the boundary between self and group?","correct_cue":"The hive switch is a mechanism for temporarily suppressing self-interest and enabling group cohesion. Durkheim called it collective effervescence. It is triggered by synchrony (moving in rhythm), shared intentionality (doing something together with purpose), and the presence of a sacred focus (a symbol, cause, or leader that represents the group). The experience is intrinsically rewarding - the brain releases endorphins during synchronized activity, creating a natural high. This is why militaries use drill, why religions use chant, and why sports fans wear the same colors. The hive switch makes us 10% bee.","distractors":["The experience is purely social conformity - people pretend to feel unity to fit in with the crowd","This is a uniquely human cultural invention that appears only in complex societies","The effect is limited to religious contexts and does not appear in secular group activities"],"underlying_psychology":"Haidt draws on Durkheims concept of collective effervescence, which Durkheim identified as the origin of religious experience. When people assemble and act together in synchrony, they generate a shared emotional experience that feels transcendently real. This experience creates the sense of the sacred - something set apart and inviolable. The hive switch is key to understanding how groups bind. Under the influence of the hive switch, individuals become willing to sacrifice for the group, obey authority, and defend the groups sacred values. This is the mechanism by which morality binds and blinds.","player_mission":"Why do humans across cultures engage in synchronized, collective rituals that dissolve the boundary between self and group?","difficulty_level":"Beginner","key_concepts":["William McNeill, drafted into the U.S. Army in September 1941, found that prolonged movement in unison during drilling produced a strange sense of personal enlargement, a sort of swelling out, becoming bigger than life","McNeill hypothesized that muscular bonding - moving together in time - evolved long before the beginning of recorded history for shutting down the self and creating a temporary superorganism","McNeill concluded that the key innovation of Greek, Roman, and later European armies was synchronous drilling and marching, and the Macedonian phalanx was the superorganism Alexander the Great used to defeat much larger armies","McNeill studied accounts of men in battle and found that men risk their lives not so much for their country or their ideals as for their comrades-in-arms","One veteran quoted by McNeill said that when their 'I' passes insensibly into a 'we,' self-sacrifice becomes easy, for 'I may fall, but I do not die, for that which is real in me goes forward and lives on in the comrades for whom I gave up my life'","The hive hypothesis states that human beings are conditional hive creatures who have the ability, under special conditions, to transcend self-interest and lose themselves temporarily and ecstatically in something larger than themselves","The hive switch is a group-related adaptation that can only be explained by a theory of between-group selection, as Williams said - it is an adaptation for making groups more cohesive","Barbara Ehrenreich's Dancing in the Streets argues that collective and ecstatic dancing is a nearly universal biotechnology for binding groups together and a form of muscular bonding that fosters love, trust, and equality","Early Christianity was a danced religion until dancing in church was suppressed in the Middle Ages, and Ehrenreich traces Europe's abandonment of ecstatic dancing to the rise of individualism beginning in the sixteenth century","Ehrenreich noted that if homosexual attraction is the love that dares not speak its name, the love that binds people to the collective has no name at all to speak","Durkheim insisted there were social facts - such as the suicide rate or norms about patriotism - that are not reducible to facts about individuals, and he described Homo sapiens as Homo duplex, existing as an individual and as part of the larger society","Durkheim's second set of social sentiments makes the person 'simply a part of a whole, whose actions I follow, and whose influence I am subject to'"]},{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Social Psychology | Group Cohesion | Self-Transcendence","topic":"Engineering the Hive: How Leaders Create Group Unity","stage_1_observation":"A cult leader requires initiates to undergo a painful hazing ritual. A drill sergeant makes recruits march in perfect unison for hours while shouting cadence. A football coach has the team do synchronized warm-up drills before each game. All three are using the same psychological mechanism: synchrony and shared suffering create group cohesion.","stage_1_question":"Why does shared physical effort, synchrony, or suffering create such powerful bonds between people?","stage_1_correct_answer":"Synchrony triggers the hive switch. Moving in rhythm with others activates endorphin release and creates a sense of shared intentionality. Shared suffering (effort justification) deepens the bond because the rider must justify having endured it: if I suffered for this group, it must be valuable. The combination of synchrony and shared sacrifice is the most powerful group-binding force known. This is why effective groups across cultures independently invented marching, chanting, and shared physical challenges.","stage_2_intervention":"You are the new CEO of a company with toxic silos. Engineering and Sales actively hate each other. They barely speak. Finger-pointing is constant. Team-building happy hours have failed. According to the hive switch research, what kind of intervention could break the silos and create genuine unity?","stage_2_correct_answer":"Create a shared physical challenge that requires synchrony and cooperation. A weekend ropes course, a company-wide hike, or a team obstacle race where both teams must work together to succeed. The key elements: (1) synchrony - activities where everyone moves together (group calisthenics, moving a large object together). (2) Shared hardship - something moderately difficult that requires persistence (a long hike, a challenging puzzle). (3) Shared goal - a superordinate objective neither team can achieve alone. (4) Debrief and ritual - afterward, create a shared symbol or story that represents the experience. Happy hours fail because they do not trigger the hive switch. Synchronous, challenging, goal-directed activity triggers it reliably.","boss_battle_explanation":"Layer 1 - Hive Switch Trigger: Synchrony + shared intentionality + sacred focus. Layer 2 - Neurochemistry: Endorphins, oxytocin, and dopamine create a natural high from group bonding. Layer 3 - Effort Justification: The rider justifies the effort by valuing the group more. Layer 4 - Sacred Symbol: The shared experience creates a story, symbol, or ritual that represents the group and can be revisited to rekindle the bond. Layer 5 - Risks: The hive switch can be hijacked by demagogues. The same mechanism that bonds a team can bond a lynch mob.","player_mission":"","difficulty_level":"Intermediate","visual_frame_description":"A grand hall where one banner reads 'Ask not what your country can do for you' and a second, in heavy black type, preaches renunciation of self-interest for a purely spiritual existence; below, two glass nations stand side by side, one swarming with little glowing hives and one flat and dark.","key_concepts":["William McNeill, drafted into the U.S. Army in September 1941, found that prolonged movement in unison during drilling produced a strange sense of personal enlargement, a sort of swelling out, becoming bigger than life","McNeill hypothesized that muscular bonding - moving together in time - evolved long before the beginning of recorded history for shutting down the self and creating a temporary superorganism","McNeill concluded that the key innovation of Greek, Roman, and later European armies was synchronous drilling and marching, and the Macedonian phalanx was the superorganism Alexander the Great used to defeat much larger armies","McNeill studied accounts of men in battle and found that men risk their lives not so much for their country or their ideals as for their comrades-in-arms","One veteran quoted by McNeill said that when their 'I' passes insensibly into a 'we,' self-sacrifice becomes easy, for 'I may fall, but I do not die, for that which is real in me goes forward and lives on in the comrades for whom I gave up my life'","The hive hypothesis states that human beings are conditional hive creatures who have the ability, under special conditions, to transcend self-interest and lose themselves temporarily and ecstatically in something larger than themselves","The hive switch is a group-related adaptation that can only be explained by a theory of between-group selection, as Williams said - it is an adaptation for making groups more cohesive","Barbara Ehrenreich's Dancing in the Streets argues that collective and ecstatic dancing is a nearly universal biotechnology for binding groups together and a form of muscular bonding that fosters love, trust, and equality","Early Christianity was a danced religion until dancing in church was suppressed in the Middle Ages, and Ehrenreich traces Europe's abandonment of ecstatic dancing to the rise of individualism beginning in the sixteenth century","Ehrenreich noted that if homosexual attraction is the love that dares not speak its name, the love that binds people to the collective has no name at all to speak","Durkheim insisted there were social facts - such as the suicide rate or norms about patriotism - that are not reducible to facts about individuals, and he described Homo sapiens as Homo duplex, existing as an individual and as part of the larger society","Durkheim's second set of social sentiments makes the person 'simply a part of a whole, whose actions I follow, and whose influence I am subject to'"]},{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Primatology | Precursors to Morality","topic":"De Waal's Building Blocks: Primate Precursors of Human Morality","column_a_label":"Building Blocks Present in Chimpanzees and Bonobos - Empathy, reciprocity, fairness, reconciliation, and internalized social norms exist in rudimentary form in our closest relatives.","column_b_label":"Uniquely Human Additions - Language, cumulative culture, large-scale cooperation, complex moral systems, and the ability to form moral communities around abstract principles.","rows":[{"scenario":"A chimpanzee consoles a defeated rival by embracing and grooming them after a fight","correct_column":"A","reason":"Reconciliation and empathy are present in chimpanzees — de Waal documented this extensively in Chimpanzee Politics"},{"scenario":"A capuchin monkey refuses a cucumber reward when it sees another monkey getting a grape for the same task","correct_column":"A","reason":"Fairness sensitivity exists in non-human primates — Brosnan and de Waal 2003 found that monkeys notice and protest inequity"},{"scenario":"Two chimpanzees carry a heavy log together to access food neither could access alone","correct_column":"B","reason":"While chimps cooperate, they rarely engage in the kind of planned, coordinated collaboration that requires shared intentionality and commitment to a joint goal"},{"scenario":"A human gives blood to a stranger they will never meet, with no expectation of return","correct_column":"B","reason":"Large-scale altruism toward anonymous strangers is uniquely human and requires cultural institutions and moral communities"},{"scenario":"A bonobo shares food with a non-relative after a grooming session","correct_column":"A","reason":"Reciprocal altruism exists in rudimentary form — de Waal found that bonobos share more with those who groomed them earlier"}],"explanation":"De Waal argues that morality has evolutionary precursors. Chimpanzees and bonobos show empathy (consolation behavior, yawn contagion), reciprocity (food sharing based on prior grooming), and proto-fairness (the grape-cucumber inequity aversion). But humanity added something new: the ability to create moral communities around abstract sacred values, enforced by third-party punishment and justified by shared narratives. Haidt uses de Waal to show that morality is not a thin veneer over a selfish nature. The building blocks were present in our primate ancestors. Group selection then built upon these blocks to create the uniquely human righteous mind.","player_mission":"","difficulty_level":"Advanced","visual_frame_description":"A corporate boardroom split down the middle: on one side a flowchart of incentives under surveillance cameras, on the other a floor of employees exercising in synchrony; a single clapping hand hovers above both, captioned: leadership is the complement of followership.","key_concepts":["William McNeill, drafted into the U.S. Army in September 1941, found that prolonged movement in unison during drilling produced a strange sense of personal enlargement, a sort of swelling out, becoming bigger than life","McNeill hypothesized that muscular bonding - moving together in time - evolved long before the beginning of recorded history for shutting down the self and creating a temporary superorganism","McNeill concluded that the key innovation of Greek, Roman, and later European armies was synchronous drilling and marching, and the Macedonian phalanx was the superorganism Alexander the Great used to defeat much larger armies","McNeill studied accounts of men in battle and found that men risk their lives not so much for their country or their ideals as for their comrades-in-arms","One veteran quoted by McNeill said that when their 'I' passes insensibly into a 'we,' self-sacrifice becomes easy, for 'I may fall, but I do not die, for that which is real in me goes forward and lives on in the comrades for whom I gave up my life'","The hive hypothesis states that human beings are conditional hive creatures who have the ability, under special conditions, to transcend self-interest and lose themselves temporarily and ecstatically in something larger than themselves","The hive switch is a group-related adaptation that can only be explained by a theory of between-group selection, as Williams said - it is an adaptation for making groups more cohesive","Barbara Ehrenreich's Dancing in the Streets argues that collective and ecstatic dancing is a nearly universal biotechnology for binding groups together and a form of muscular bonding that fosters love, trust, and equality","Early Christianity was a danced religion until dancing in church was suppressed in the Middle Ages, and Ehrenreich traces Europe's abandonment of ecstatic dancing to the rise of individualism beginning in the sixteenth century","Ehrenreich noted that if homosexual attraction is the love that dares not speak its name, the love that binds people to the collective has no name at all to speak","Durkheim insisted there were social facts - such as the suicide rate or norms about patriotism - that are not reducible to facts about individuals, and he described Homo sapiens as Homo duplex, existing as an individual and as part of the larger society","Durkheim's second set of social sentiments makes the person 'simply a part of a whole, whose actions I follow, and whose influence I am subject to'"]}]},{"id":"rm_ch10b","title":"Chapter 10: The Hive Switch (Part 2)","pages":"315-354","assets":[{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Anthropology | Collective Rituals | Hive Switch","topic":"Ecstatic Dancing: The Original Hive Switch Technology","stage_1_observation":"Throughout human history and across cultures, ecstatic dancing has been a central ritual. Medieval Christians danced in churches until it was suppressed. Sufi whirling dervishes spin into trance. San Bushmen dance all night around fires. Modern ravers dance to electronic beats. In all cases, the pattern is the same: synchrony, exhaustion, loss of self-consciousness, and a sense of merging with something larger. Barbara Ehrenreich argues that ecstatic dancing was early humanity's biotechnology for group bonding.","stage_1_question":"Why does ecstatic dancing produce such powerful experiences of unity and self-transcendence across all cultures?","stage_1_correct_answer":"Dancing triggers the hive switch through multiple channels simultaneously: (1) Synchrony — moving in rhythm with others activates mirror neurons and releases endorphins. (2) Exhaustion — physical exertion lowers ego defenses and reduces self-monitoring. (3) Shared intentionality — everyone is doing the same thing for the same purpose. (4) Sensory overload — rhythmic sound and repetitive movement overwhelm conscious processing. Together these dissolve the boundary between self and group. Haidt argues this is why virtually all human societies independently invented collective dance rituals — the hive switch is a universal human capacity.","stage_2_intervention":"You are the leader of a newly formed team that has zero cohesion. They have never worked together, come from different departments, and have no shared identity. Traditional team-building (trust falls, icebreakers) has failed. You have one afternoon to build genuine team bonding. You cannot use religious rituals. Design an ecstatic dance intervention for a modern secular workplace.","stage_2_correct_answer":"Use the principles of ecstatic dance without the religious context. (1) Physical synchrony — start with simple group exercises where everyone moves together (group calisthenics, carrying something heavy together in rhythm, or a drum circle). (2) Music — use rhythmic, repetitive music that fills the space and demands a physical response. (3) Progressive intensity — start easy, build to moderate physical exertion over 30-45 minutes. (4) No talking — minimize verbal processing; let the body lead. (5) Shared debrief — after the exertion, share a meal together where people can reflect. The key is that the synchrony and physical exertion do the bonding work — talking about it afterward helps the rider make sense of what the elephant experienced. This is not a party. It is applied Durkheimian biotechnology.","boss_battle_explanation":"Layer 1 - Universal Human Practice: Ecstatic dancing appears in all cultures as a bonding technology. Layer 2 - Mechanisms: Synchrony, exhaustion, shared intentionality, sensory overload. Layer 3 - Neurochemistry: Endorphins, oxytocin, dopamine. Layer 4 - Dissolution of Self: The boundary between self and group temporarily dissolves. Layer 5 - Modern Application: Secular versions of collective effervescence (concerts, sports events, group exercise, raves) serve the same function. The hive switch is always available — it just needs the right triggers.","player_mission":"","difficulty_level":"Intermediate","visual_frame_description":"A grand hall where one banner reads 'Ask not what your country can do for you' and a second, in heavy black type, preaches renunciation of self-interest for a purely spiritual existence; below, two glass nations stand side by side, one swarming with little glowing hives and one flat and dark.","key_concepts":["William McNeill, drafted into the U.S. Army in September 1941, found that prolonged movement in unison during drilling produced a strange sense of personal enlargement, a sort of swelling out, becoming bigger than life","McNeill hypothesized that muscular bonding - moving together in time - evolved long before the beginning of recorded history for shutting down the self and creating a temporary superorganism","McNeill concluded that the key innovation of Greek, Roman, and later European armies was synchronous drilling and marching, and the Macedonian phalanx was the superorganism Alexander the Great used to defeat much larger armies","McNeill studied accounts of men in battle and found that men risk their lives not so much for their country or their ideals as for their comrades-in-arms","One veteran quoted by McNeill said that when their 'I' passes insensibly into a 'we,' self-sacrifice becomes easy, for 'I may fall, but I do not die, for that which is real in me goes forward and lives on in the comrades for whom I gave up my life'","The hive hypothesis states that human beings are conditional hive creatures who have the ability, under special conditions, to transcend self-interest and lose themselves temporarily and ecstatically in something larger than themselves","The hive switch is a group-related adaptation that can only be explained by a theory of between-group selection, as Williams said - it is an adaptation for making groups more cohesive","Barbara Ehrenreich's Dancing in the Streets argues that collective and ecstatic dancing is a nearly universal biotechnology for binding groups together and a form of muscular bonding that fosters love, trust, and equality","Early Christianity was a danced religion until dancing in church was suppressed in the Middle Ages, and Ehrenreich traces Europe's abandonment of ecstatic dancing to the rise of individualism beginning in the sixteenth century","Ehrenreich noted that if homosexual attraction is the love that dares not speak its name, the love that binds people to the collective has no name at all to speak","Durkheim insisted there were social facts - such as the suicide rate or norms about patriotism - that are not reducible to facts about individuals, and he described Homo sapiens as Homo duplex, existing as an individual and as part of the larger society","Durkheim's second set of social sentiments makes the person 'simply a part of a whole, whose actions I follow, and whose influence I am subject to'"]}]},{"id":"rm_ch11","title":"Chapter 11: Religion Is a Team Sport","pages":"355-395","assets":[{"asset_type":"DECEPTION_AUDIT_FILE","domain":"Psychology of Religion | Group Selection","topic":"The Puzzle of Religion: Why Would a Rational Mind Believe in Gods?","subject_baseline":"Religious belief is universal across human cultures and history. Despite enormous variation in specific beliefs, all religions share certain features: supernatural agents, rituals, sacred texts, moral codes, and community bonding. Secularization in the West led many to predict religions decline - yet religion remains powerful globally.","interrogation_transcript":"The New Atheists (Dawkins, Hitchens, Harris) argued that religion is a delusion, a virus of the mind, a byproduct of cognitive biases that should be eliminated through reason. Haidt offers a different view: religion is not a cognitive accident. It is a group-level adaptation that enabled large-scale cooperation. Religious communities outcompeted secular ones in the ancestral environment because they could solve the free-rider problem through supernatural surveillance (gods see everything), costly signaling (rituals prove commitment), and group cohesion (shared sacred values).","observed_nonverbal_log":["Religious belief is not an individual cognitive error but a group adaptation","Religions solve the free-rider problem through supernatural policing","Costly rituals (fasting, pilgrimage, tithing) signal genuine commitment","Shared belief in sacred values creates the strongest possible ingroup bonds"],"question":"If religion evolved as a group-binding mechanism, does the truth or falsity of religious claims matter for its adaptive function?","correct_diagnosis":"Not directly. From a group-selection perspective, a religion does not need to be true to be adaptive. It needs to motivate cooperation, punish free-riders, and coordinate collective action. Belief in supernatural watchers who punish oath-breakers is extremely effective at maintaining honesty in large groups where reputation is insufficient. The question Haidt poses: if atheism is correct, can atheist societies develop equally effective group-binding mechanisms? Secular societies have replaced religious rituals with national holidays, secular ceremonies, and civic institutions - but the binding power of these may be weaker than religious bonds. The Durkheimian question: can a society survive without the sacred?","distractors":["Religion is purely a byproduct of cognitive biases (agency detection, theory of mind) and has no adaptive function","Religions that were false died out because they could not compete with true religions that gave accurate information","Religion is a parasite that exploits human cognitive vulnerabilities for its own replication without benefiting humans"],"diagnostic_breakdown":"Stage 1 - Supernatural Surveillance: The belief that gods see everything reduces cheating when nobody is watching. Stage 2 - Costly Signaling: Public rituals that are expensive or painful prove genuine commitment - fakers will not pay the cost. Stage 3 - Group Cohesion: Shared sacred values create the strongest possible ingroup bonds. Stage 4 - Moral Community: Religious groups enforce moral norms and punish violators. Stage 5 - Intergroup Competition: Groups with stronger religious bonding outcompete groups with weaker bonding. This is not an argument for the truth of any religion. It is an explanation of why religion evolved and why it persists.","player_mission":"If religion evolved as a group-binding mechanism, does the truth or falsity of religious claims matter for its adaptive function?","difficulty_level":"Intermediate","visual_frame_description":"A pathology lab where slides of mental machinery are projected, each misfiring into a god-shaped glow; on one bench a parasite-controlled ant climbs a blade of grass while the caption reads 'suicide for the ant, adaptive for the parasite.'","key_concepts":["Every Saturday in the fall, millions of people pack themselves into stadiums at colleges across the United States to participate in a ritual that can only be described as tribal","At UVA the ritual begins with costumes: men wear dress shirts with UVA neckties, women wear skirts or dresses sometimes with pearl necklaces, and some students paint the logo of the Cavaliers - a V crossed by two swords - on their faces","The students sing the same song UVA students have sung together for over a century, to the tune of 'Auld Lang Syne,' beginning 'That good old song of Wah-hoo-wah'","After locking arms and swaying as a single mass, the students illustrate McNeill's thesis that muscular bonding warms people up for coordinated military action, with aggressive fist-pumping in sync with a nonsensical battle chant","Collective effervescence is guaranteed at the game, along with collective outrage at questionable calls by the referees, collective triumph if the team wins, and collective grief if the team loses","From a Durkheimian perspective the function of the cheering and chanting is not victory but the creation of a community - a college football game is a superb analogy for religion","From a naive perspective college football is an extravagant, costly, wasteful institution that impairs rational thinking, but from a sociologically informed perspective it pulls people up from the profane level to the sacred level and flips the hive switch","The school spirit it augments attracts better students and more alumni donations, which improves the experience for the entire community, including professors with no interest in sports","Durkheim defined a religion as a unified system of beliefs and practices relative to sacred things, things set apart and forbidden, which unite into one single moral community called a Church all those who adhere to them","Religion cannot be studied in lone individuals any more than hivishness can be studied in lone bees - religions are social facts","Many scientists misunderstand religion because they examine only what is most visible - individuals and their supernatural beliefs - and conclude it is an extravagant, costly, wasteful institution that leaves a long trail of victims","The third principle of moral psychology, that morality binds and blinds, must be honored before rendering a fair judgment about religion and its relationship to morality and politics"]},{"asset_type":"DYNAMIC_DIALOGUE_SIM","domain":"Psychology of Religion | Interfaith Understanding","topic":"Sacred Values: What If Your Sacred Is Someone Elses Profane?","dialogue":[{"speaker":"Secular Skeptic","line":"Religion causes more harm than good. The Crusades, the Inquisition, 9/11, the Troubles. We would be better off without it. Faith is belief without evidence, and belief without evidence is dangerous."},{"speaker":"Religious Believer","line":"You are looking at the worst examples and ignoring the good. Religions feed the hungry, clothe the poor, comfort the dying, and build communities. My faith is the most important thing in my life. It gives me meaning, purpose, and a moral framework."},{"speaker":"You (Mediator)","line":"Haidt would say you are both right in ways you do not realize. The skeptic correctly identifies that religion can fuel intergroup conflict - morality binds and blinds. The believer correctly identifies that religion builds cohesive communities that do enormous good. The key insight: religions capacity for harm and for good come from the same source - the hive switch that binds groups together around sacred values. A group bound by sacred values can move mountains. Whether they move mountains to build hospitals or to destroy enemies depends on context, leadership, and which sacred values are activated. The question should shift from is religion good or bad? to how do we channel groupishness toward constructive rather than destructive ends?"}],"psychological_breakdown":"Haidts approach to religion is neither the atheist debunking nor the believer apologetics. It is functionalist: religion evolved because groups that had it outcompeted groups that did not. The same binding power that makes religions capable of great charity also makes them capable of great cruelty. Understanding religion as a group-selection adaptation explains both its universality and its moral ambiguity. It does not answer the truth question (does God exist?). It answers the function question (what does religion do for groups?). The answer: it enables the extraordinary human capacity for large-scale cooperation by making groups sacred.","player_mission":"","difficulty_level":"Intermediate","visual_frame_description":"A panel room where three books float above the table - The End of Faith, The God Delusion, Breaking the Spell - while behind them a football arcs across a stadium, and a caption reads: understanding religion by studying beliefs about God is like understanding football by studying the movements of the ball.","key_concepts":["Every Saturday in the fall, millions of people pack themselves into stadiums at colleges across the United States to participate in a ritual that can only be described as tribal","At UVA the ritual begins with costumes: men wear dress shirts with UVA neckties, women wear skirts or dresses sometimes with pearl necklaces, and some students paint the logo of the Cavaliers - a V crossed by two swords - on their faces","The students sing the same song UVA students have sung together for over a century, to the tune of 'Auld Lang Syne,' beginning 'That good old song of Wah-hoo-wah'","After locking arms and swaying as a single mass, the students illustrate McNeill's thesis that muscular bonding warms people up for coordinated military action, with aggressive fist-pumping in sync with a nonsensical battle chant","Collective effervescence is guaranteed at the game, along with collective outrage at questionable calls by the referees, collective triumph if the team wins, and collective grief if the team loses","From a Durkheimian perspective the function of the cheering and chanting is not victory but the creation of a community - a college football game is a superb analogy for religion","From a naive perspective college football is an extravagant, costly, wasteful institution that impairs rational thinking, but from a sociologically informed perspective it pulls people up from the profane level to the sacred level and flips the hive switch","The school spirit it augments attracts better students and more alumni donations, which improves the experience for the entire community, including professors with no interest in sports","Durkheim defined a religion as a unified system of beliefs and practices relative to sacred things, things set apart and forbidden, which unite into one single moral community called a Church all those who adhere to them","Religion cannot be studied in lone individuals any more than hivishness can be studied in lone bees - religions are social facts","Many scientists misunderstand religion because they examine only what is most visible - individuals and their supernatural beliefs - and conclude it is an extravagant, costly, wasteful institution that leaves a long trail of victims","The third principle of moral psychology, that morality binds and blinds, must be honored before rendering a fair judgment about religion and its relationship to morality and politics"]},{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Psychology of Religion | Competing Models","topic":"Two Models of Religion: New Atheist (Belief-First) vs Durkheimian (Bonding-First)","column_a_label":"New Atheist Model (Dawkins, Dennett, Harris) - Religion is a set of beliefs about supernatural beings. These beliefs are false and irrational. Religion persists because it is a cognitive byproduct or a meme/virus. The solution: education and reason.","column_b_label":"Durkheimian Model (Durkheim, Haidt) - Religion is a set of practices that bind groups together around sacred values. Beliefs are secondary to bonding. Religion persists because groups that had it outcompeted groups that did not. The solution: understand its function before criticizing it.","rows":[{"scenario":"A scientist argues that belief in God is like believing in Santa Claus — comforting but false","correct_column":"A","reason":"The New Atheist focus is on the truth value of beliefs — false beliefs are delusions regardless of their social function"},{"scenario":"An anthropologist observes that a community without shared rituals quickly fragments into competing factions","correct_column":"B","reason":"The Durkheimian focus is on the binding function of religion — rituals create the collective effervescence that holds groups together"},{"scenario":"A missionary focuses on teaching correct doctrine to convert people","correct_column":"A","reason":"The belief-first approach assumes that changing beliefs changes everything — get the doctrine right, and behavior follows"},{"scenario":"A congregation feels closest to God and to each other during shared singing and ritual, not during the sermon","correct_column":"B","reason":"The bonding-first approach predicts that the collective experience matters more than the specific beliefs — the effervescence is the point"},{"scenario":"Dawkins argues that religion is a virus of the mind that hijacks cognitive modules for its own replication","correct_column":"A","reason":"The meme/virus theory treats religion as a parasite that exploits cognitive vulnerabilities without providing benefits"}],"explanation":"Haidt contrasts these models throughout Chapter 11. The New Atheist model (which he calls the Platonic/Delusion model) sees religion as primarily about false beliefs. The Durkheimian model sees religion as primarily about group bonding. Haidt argues the Durkheimian model better explains: (1) why religion is universal across cultures, (2) why religious people are happier and more generous, (3) why even secular societies develop quasi-religious rituals around national holidays and sports teams, and (4) why atheist societies have not replaced religion with anything equally binding. He does not argue that religion is true — only that it is an adaptation, not a bug.","player_mission":"","difficulty_level":"Advanced","visual_frame_description":"A sorting machine fed by two columns, Memes and Moralistic Gods; each god arrives thundering against adultery and oath-breaking, while a balance scale weighs costly sacrifices against commune longevity and a graph rises perfectly linearly for the religious communes.","key_concepts":["Every Saturday in the fall, millions of people pack themselves into stadiums at colleges across the United States to participate in a ritual that can only be described as tribal","At UVA the ritual begins with costumes: men wear dress shirts with UVA neckties, women wear skirts or dresses sometimes with pearl necklaces, and some students paint the logo of the Cavaliers - a V crossed by two swords - on their faces","The students sing the same song UVA students have sung together for over a century, to the tune of 'Auld Lang Syne,' beginning 'That good old song of Wah-hoo-wah'","After locking arms and swaying as a single mass, the students illustrate McNeill's thesis that muscular bonding warms people up for coordinated military action, with aggressive fist-pumping in sync with a nonsensical battle chant","Collective effervescence is guaranteed at the game, along with collective outrage at questionable calls by the referees, collective triumph if the team wins, and collective grief if the team loses","From a Durkheimian perspective the function of the cheering and chanting is not victory but the creation of a community - a college football game is a superb analogy for religion","From a naive perspective college football is an extravagant, costly, wasteful institution that impairs rational thinking, but from a sociologically informed perspective it pulls people up from the profane level to the sacred level and flips the hive switch","The school spirit it augments attracts better students and more alumni donations, which improves the experience for the entire community, including professors with no interest in sports","Durkheim defined a religion as a unified system of beliefs and practices relative to sacred things, things set apart and forbidden, which unite into one single moral community called a Church all those who adhere to them","Religion cannot be studied in lone individuals any more than hivishness can be studied in lone bees - religions are social facts","Many scientists misunderstand religion because they examine only what is most visible - individuals and their supernatural beliefs - and conclude it is an extravagant, costly, wasteful institution that leaves a long trail of victims","The third principle of moral psychology, that morality binds and blinds, must be honored before rendering a fair judgment about religion and its relationship to morality and politics"]}]},{"id":"rm_ch12a","title":"Chapter 12: Can't We All Disagree More Constructively? (Part 1)","pages":"396-436","assets":[{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Political Psychology | Bridging Divides","topic":"The Moral Matrix Trap: How to Escape Your Own Righteousness","stage_1_observation":"Haidt describes his own journey. He was a liberal Democrat who believed conservatives were motivated by selfishness, stupidity, and hatred. Then he started reading conservative thinkers seriously and administering moral foundations questionnaires. He discovered that conservatives have genuine moral concerns (Loyalty, Authority, Sanctity) that he had dismissed as non-moral. The moral matrix trap: we believe our moral matrix is the only one, and everyone outside it is morally deficient.","stage_1_question":"Why is it so hard to see that the other side might have a genuine moral framework rather than being selfish, stupid, or evil?","stage_1_correct_answer":"The righteous mind is designed for coalitional defense. When we encounter someone with different moral foundations, we experience them as not just wrong but dangerous to our moral order. Our elephant reacts with disgust and contempt. Our rider constructs elaborate reasons why they are bad people. The moral matrix trap works because we cannot directly experience the other sides intuitions. We can only hear their post-hoc rationalizations, which sound weak and self-serving to us because we evaluate them through our own foundations.","stage_2_intervention":"You are attending a family gathering. Your uncle makes a political comment that triggers you. Your first instinct is to argue, correct him, show him the evidence. According to Haidts research on moral matrices, what should you do instead to actually understand each other?","stage_2_correct_answer":"Three steps: (1) Suspend the correction instinct. Your first impulse to argue is the elephant defending its territory. Instead, signal genuine curiosity: Can you help me understand why you feel that way? This shifts from adversarial to collaborative. (2) Listen for the moral foundation. Behind his position is likely a Loyalty concern (protecting our country), Authority concern (respecting tradition/order), Sanctity concern (protecting sacred values), or Liberty concern (freedom from government overreach). Name the foundation you hear: It sounds like you are concerned about people not following the rules and the breakdown of order. Is that close? (3) Validate the foundation without agreeing with the policy. You can say: I understand why that would concern you. I also care about community safety and order - thats important. Then you can share your own concern from a different foundation without dismissing his. The goal is not to win the argument but to understand the moral logic driving his position.","boss_battle_explanation":"Layer 1 - Moral Matrix Trap: Assuming everyone shares your moral foundations. Layer 2 - The Correction Reflex: Trying to win arguments rather than understand positions. Layer 3 - Foundation Listening: Learning to hear the moral foundation behind the rhetoric. Layer 4 - Validation Without Agreement: You can acknowledge someones moral concern without accepting their policy conclusion. Layer 5 - The Goal Shift: From convert the opponent to understand the other. The righteous mind will always divide us. But if we understand each others righteousness, we can disagree more constructively.","player_mission":"","difficulty_level":"Intermediate","visual_frame_description":"A courtroom where two giant hands, one labeled Care and one labeled Liberty, hold a scale of externalities; exhibits line the bench - a leaded gas pump, a can of peas, a hospital checklist, a beehive with a single dying bee being rescued by a giant hand - while a banner reads 'Morality binds and blinds.'","key_concepts":["Haidt defines ideology simply as a set of beliefs about the proper order of society and how it can be achieved","At the French Assembly of 1789, delegates who favored preservation sat on the right side of the chamber while those who favored change sat on the left, and the terms right and left have stood for conservatism and liberalism ever since","Political theorists since Marx assumed people chose ideologies to further their self-interest, but when political scientists looked into it they found that self-interest does a remarkably poor job of predicting political attitudes","Studies of twins in the 1980s showed that identical twins are more similar than fraternal twins on just about everything, and identical twins reared in separate households usually turn out very similar","Genetics explains between a third and a half of the variability among people on their political attitudes, while being raised in a liberal or conservative household accounts for much less","Innate does not mean unmalleable; it means organized in advance of experience, with the genes guiding the construction of the brain in the uterus as a first draft that gets revised by childhood experiences","Genes related to glutamate and serotonin, both involved in the brain's response to threat and fear, fit findings that conservatives react more strongly to signs of danger, including germs, contamination, and sudden blasts of white noise","Genes related to receptors for the neurotransmitter dopamine, tied to sensation-seeking and openness to experience, correlate with liberalism, echoing Montaigne's line that the only things he finds rewarding are variety and the enjoyment of diversity","John Jost's major review found that nearly all the traits distinguishing liberals and conservatives are conceptually related to threat sensitivity - conservatives react more strongly to reminders of death - or to openness to experience","Even though the effects of any single gene are tiny, the genes collectively give some people brains more reactive to threats and less pleased by novelty, and these are the two main personality factors that distinguish the sides","Women who called themselves liberals as adults had been rated by their nursery school teachers as more curious, verbal, and self-reliant, but also more assertive and aggressive, less obedient and neat","Genes contribute to just about every aspect of our personalities: how much you like jazz, spicy foods, and abstract art, your likelihood of divorce, your degree of religiosity, and your political orientation as an adult"]},{"asset_type":"DYNAMIC_DIALOGUE_SIM","domain":"Political Psychology | Civil Discourse","topic":"The Civil Discourse Challenge: Debating Across the Moral Divide","dialogue":[{"speaker":"Liberal","line":"Universal healthcare is a human right. Every developed country has it except the US. People are dying because they cannot afford insulin. How can you possibly oppose this?"},{"speaker":"Conservative","line":"I do not want people to die. But healthcare is not a right. It is a service. The government should not control healthcare. When the government runs things, costs go up and quality goes down. Look at the VA system. Look at wait times in Canada."},{"speaker":"Mediator","line":"Both of you are using different moral foundations. The liberal uses Care: people are suffering and need help. The conservative uses Liberty: government control is oppressive and inefficient. Neither is wrong about their foundation. But notice: neither addresses the others concern. The liberal does not address the Liberty concern about government overreach. The conservative does not address the Care concern about dying patients. A constructive conversation would require each to acknowledge the others moral concern. The liberal could say: I understand your worry about government power - I share that concern. Here is why I think this system has enough checks and balances. The conservative could say: I do not want people to die. Can we design a system that helps the vulnerable without giving the government too much control? Both need to validate the others foundation before their own can be heard."}],"psychological_breakdown":"Constructive disagreement requires escaping the moral matrix trap. Key techniques: (1) Find the moral foundation behind the others position and validate it before stating your own. (2) Distinguish between the moral foundation and the policy - you can acknowledge the concern without endorsing the policy. (3) Remember that the other person is also a participant in a moral order and thinks they are doing good. (4) Use Haidts principle: the mind is divided like a rider on an elephant. You cannot win the rider by arguing about reasons because the elephant already decided. To reach the elephant, you need to speak to its moral concerns.","player_mission":"","difficulty_level":"Intermediate","visual_frame_description":"A holiday table divided down the middle: on one side a wall of books about oppression and a poster of Obama's 2008 promise of change; on the other a bank ledger, a church pew, and a campaign button reading 'Country First'; at the head of the table an electric guitar stands unplayed.","key_concepts":["Haidt defines ideology simply as a set of beliefs about the proper order of society and how it can be achieved","At the French Assembly of 1789, delegates who favored preservation sat on the right side of the chamber while those who favored change sat on the left, and the terms right and left have stood for conservatism and liberalism ever since","Political theorists since Marx assumed people chose ideologies to further their self-interest, but when political scientists looked into it they found that self-interest does a remarkably poor job of predicting political attitudes","Studies of twins in the 1980s showed that identical twins are more similar than fraternal twins on just about everything, and identical twins reared in separate households usually turn out very similar","Genetics explains between a third and a half of the variability among people on their political attitudes, while being raised in a liberal or conservative household accounts for much less","Innate does not mean unmalleable; it means organized in advance of experience, with the genes guiding the construction of the brain in the uterus as a first draft that gets revised by childhood experiences","Genes related to glutamate and serotonin, both involved in the brain's response to threat and fear, fit findings that conservatives react more strongly to signs of danger, including germs, contamination, and sudden blasts of white noise","Genes related to receptors for the neurotransmitter dopamine, tied to sensation-seeking and openness to experience, correlate with liberalism, echoing Montaigne's line that the only things he finds rewarding are variety and the enjoyment of diversity","John Jost's major review found that nearly all the traits distinguishing liberals and conservatives are conceptually related to threat sensitivity - conservatives react more strongly to reminders of death - or to openness to experience","Even though the effects of any single gene are tiny, the genes collectively give some people brains more reactive to threats and less pleased by novelty, and these are the two main personality factors that distinguish the sides","Women who called themselves liberals as adults had been rated by their nursery school teachers as more curious, verbal, and self-reliant, but also more assertive and aggressive, less obedient and neat","Genes contribute to just about every aspect of our personalities: how much you like jazz, spicy foods, and abstract art, your likelihood of divorce, your degree of religiosity, and your political orientation as an adult"]},{"asset_type":"CUE_SCRUBBER_STATION","domain":"Social Psychology | Situational Factors | Helping Behavior","topic":"The Good Samaritan Study: When Character Does Not Matter","visual_frame_description":"Darley and Batson 1973: Princeton Theological Seminary students were told they were to give a brief talk about the Good Samaritan parable. Some were told they were late (high hurry), some had moderate time, some had plenty of time. On the way to the talk, each passed a slumped, coughing man in a doorway. Of those in a hurry, only 10% stopped to help. Of those with time to spare, 63% stopped. The seminarians literally walked past a suffering person while on their way to talk about the Good Samaritan.","target_channel":"Situational Override | Character-Irrelevant Helping","question":"Why did seminarians studying the Good Samaritan parable walk past a person in clear need of help?","correct_cue":"Haidt uses this study to show the power of the situation over moral character. The seminarians were not bad people — they were people in a hurry. The situational pressure (time urgency) overrode their compassionate impulses and their explicit moral values. The elephant (intuitive compassion) was silenced by the urgency frame. The rider then justified the decision: someone else will help, I am late, he does not look that sick. The study proves that moral behavior is not primarily driven by character or values. It is driven by situational forces that activate or suppress moral intuitions.","distractors":["The seminarians were not truly religious — only nominally devout people who lacked genuine compassion","The helping scenario was ambiguous — participants were not sure the man actually needed help","The study has been debunked and the findings are not replicable"],"underlying_psychology":"The Good Samaritan study is a devastating demonstration of the power of the situation. It is the social psychology equivalent of the Milgram obedience study: good people fail to act morally when situational pressures are strong. The factors that predicted helping: (1) being in a hurry, (2) having just read the Good Samaritan parable (paradoxically, this did not increase helping — some seminarians stepped over the victim while mentally composing their talk about helping). The only reliable predictor was time pressure. This study supports Haidts argument that moral behavior is intuitive, not principled. When the intuition is suppressed (by hurry, distraction, or competing frames), the principle alone does not produce action.","player_mission":"Why did seminarians studying the Good Samaritan parable walk past a person in clear need of help?","difficulty_level":"Beginner","key_concepts":["Haidt defines ideology simply as a set of beliefs about the proper order of society and how it can be achieved","At the French Assembly of 1789, delegates who favored preservation sat on the right side of the chamber while those who favored change sat on the left, and the terms right and left have stood for conservatism and liberalism ever since","Political theorists since Marx assumed people chose ideologies to further their self-interest, but when political scientists looked into it they found that self-interest does a remarkably poor job of predicting political attitudes","Studies of twins in the 1980s showed that identical twins are more similar than fraternal twins on just about everything, and identical twins reared in separate households usually turn out very similar","Genetics explains between a third and a half of the variability among people on their political attitudes, while being raised in a liberal or conservative household accounts for much less","Innate does not mean unmalleable; it means organized in advance of experience, with the genes guiding the construction of the brain in the uterus as a first draft that gets revised by childhood experiences","Genes related to glutamate and serotonin, both involved in the brain's response to threat and fear, fit findings that conservatives react more strongly to signs of danger, including germs, contamination, and sudden blasts of white noise","Genes related to receptors for the neurotransmitter dopamine, tied to sensation-seeking and openness to experience, correlate with liberalism, echoing Montaigne's line that the only things he finds rewarding are variety and the enjoyment of diversity","John Jost's major review found that nearly all the traits distinguishing liberals and conservatives are conceptually related to threat sensitivity - conservatives react more strongly to reminders of death - or to openness to experience","Even though the effects of any single gene are tiny, the genes collectively give some people brains more reactive to threats and less pleased by novelty, and these are the two main personality factors that distinguish the sides","Women who called themselves liberals as adults had been rated by their nursery school teachers as more curious, verbal, and self-reliant, but also more assertive and aggressive, less obedient and neat","Genes contribute to just about every aspect of our personalities: how much you like jazz, spicy foods, and abstract art, your likelihood of divorce, your degree of religiosity, and your political orientation as an adult"]}]},{"id":"rm_ch12b","title":"Chapter 12: Can't We All Disagree More Constructively? (Part 2)","pages":"396-436","assets":[{"asset_type":"DYNAMIC_DIALOGUE_SIM","domain":"Evolutionary Psychology | Cultural Evolution","topic":"The Meme's Eye View: Dawkins on Religion as Cultural Replicator","dialogue":[{"speaker":"Evolutionary Biologist","line":"Religions are sets of memes that replicate through populations. A meme is a unit of cultural information — an idea, a ritual, a belief — that spreads from mind to mind like a gene spreads through bodies. Successful memes are those that get themselves replicated. Religions are very good at this because they include self-protection mechanisms: believe or suffer eternally, doubt is sin, questioners are heretics. The religion is not true. It is just good at surviving."},{"speaker":"Anthropologist","line":"But that frame misses the function. If religions were just viruses, why do religious communities consistently show greater generosity, lower suicide rates, and stronger social networks? Why do atheist societies not have higher rates of meaning and belonging? The meme metaphor treats religion as a parasite, but the evidence suggests it is more like a mutualist — it survives by providing value to its hosts."},{"speaker":"Mediator","line":"This is exactly the debate Haidt stages. Dawkins (The God Delusion) and Dennett (Breaking the Spell) argue that religion is a cognitive parasite that exploits evolved vulnerabilities. Haidt responds: even if true, that is only half the story. Religions also solve the free-rider problem, enable large-scale cooperation, and provide meaning. A proper understanding of religion requires both the meme lens and the function lens. The meme lens explains how religions spread. The function lens explains why groups need them."}],"psychological_breakdown":"The meme theory of religion (Dawkins 1976, Dennett 2006) explains cultural evolution through the same lens as genetic evolution: variation, selection, replication. Religions are collections of memes that have evolved to be good at replicating themselves. The Durkheimian/Haidt response: memes spread because they serve group-level functions, not just because they exploit cognitive vulnerabilities. The resolution: both perspectives are needed. Religion is a naturally occurring phenomenon that emerges from the human mind's combination of agency detection, groupishness, and the need for meaning. Understanding religion requires both the evolutionary lens (how it spreads) and the functional lens (what it does for groups).","player_mission":"","difficulty_level":"Intermediate","visual_frame_description":"A holiday table divided down the middle: on one side a wall of books about oppression and a poster of Obama's 2008 promise of change; on the other a bank ledger, a church pew, and a campaign button reading 'Country First'; at the head of the table an electric guitar stands unplayed.","key_concepts":["Haidt defines ideology simply as a set of beliefs about the proper order of society and how it can be achieved","At the French Assembly of 1789, delegates who favored preservation sat on the right side of the chamber while those who favored change sat on the left, and the terms right and left have stood for conservatism and liberalism ever since","Political theorists since Marx assumed people chose ideologies to further their self-interest, but when political scientists looked into it they found that self-interest does a remarkably poor job of predicting political attitudes","Studies of twins in the 1980s showed that identical twins are more similar than fraternal twins on just about everything, and identical twins reared in separate households usually turn out very similar","Genetics explains between a third and a half of the variability among people on their political attitudes, while being raised in a liberal or conservative household accounts for much less","Innate does not mean unmalleable; it means organized in advance of experience, with the genes guiding the construction of the brain in the uterus as a first draft that gets revised by childhood experiences","Genes related to glutamate and serotonin, both involved in the brain's response to threat and fear, fit findings that conservatives react more strongly to signs of danger, including germs, contamination, and sudden blasts of white noise","Genes related to receptors for the neurotransmitter dopamine, tied to sensation-seeking and openness to experience, correlate with liberalism, echoing Montaigne's line that the only things he finds rewarding are variety and the enjoyment of diversity","John Jost's major review found that nearly all the traits distinguishing liberals and conservatives are conceptually related to threat sensitivity - conservatives react more strongly to reminders of death - or to openness to experience","Even though the effects of any single gene are tiny, the genes collectively give some people brains more reactive to threats and less pleased by novelty, and these are the two main personality factors that distinguish the sides","Women who called themselves liberals as adults had been rated by their nursery school teachers as more curious, verbal, and self-reliant, but also more assertive and aggressive, less obedient and neat","Genes contribute to just about every aspect of our personalities: how much you like jazz, spicy foods, and abstract art, your likelihood of divorce, your degree of religiosity, and your political orientation as an adult"]}]},{"id":"rm_conclusion","title":"Conclusion: The One Lesson","pages":"437-440","assets":[{"asset_type":"DECEPTION_AUDIT_FILE","domain":"Moral Psychology | The Big Picture","topic":"The One Lesson: We Are All Self-Righteous and That Is Okay If We Understand It","subject_baseline":"A liberal activist and a conservative activist watch the same political rally. The liberal sees a hateful demagogue whipping up racism and ignorance. The conservative sees a patriot defending traditional values against cultural decline. Both are certain of their moral judgment. Both are right about their own moral matrix. Both are wrong about the others motives.","interrogation_transcript":"Haidts one lesson: The righteous mind was not designed to perceive truth objectively. It was designed to bind groups together and compete against other groups. This means that human beings are naturally self-righteous - we naturally believe our side is good and the other side is bad. This is not a defect that can be eliminated through education or reason. It is a feature of our evolved moral psychology. The only escape is understanding that everyone is doing it - including ourselves. The goal is not to achieve perfect objectivity (impossible) but to develop the humility to recognize our own righteous certainty as a product of our moral matrix.","observed_nonverbal_log":["Both sides are equally certain of their righteousness","Both sides see the other as the aggressor and themselves as the defender","Neither side can see their own blind spots","The structure of the disagreement is identical - only the content differs"],"question":"After 12 chapters of evidence that the righteous mind is designed for division, is there any hope for bridging political and moral divides?","correct_diagnosis":"Yes, but not through the rationalist fantasy of eliminating bias and achieving objectivity. The hope is in understanding. When you understand that moral matrices are real - that the other side is not evil or stupid but operating from different moral foundations - you can disagree without demonizing. The hope is not that we will agree. The hope is that we can treat each other with moral humility - recognizing that our own righteous certainty is partly a product of our evolved tribalism and cultural conditioning. Haidts one practical prescription: before judging, try to enter the others moral matrix. Read their best authors. Listen to their concerns in their language. You will still disagree. But you will no longer see each other as monsters.","distractors":["No - human nature is fundamentally divisive and political polarization will only increase until society collapses","Yes - as education spreads and people learn critical thinking, moral disagreement will eventually dissolve into rational consensus","No - the problem is structural (media polarization, gerrymandering, algorithmic echo chambers) and individual understanding cannot fix it"],"diagnostic_breakdown":"Stage 1 - Recognition: Understand that your moral certainty is partly a product of your moral matrix, not objective truth. Stage 2 - Curiosity: Ask what foundations drive the other sides positions. Stage 3 - Translation: Learn to express your concerns in the other sides moral language. Stage 4 - Respect: Treat the other side as operating from a genuine (different) moral order, not from ignorance or evil. Stage 5 - Cooperation: Find shared foundations (Liberty appeals to both sides) and superordinate goals (defending democracy, national prosperity). The righteous mind will always divide. But understanding why it divides can make the division less destructive.","player_mission":"After 12 chapters of evidence that the righteous mind is designed for division, is there any hope for bridging political and moral divides?","difficulty_level":"Intermediate","visual_frame_description":"A file room where two narratives unroll like scrolls: one showing crowds storming the Bastille and freeing the prisoners, the other a family reclaiming its home from termites and repairing the joists; a rubber stamp reads 'Morality binds and blinds.'","key_concepts":["Haidt defines ideology simply as a set of beliefs about the proper order of society and how it can be achieved","At the French Assembly of 1789, delegates who favored preservation sat on the right side of the chamber while those who favored change sat on the left, and the terms right and left have stood for conservatism and liberalism ever since","Political theorists since Marx assumed people chose ideologies to further their self-interest, but when political scientists looked into it they found that self-interest does a remarkably poor job of predicting political attitudes","Studies of twins in the 1980s showed that identical twins are more similar than fraternal twins on just about everything, and identical twins reared in separate households usually turn out very similar","Genetics explains between a third and a half of the variability among people on their political attitudes, while being raised in a liberal or conservative household accounts for much less","Innate does not mean unmalleable; it means organized in advance of experience, with the genes guiding the construction of the brain in the uterus as a first draft that gets revised by childhood experiences","Genes related to glutamate and serotonin, both involved in the brain's response to threat and fear, fit findings that conservatives react more strongly to signs of danger, including germs, contamination, and sudden blasts of white noise","Genes related to receptors for the neurotransmitter dopamine, tied to sensation-seeking and openness to experience, correlate with liberalism, echoing Montaigne's line that the only things he finds rewarding are variety and the enjoyment of diversity","John Jost's major review found that nearly all the traits distinguishing liberals and conservatives are conceptually related to threat sensitivity - conservatives react more strongly to reminders of death - or to openness to experience","Even though the effects of any single gene are tiny, the genes collectively give some people brains more reactive to threats and less pleased by novelty, and these are the two main personality factors that distinguish the sides","Women who called themselves liberals as adults had been rated by their nursery school teachers as more curious, verbal, and self-reliant, but also more assertive and aggressive, less obedient and neat","Genes contribute to just about every aspect of our personalities: how much you like jazz, spicy foods, and abstract art, your likelihood of divorce, your degree of religiosity, and your political orientation as an adult"]}]},{"id":"rm00","title":"Introduction","pages":"1-15","assets":[{"asset_type":"CUE_SCRUBBER_STATION","domain":"Moral Psychology | Intuition vs Reason | Political Division","topic":"Cue Scrubber: The Self-Righteousness Radar","visual_frame_description":"A living room turned diagnostic cockpit: every moral outburst in the conversation glows red, a radar sweep labels each flash as intuition or strategy, and a ticker over the couch scrolls the dictionary definitions of righteous and self-righteous.","player_mission":"Scan a stream of overheard arguments, news clips, and dinner-table spats for the signature flashes of the righteous mind: the instant moralistic snap, the outraged sense of justice, the certainty that your side's moral arguments are plain truth. Ring each moment of 'for' versus 'against' before the speaker can dress it up as objective reasoning, and flag which of the book's three principles the incident is obeying.","key_concepts":["Rodney King's appeal 'Can we all get along?' was made famous on May 1, 1992, after a jury failed to convict four Los Angeles police officers, triggering six days of rioting in which fifty-three people were killed and more than seven thousand buildings were torched","The word righteous comes from the old Norse rettviss and old English rihtwis, both meaning 'just, upright, virtuous,' and now carries religious connotations because it is usually used to translate the Hebrew word tzedek","Self-righteous means 'convinced of one's own righteousness, especially in contrast with the actions and beliefs of others; narrowly moralistic and intolerant'","Haidt chose the title The Righteous Mind over The Moral Mind to convey that human nature is not just intrinsically moral, it's also intrinsically moralistic, critical, and judgmental","An obsession with righteousness, leading inevitably to self-righteousness, is the normal human condition - a feature of our evolutionary design, not a bug that crept into minds that would otherwise be objective and rational","Righteous minds made it possible for human beings, but no other animals, to produce large cooperative groups, tribes, and nations without the glue of kinship","Our righteous minds guarantee that our cooperative groups will always be cursed by moralistic strife","Some degree of conflict among groups may even be necessary for the health and development of any society","One modern definition of righteous captures its linkage with judgmentalism: 'arising from an outraged sense of justice, morality, or fair play'","The book quotes Matthew: 'Why do you see the speck in your neighbor's eye, but do not notice the log in your own eye?'","The eighth-century Chinese Zen master Sen-ts'an wrote that the struggle between 'for' and 'against' is the mind's worst disease","Haidt says that a world without moralism, gossip, and judgment would quickly decay into chaos"],"difficulty_level":"Beginner"},{"asset_type":"DYNAMIC_DIALOGUE_SIM","domain":"Moral Psychology | Intuition vs Reason | Political Division","topic":"Dialogue: Stuck Here for a While","visual_frame_description":"A 1992 living-room broadcast set: on one screen Rodney King stumbles through his appeal with tears in his eyes while helicopter footage of burning Los Angeles rolls behind the anchor, and a caption asks how we are all stuck here together for a while.","player_mission":"Play both sides of King's television interview: the beaten man fighting back tears and often repeating himself as he asks why we can't get along, and the nation watching the nightly news from Washington as though it were sent from helicopters over a war zone. Then step into the polite-companion seat and run the conversation about politics and religion that etiquette forbids, trying to keep it civil, curious, and fun in mixed company.","key_concepts":["King followed his famous phrase with the rarely quoted plea: 'Please, we can get along here. We all can get along. I mean, we're all stuck here for a while. Let's try to work it out.'","Haidt hesitated to open the book with King's words because the appeal is so overused it has become cultural kitsch, more often said for laughs than as a serious plea for mutual understanding","Most Americans nowadays ask King's question not about race relations but about political relations and the collapse of cooperation across party lines","Many Americans feel as though the nightly news from Washington is being sent to us from helicopters circling over the city, delivering dispatches from the war zone","Etiquette books tell us not to discuss politics and religion in polite company, but Haidt says go ahead, because both are expressions of our underlying moral psychology","An understanding of moral psychology can help to bring people together","Haidt's goal is to drain some of the heat, anger, and divisiveness out of politics and religion and replace them with awe, wonder, and curiosity","Haidt hopes his book will make conversations about morality, politics, and religion more common, more civil, and more fun, even in mixed company","In the United States, liberal refers to progressive or left-wing politics, while in Europe liberal is truer to its original meaning - valuing liberty above all else, including in economic activities - more like the American libertarian","Enlightenment, or wisdom, requires us all to take the logs out of our own eyes and then escape from our ceaseless, petty, and divisive moralism","Sen-ts'an wrote: 'The Perfect Way is only difficult for those who pick and choose; Do not like, do not dislike; all will then be clear.'","People who devote their lives to studying something often come to believe that the object of their fascination is the key to understanding everything - this is one of those books, and its key is moral psychology"],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate"},{"asset_type":"DECEPTION_AUDIT_FILE","domain":"Moral Psychology | Intuition vs Reason | Political Division","topic":"Audit: The Three-Principles File Room","visual_frame_description":"A vast archive where three principle-seals glow over three aisles - Rider and Elephant, Six Taste Receptors, Chimp and Bee - and filing clerks race to stamp each incoming claim with the right seal while a Darwin quote about cooperative groups hangs above the door.","player_mission":"Receive a flood of claims about human nature and file each one under the principle it illustrates: intuitions come first and strategic reasoning second; there is more to morality than harm and fairness; or morality binds and blinds. For each entry record the metaphor that belongs in the drawer - the rider on the elephant, the tongue with six taste receptors, or the 90-percent-chimp and 10-percent-bee split - before the file room closes.","key_concepts":["Part I's principle: intuitions come first, strategic reasoning second - moral intuitions arise automatically and almost instantaneously, long before moral reasoning has a chance to get started","Moral arguments people offer are mostly post hoc constructions made up on the fly, crafted to advance one or more strategic objectives","The central metaphor of Part I: the mind is divided, like a rider on an elephant, and the rider's job is to serve the elephant","The rider is our conscious reasoning - the stream of words and images of which we are fully aware - and the elephant is the other 99 percent of mental processes that actually govern most of our behavior","Haidt developed the rider-and-elephant metaphor in his earlier book, The Happiness Hypothesis","Part II's metaphor: the righteous mind is like a tongue with six taste receptors, and secular Western moralities are like cuisines that try to activate just one or two of them","Besides harm and fairness, people have powerful moral intuitions related to liberty, loyalty, authority, and sanctity","Haidt argues that politicians on the right have a built-in advantage when it comes to cooking meals that voters like","Part III's principle: morality binds and blinds, with human beings as 90 percent chimp and 10 percent bee","Darwin said long ago that the most cohesive and cooperative groups generally beat the groups of selfish individualists","Darwin's ideas about group selection fell out of favor in the 1960s, but recent discoveries are putting his ideas back into play","Our bee-like nature facilitates altruism, heroism, war, and genocide"],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate"},{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Moral Psychology | Intuition vs Reason | Political Division","topic":"Discrimination Matrix: Rider or Elephant","visual_frame_description":"A two-lane sorting chute: one lane labelled Elephant, 99 Percent of Mental Processes, the other Rider, Conscious Reasoning; each incoming mental event slides into its lane while a counter shows how rarely the rider actually steers.","player_mission":"Sort a parade of mental events onto the beast that produces them: the automatic flash of liking or judgment onto the elephant, the stream of words and images you are fully aware of onto the rider. Then judge the controversial cases - the moral arguments that feel like truth but advance a team's agenda - before the sorter stamps each event with the book's first principle.","key_concepts":["The rider is our conscious reasoning - the stream of words and images of which we are fully aware","The elephant is the other 99 percent of mental processes - the ones that occur outside of awareness but that actually govern most of our behavior","If you think moral reasoning is something we do to figure out the truth, you'll be constantly frustrated by how foolish, biased, and illogical people become when they disagree with you","If you think of moral reasoning as a skill we humans evolved to further our social agendas - to justify our own actions and defend the teams we belong to - then things will make a lot more sense","Haidt advises keeping your eye on the intuitions and not taking people's moral arguments at face value","The rider-and-elephant metaphor is used to solve puzzles such as why it seems like everyone else is a hypocrite and why political partisans are so willing to believe outrageous lies and conspiracy theories","Individuals compete with individuals within every group, and we are the descendants of primates who excelled at that competition - this gives us the ugly side of our nature","We are selfish hypocrites so skilled at putting on a show of virtue that we fool even ourselves","Under special circumstances we have the ability to shut down our petty selves and become like cells in a larger body, or like bees in a hive, working for the good of the group","Hivish experiences are often among the most cherished of our lives, although our hivishness can blind us to other moral concerns","People bind themselves into political teams that share moral narratives, and once they accept a particular narrative, they become blind to alternative moral worlds","Haidt will use this perspective to explain why some people are conservative, others are liberal or progressive, and still others become libertarians"],"difficulty_level":"Advanced"},{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Moral Psychology | Intuition vs Reason | Political Division","topic":"Boss Battle: The Log in Your Own Eye","visual_frame_description":"A mirror hall built from Matthew's proverb: every wall shows a neighbor's speck rendered in gold, while in the center a single log floats in your own reflected eye, and a ledger over the door asks which defects our design lets us see.","player_mission":"Face the final exam of the righteous mind: a gallery of other people's moral failures, each speck magnified, while your own log waits unnoticed in your own eye. Sort the gallery into moments when moralism is protecting a real community and moments when it is only defending your team, then draft the closing case: drop the moralism, apply some moral psychology, and analyze the game we are all playing.","key_concepts":["Haidt quotes the Bible: 'Why do you see the speck in your neighbor's eye, but do not notice the log in your own eye? ... You hypocrite, first take the log out of your own eye, and then you will see clearly to take the speck out of your neighbor's eye.'","The take-home message of the book is the realization that we are all self-righteous hypocrites","Sen-ts'an wrote: 'If you want the truth to stand clear before you, never be for or against'","The struggle between 'for' and 'against' plays out in each of our righteous minds, and among all of our righteous groups","Haidt's book takes the reader on a tour of human nature and history from the perspective of moral psychology, giving a new way to think about politics and religion","Haidt says we are downright lucky that we evolved the complex moral psychology that allowed our species to burst out of the forests and savannas and into modern societies in just a few thousand years","The link between righteousness and judgmentalism appears in terms such as self-righteous, which means 'convinced of one's own righteousness, especially in contrast with the actions and beliefs of others'","Righteous minds made civilization possible, but they guarantee that our cooperative groups will always be cursed by moralistic strife","Haidt wants to show that an obsession with righteousness is a feature of our evolutionary design, not an error that crept into otherwise objective and rational minds","If we want to understand ourselves, our divisions, our limits, and our potentials, we need to step back, drop the moralism, apply some moral psychology, and analyze the game we're all playing","Haidt does not recommend living like Sen-ts'an: a world without moralism, gossip, and judgment would quickly decay into chaos","The book promises to replace heat, anger, and divisiveness with awe, wonder, and curiosity about our moral psychology"],"difficulty_level":"Advanced"}]},{"id":"rm01","title":"Chapter 1: Where Does Morality Come From?","pages":"3-26","assets":[{"asset_type":"CUE_SCRUBBER_STATION","domain":"Moral Psychology | Intuition vs Reason | Political Division","topic":"Cue Scrubber: The Victim Factory","visual_frame_description":"An interrogation gallery of small vignettes frozen mid-story: each respondent's first flash of disgust glows red before the words appear, and a victim-fabrication meter spools upward whenever anyone claims harm the text never mentions.","player_mission":"Scan harmless-taboo stories as they flash past - the family that cooks its dead dog, the man and the chicken, the flag shredder, the widow eating fish - and catch each respondent's tell: the instant flash of disgust, the halfhearted invented victim, the apologetic 'maybe the woman will feel guilty afterward,' the preposterous toilet-clogging rags. Stamp the exact moment the moral judgment arrives, then watch the reasoning scramble to catch up.","key_concepts":["In the dog story, a family's dog is killed by a car in front of their house, and they cook and eat it, with nobody seeing them; most well-educated people felt an initial flash of disgust but hesitated before calling it morally wrong","In the chicken story, a man buys a chicken at the supermarket each week, has sexual intercourse with it before cooking, then eats it - a kind of recycling that some research subjects called an efficient use of natural resources","Educated liberal Westerners give nuanced answers acknowledging the man's right to do what he wants as long as he hurts no one, while most people on the planet think sex with a chicken carcass is morally wrong","Haidt called his invented stories 'harmless taboo violations' and found the ones that worked best fell into two categories: disgust and disrespect","One of his disrespect stories: a woman cleaning out her closet finds her old American flag, cuts it up into pieces, and uses the rags to clean her bathroom","In 38 percent of the 1,620 times that people heard a harmless-offensive story, they claimed that somebody was harmed, even though the stories were written to remove all conceivable harm","Subjects usually offered their invented victims up halfheartedly and almost apologetically, as in 'Well, I don't know, maybe the woman will feel guilty afterward about throwing out her flag?'","A child justified condemning the flag shredder by claiming the rags might clog up the toilet and cause it to overflow","Haidt trained his interviewers to correct people gently when they made claims that contradicted the text of the story, such as by pointing out that nobody saw the woman","When their invented victims were challenged, many subjects said things like 'I know it's wrong, but I just can't think of a reason why' - they were morally dumbfounded","The dumbfounded subjects were reasoning and working hard, but it was not reasoning in search of truth; it was reasoning in support of their emotional reactions","Haidt published these findings in one of the top psychology journals in October 1993, and almost nobody cited the work, even to attack it, in the first five years"],"difficulty_level":"Beginner"},{"asset_type":"DYNAMIC_DIALOGUE_SIM","domain":"Moral Psychology | Intuition vs Reason | Political Division","topic":"Dialogue: The Urinal Interview","visual_frame_description":"A McDonald's restroom rendered as a classroom: a toddler stands at the urinal with his hand raised, the father sighs, and above the sinks a chalkboard traces each escalating transgression while a gauge labeled Cosmic Justice holds steady at everyone-in-trouble.","player_mission":"Play the four-year-old Kohlberg interviewer in the McDonald's bathroom, probing your father with escalating hypotheticals - the urinal, the sink, the home sink - until the whole family poops in the sink at home and cosmic justice still demands that 'we'd all get in trouble.' Then switch seats and answer as the dad: is the moral being taught through reasons, or through the emotions of disgust and fear?","key_concepts":["Haidt once overheard a Kohlberg-style moral judgment interview in the bathroom of a McDonald's in northern Indiana, where the interviewer was a Caucasian male approximately four years old and the subject his roughly thirty-year-old father","The interview began at adjacent urinals with the question 'Dad, what would happen if I pooped in here [the urinal]?' and moved to the sink, then the sink at home","The final probe, 'Well, what would happen if we all pooped in the sink at home?', was answered by the father: 'I guess we'd all get in trouble.'","Even when everyone cooperates in the rule violation so that nobody can play the role of punisher, the father still clings to a notion of cosmic justice in which the whole family would get in trouble","Haidt's point: the father was not demonstrating his best moral reasoning but trying to get his curious son to feel the right emotions - disgust and fear - to motivate appropriate bathroom behavior","Kohlberg created a set of moral dilemmas, such as whether a man named Heinz should break into a drugstore to steal a drug that would save his dying wife","Kohlberg also asked whether a girl named Louise should reveal to her mother that her younger sister had lied","It didn't much matter whether the child said yes or no to a dilemma; what mattered were the reasons children gave when they tried to explain their answers","Kohlberg found a six-stage progression in children's reasoning about the social world that matched the stages Piaget had found in reasoning about the physical world","At the conventional stages kids enter the age of petty legalism, well remembered by those with siblings: 'I'm not hitting you. I'm using your hand to hit you. Stop hitting yourself!'","In the two post-conventional stages, adolescents sometimes justify dishonesty or law-breaking in pursuit of still higher goods, particularly justice","Kohlberg painted an inspiring rationalist image of children as 'moral philosophers' trying to work out coherent ethical systems for themselves"],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate"},{"asset_type":"DECEPTION_AUDIT_FILE","domain":"Moral Psychology | Intuition vs Reason | Political Division","topic":"Audit: The Harmless-Taboo Field Log","visual_frame_description":"A three-column ledger stamped Philadelphia, Porto Alegre, Recife, with class and age tabs; each judgment card snaps into its column while a Harm Perceived gauge shows the cultural differences widening, not shrinking, after filtering.","player_mission":"File every judgment from Haidt's three-city study under the correct column - moral violation, social convention, or the contested ground in between - and record which city, social class, and age group made each call. Balance the ledger against the probe question 'Do you think anyone was harmed?' and note whether the cultural differences shrink or grow once the victim-seers are filtered out.","key_concepts":["Haidt's study design was 'three by two by two': three cities, two levels of social class, and two age groups (children ten to twelve, adults eighteen to twenty-eight), with thirty people in each of twelve groups, for a total of 360 interviews","Haidt predicted that Philadelphia would be the most individualistic and therefore the most Turiel-like city, and Recife, in Brazil's northeast, the most sociocentric","All four of Haidt's Philadelphia groups confirmed Turiel's finding that Americans distinguish moral violations like pushing a boy off a swing from conventional violations like refusing to wear a school uniform","The upper-class Brazilians looked just like the Americans on those stories, but the working-class Brazilian kids usually thought it was wrong, and universally wrong, to break the social convention and not wear the uniform","There were separate significant effects of city, social class, and age, and unexpectedly the effect of social class was much larger than the effect of city","Well-educated people in all three cities were more similar to each other than they were to their lower-class neighbors - there was more moral variation a few blocks west of Haidt's campus than five thousand miles south","When subjects who said someone was harmed were filtered out, the cross-cultural differences got bigger, not smaller","Turiel found that children as young as five recognize that rules preventing harm are moral rules, which he defined as rules related to 'justice, rights, and welfare pertaining to how people ought to relate to each other'","Shweder and two collaborators interviewed 180 children aged five to thirteen and 60 adults in Hyde Park, Chicago, plus Brahmin children and adults in Bhubaneswar and 120 people from low 'untouchable' castes - six hundred long interviews","Shweder used thirty-nine very short stories in which someone violates a rule; Indian subjects, even five-year-olds, said food, clothing, and gender practices were wrong, universally wrong, and unalterably wrong","Shweder found almost no trace of social-conventional thinking in Orissa, where, as he put it, 'the social order is a moral order'","Even in Chicago, Americans treated restrictions on a widow eating fish as morally wrong for anyone to impose - the social order is a moral order there too, but an individualistic one built around protecting individuals and their freedom"],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate"},{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Moral Psychology | Intuition vs Reason | Political Division","topic":"Discrimination Matrix: Moral or Conventional","visual_frame_description":"A library of rules with two great shelves - Moral and Conventional - and a widening third shelf labelled The Thicker Moral Order, where the Hua's food rules, purity laws, and a widow's fish dinner land while a card flips over to explain the informational assumptions behind each call.","player_mission":"Route each case to the right shelf - moral rules built on harm and fairness, social conventions that are arbitrary and changeable, or the contested zone where the moral domain broadens. Sort Turiel's swing-pusher and uniform-refuser, Shweder's thirty-nine stories, the Hua's food taboos, and Leviticus' swarming things, then face the court of informational assumptions: is the widow's fish a 'hot' food or just dinner?","key_concepts":["Turiel discovered that children as young as five usually say a boy was wrong to break a school-uniform rule, but that it would be OK if the teacher gave permission or in another school with no such rule - social conventions are arbitrary and changeable to some extent","Nearly all kids say a girl who pushes a boy off a swing would be wrong even if the teacher said it was OK and even in another school with no rules about pushing - rules that prevent harm are moral, unalterable, and universal","Haidt validated his methods using Turiel's own two stories: a girl pushing a boy off a swing as a clear moral violation, and a boy refusing to wear a school uniform as a conventional violation","The Hua of New Guinea have developed elaborate food rules by which they judge each other and govern their lives, which anthropologist Anna Meigs called 'a religion of the body'","The Hebrew Bible is taken up with rules about food, menstruation, sex, skin, and the handling of corpses, including prohibitions on eating or touching 'the swarming things that swarm upon the earth'","Some biblical rules clearly avoid disease, such as the long sections of Leviticus on leprosy, while others follow a more emotional logic of avoiding disgust","Other biblical rules keep categories pure or avoid mixing, such as bans on clothing made from two different fibers","Turiel's rebuttal essay argued that many of Shweder's thirty-nine stories were trick questions: Hindus in Orissa believe fish is a 'hot' food that stimulates sexual appetite, so a widow who eats fish might have sex, offend her dead husband's spirit, and prevent her reincarnation at a higher level","Turiel argued that once Indian 'informational assumptions' are understood, most of the thirty-nine stories really were moral violations with victims Americans could not see","Haidt agreed with Turiel's defense on one point: Shweder's study was missing the experimental control of asking subjects whether anyone was harmed","When Haidt filtered out subjects who perceived harm, the cultural differences got bigger, not smaller - strong support for Shweder's claim that the moral domain goes far beyond harm","In Haidt's conclusion, the moral domain varies by culture, and morality cannot be entirely self-constructed by children from their growing understanding of harm"],"difficulty_level":"Advanced"},{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Moral Psychology | Intuition vs Reason | Political Division","topic":"Boss Battle: The Rationalist's Last Stand","visual_frame_description":"A university lecture hall at war: on one side a chalkboard of water glasses and marble games, on the other a shadow-wall of witch doctors, headhunters, and Bhubaneswar temples; a gavel hovers over the podium where the verdict 'The moral domain varies by culture' is about to be read.","player_mission":"Defend the rationalist paradigm in the great debate: argue as Piaget, Kohlberg, and Turiel that children self-construct morality from experiences of harm, then switch sides and let Shweder, the Azande, the Ilongot, and your own 360 interviews tear it down. Survive the moment the control for harm fails, and read the final verdict: the moral domain varied across nations and social classes, and Shweder won the debate.","key_concepts":["Piaget, the greatest developmental psychologist of all time, began his career as a zoologist studying mollusks and insects in his native Switzerland","Piaget poured water into two identical glasses, then one tall skinny glass, and found children under six or seven say the tall skinny glass holds more because the level is higher - they don't grasp conservation of volume, and adult explanations are pointless until their minds are ready","Piaget applied his cognitive-developmental approach to morality by playing marbles with children, sometimes deliberately breaking rules and playing dumb to reveal their growing respect for rules and turn taking","Piaget argued that children's moral understanding, like their understanding of water glasses, is neither innate nor learned from adults but self-constructed as kids play with other kids","In this book, rationalist describes anyone who believes that reasoning is the most important and reliable way to obtain moral knowledge","Kohlberg's most influential finding was that the most morally advanced kids were those with frequent opportunities for role taking, which come from egalitarian peer relationships rather than hierarchical ones like those with teachers and parents","Piaget and Kohlberg both thought parents and other authorities were obstacles to moral development, and that forcing obedience would freeze children at the conventional level","For twenty-five years, from the 1970s through the 1990s, moral psychologists mostly interviewed young people about dilemmas, using a framework that predefined morality as justice while denigrating authority, hierarchy, and tradition","In 1987 Haidt was admitted to the graduate program in psychology at the University of Pennsylvania, where Jonathan Baron asked him point-blank: 'Is moral thinking any different from other kinds of thinking?'","In Alan Fiske's cultural psychology course Haidt read that the Azande believed witches were as likely to be men as women, and the fear of being called a witch made them careful not to make their neighbors angry or envious","Among the Ilongot of the Philippines, young men gained honor by cutting off people's heads, and some puzzling killings of strangers channeled in-group resentments into a group-strengthening 'hunting party' capped by a long night of communal celebratory singing","Haidt's conclusion: 'We're born to be righteous, but we have to learn what, exactly, people like us should be righteous about'"],"difficulty_level":"Advanced"}]},{"id":"rm02","title":"Chapter 2: The Intuitive Dog and Its Rational Tail","pages":"27-52","assets":[{"asset_type":"CUE_SCRUBBER_STATION","domain":"Moral Psychology | Intuition vs Reason | Political Division","topic":"Cue Scrubber: The Dumbfounding Transcript Scanner","visual_frame_description":"A transcription theater where each utterance is tagged in real time - judgment flash in red, reason offered in blue, reason discarded in grey - and a Humean verdict lamp glows when the subject ends exactly where they began.","player_mission":"Scan interview transcripts for the signature of the social intuitionist model: the instant 'Yeah, I think it's wrong,' the flailing reasons, the discarded arguments, the stammering 'I don't know, what did you ask me?' Ring every moment when the servant comes back empty-handed and the master does not change his mind, and separate the Heinz-style reasoning cases from the gut-driven taboo cases.","key_concepts":["Scott Murphy brought thirty UVA students into the lab, one at a time, for an extended interview, explaining that his job was to challenge their reasoning no matter what they said","In the Heinz dilemma, Scott couldn't whip up any dumbfounding, because people offered good reasons and would not abandon principles such as 'Life is more important than property'","In the 'roach juice' scenario, Scott opened a can of apple juice, then dipped a sterilized cockroach into the cup with a tea strainer and asked the subject to take a sip; 37 percent were willing","In the soul-selling scenario, Scott offered subjects $2 to sign a paper selling their soul to him after death, with a note that the form was not a legal or binding contract; only 23 percent signed without any goading","Scott convinced an extra 10 percent to sip the juice and an extra 17 percent to sign the soul-selling paper, but most people in both scenarios clung to their initial refusal","Many of the subjects who refused to sell their souls were atheists who didn't believe in souls and yet still felt uncomfortable about signing","In the Julie-and-Mark story, a brother and sister on summer vacation in France decide to make love, using birth control pills and a condom; only 20 percent of subjects said it was OK","In the Jennifer story, a vegetarian hospital worker who incinerates a fresh human cadaver cuts off a piece of flesh, cooks it, and eats it; only 13 percent said it was OK","In the harmless-taboo scenarios people generated far more reasons and discarded far more reasons than in any of the other scenarios, flailing around as Scott proved each reason irrelevant","One dumbfounded incest-story subject said: 'There's just no way I could change my mind but I just don't know how to - how to show what I'm feeling, what I feel about it. It's crazy!'","The quantified analyses showed big differences between responses to the harmless-taboo scenarios and responses to the Heinz dilemma","The results supported Hume, not Jefferson or Plato: people made moral judgments quickly and emotionally, and moral reasoning was mostly a post hoc search for reasons to justify judgments already made"],"difficulty_level":"Beginner"},{"asset_type":"DYNAMIC_DIALOGUE_SIM","domain":"Moral Psychology | Intuition vs Reason | Political Division","topic":"Dialogue: The Head-Heart Correspondence","visual_frame_description":"A candlelit Paris study: the letter unfurls on the desk and the quill splits into two speakers - a stern clerk in a powdered wig arguing with a flushed romantic voice - while the Seine glitters beyond the window and a broken heart waits in England.","player_mission":"Play Jefferson in 1786 Paris, composing the love letter to Maria Cosway as a dialogue between his head and his heart: let the head lecture with its balance-in-hand calculation, then let the heart answer that science was allotted to the head and morals to the heart. Then switch seats and decide, as Jefferson's own judgment, whether sentiment or calculation should lay the foundation of morals.","key_concepts":["In 1786, while serving as the American minister to France, Jefferson fell in love with Maria Cosway, a beautiful twenty-seven-year-old English artist","Jefferson and Cosway spent hours strolling around Paris on a perfect sunny day, and Jefferson sent messengers bearing lies to cancel his evening meetings so he could extend the day into night","Cosway's husband soon insisted on taking her back to England, leaving Jefferson in pain, and Jefferson eased that pain by writing a love letter framed as a dialogue between his head and his heart","Jefferson's head is the Platonic ideal of reason, scolding the heart: 'Everything in this world is a matter of calculation. Advance then with caution, the balance in your hand.'","The heart replies that nature assigned a divided empire: 'To you she allotted the field of science; to me that of morals'","The heart argues that reason lacks the feelings of sympathy, benevolence, gratitude, justice, love, and friendship, and that 'Morals were too essential to the happiness of man to be risked on the incertain combinations of the head'","The heart concludes: 'She laid their foundation therefore in sentiment, not in science'","The Roman poet Ovid had a character lament: 'I am dragged along by a strange new force. Desire and reason are pulling in different directions. I see the right way and approve it, but follow the wrong.'","In Plato's Timaeus, lesser deities encased the rational souls in spheres, which explains why our heads are more or less round, and animated each body with a second, mortal soul containing pleasure, pains, boldness, fear, anger, and expectation","Timaeus adds that a man who masters his emotions will be reborn into a celestial heaven of eternal happiness, while a man mastered by his passions will be reincarnated as a woman","The worshipful attitude toward reason running from Plato through Kant to Lawrence Kohlberg is called the rationalist delusion","Jefferson's model treats reason and sentiment as independent co-rulers, like the emperors of Rome who divided the empire into eastern and western halves"],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate"},{"asset_type":"DECEPTION_AUDIT_FILE","domain":"Moral Psychology | Intuition vs Reason | Political Division","topic":"Audit: The Judgment and Justification Ledger","visual_frame_description":"A laboratory ledger with two columns - Seeing-That and Reasoning-Why; cards for the Muller-Lyer lines, the four cards E K 4 7, and 2-4-6 triplets slide into their slots while a rubber stamp descends with Wason's conclusion.","player_mission":"Audit a week of cognitive experiments and file each finding under seeing-that or reasoning-why: the Muller-Lyer illusion, the Wason 4-card task, the triplet hunts, the cognitive-load tests. For every entry, record whether the judgment was produced by rapid unconscious pattern matching and whether the justification survived the test - then stamp the ledger 'Judgment and justification are separate processes.'","key_concepts":["In the Wason 4-card task, each card has a letter on one side and a number on the other, and the subject must choose the smallest number of cards to turn over to test the rule 'If there is a vowel on one side, then there is an even number on the other side'","Everyone immediately sees that you must turn over the E, but many people also turn over the 4; the correct answer is E and 7, because turning over the 4 and finding a B would not invalidate the rule, while turning over the 7 and finding a U would","When told the answer up front, people can explain it, but they are just as able to offer an explanation and just as confident whether told the right answer (E and 7) or the popular wrong answer (E and 4)","Findings such as these led Wason to the conclusion that judgment and justification are separate processes","Howard Margolis summarized: given the judgments produced by the nonconscious cognitive machinery in the brain, human beings produce rationales they believe account for their judgments, but the rationales are only ex post rationalizations","Margolis proposed two kinds of cognitive process: 'seeing-that,' the pattern matching brains have been doing for hundreds of millions of years, and 'reasoning-why'","In the Muller-Lyer illusion, one line continues to look longer than the other even after you know the two lines are the same length - you can't choose whether or not to see it","Reasoning-why is the process by which we describe how we think we reached a judgment; it occurs only in creatures with language, is not automatic, feels like work, and is easily disrupted by cognitive load","Kohlberg had convinced moral psychologists to study reasoning-why and to neglect seeing-that","The intuition launched the reasoning, but the intuition did not depend on the success of the reasoning - subjects kept their judgments even when their reasons collapsed","Moral judgments are claims that somebody did something wrong, so 'Because I don't want to' won't do; we reason to find the best possible reasons why somebody else ought to join us in our judgment","We do moral reasoning not to reconstruct the actual reasons why we ourselves came to a judgment, but to find the best possible reasons why somebody else ought to join us in it"],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate"},{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Moral Psychology | Intuition vs Reason | Political Division","topic":"Discrimination Matrix: Plato, Hume, or Jefferson","visual_frame_description":"A three-throned hall: the philosopher-king's throne with the neck as its invention, Hume's workshop where reason waits on the passions, and Jefferson's divided empire of east and west, while litigants - brain-damaged patients, stammering interviewees - file past to have their verdicts handed down.","player_mission":"Drag each thinker, each experiment, and each claim onto the correct throne - reason as master, reason as servant, or the two as co-emperors of a divided empire. Then test the sorting itself: Damasio's patients, the dumbfounded subjects, the cognitive-load studies, and the ten-second delay - which model does each one dethrone, and which one survives?","key_concepts":["Plato believed that reason could and should be the master of the passions, even if philosophers are the only ones who can reach a high level of mastery","Hume wrote that reason is, and ought only to be, the servant of the passions","Jefferson offered a third option in which reason and sentiment are independent co-rulers over a divided empire","Darwin was fascinated by morality and thought sympathy was the 'foundation-stone' of the social instincts, also writing about shame and pride associated with the desire for a good reputation","Darwin was a nativist about morality: natural selection gave us minds preloaded with moral emotions","Two waves of moralism turned nativism into a moral offense: the horror at 'social Darwinism,' and the radical politics of the 1960s and 1970s that wanted a blank slate on which any utopian vision could be sketched","Steven Pinker's The Blank Slate describes scientists becoming 'moral exhibitionists' who urged students to evaluate ideas for consistency with the progressive movement rather than for their truth","E.O. Wilson's 1975 book Sociobiology suggested in its final chapter that natural selection also influenced human behavior","Wilson charged that what moral philosophers were really doing was fabricating justifications after 'consulting the emotive centers' of their own brains, and he predicted ethics would soon be 'biologicized'","Wilson's dreamed-of 'new synthesis' of philosophy, biology, and evolution he later referred to as consilience - the 'jumping together' of ideas to create a unified body of knowledge","Damasio's patients with damage to the ventromedial prefrontal cortex retained full knowledge of right and wrong, showed no IQ deficits, and scored well on Kohlberg's tests, yet made foolish decisions and their lives fell apart","Choosing with no emotional input becomes like picking the best washing machine among ten options minute after minute - the options quickly exceed the six-or-seven-item capacity of our short-term memory"],"difficulty_level":"Advanced"},{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Moral Psychology | Intuition vs Reason | Political Division","topic":"Boss Battle: The Devil's Advocate Gauntlet","visual_frame_description":"A stark interview room where a single lamp lights a subject and a challenger with a clipboard; on the table sit a cup of apple juice, a white box with a sterilized cockroach, a $2 soul contract, and a stack of transcripts that refuse to move toward change.","player_mission":"Run Scott Murphy's interview as the relentless challenger: take thirty subjects through the Heinz dilemma, the roach juice, the soul-selling paper, Julie and Mark, and Jennifer's cannibalism, stripping away every reason they offer. The boss shows its hand when you try to whip up dumbfounding on Heinz and fail - then again when atheists refuse to sell their souls - and your score depends on how many subjects change their minds, which should be almost none, and how many reasons you demolish, which should be almost all.","key_concepts":["The social intuitionist model's four main links: intuitive judgment, post hoc reasoning, reasoned persuasion, and social persuasion, with two rarely used links of reasoned judgment and private reflection","Haidt published the model in 2001 in an article titled 'The Emotional Dog and Its Rational Tail,' later wishing he'd called the dog 'intuitive' instead","Intuitions, including emotional responses, are a kind of cognition - they're just not a kind of reasoning","Contrasting emotion with cognition is as pointless as contrasting rain with weather, or cars with vehicles","If someone runs up behind you on a dark street, your fear system detects the threat and triggers your sympathetic nervous system, firing up the fight-or-flight response, cranking up your heart rate, and widening your pupils","Asked whether it is better to save the lives of five strangers or one, you just see, instantly, that five is better than one - no emotion or reasoning process is needed","The rider can see further into the future, learn new skills, and master new technologies, but most importantly it acts as the spokesman for the elephant, fabricating post hoc explanations for whatever the elephant has just done","Once human beings developed language and began to use it to gossip about each other, it became extremely valuable for elephants to carry around a full-time public relations firm on their backs","Link 6, private reflection, is dotted in the model because most of us change our minds on moral issues only with prompting from other people","Other people influence us constantly just by revealing that they like or dislike somebody - that is link 4, the social persuasion link","You can't make a dog happy by forcibly wagging its tail, and you can't change people's minds by utterly refuting their arguments","Hume: 'it is in vain to expect, that any logic, which speaks not to the affections, will ever engage him to embrace sounder principles'"],"difficulty_level":"Advanced"}]},{"id":"rm03","title":"Chapter 3: Elephants Rule","pages":"53-72","assets":[{"asset_type":"CUE_SCRUBBER_STATION","domain":"Moral Psychology | Intuition vs Reason | Political Division","topic":"Cue Scrubber: The Affect Flash Scanner","visual_frame_description":"A neural cockpit where every perceived word, face, and slogan trails a comet of affect; gauges show the flash arriving at 200 milliseconds and the lean persisting for about a second, while a slow-motion belt carries the Muller-Lyer illusion to prove you can't unsee what you see.","player_mission":"Scan a stream of words, faces, and policy slogans for the tell of affective primacy: the flash of lean that lands within a quarter second and steers the verdict before thought arrives. Catch the hypnotic code words 'take' and 'often' sneaking into stories, catch the partisan's slow lane on pro-life or Clinton, and catch the tenth-of-a-second competence judgment before it decides an election.","key_concepts":["Thalia Wheatley hypnotized UVA students to feel a flash of disgust whenever they saw a certain word - take for half the subjects, often for the others - and instructed them while in a trance that they would not remember anything she had told them","Subjects judged six moral-violation stories as more disgusting and morally wrong when their code word was embedded, as when a congressman 'takes bribes from the tobacco lobby'","The seventh story had no moral violation: student council president Dan scheduling discussions between students and faculty, with 'tries to take topics that appeal to both professors and students' versus 'often picks topics'","Many subjects who felt the artificial flash of disgust still condemned Dan, making up absurd reasons like 'I don't know, it just seems like he's up to something'","Wilhelm Wundt, the founder of experimental psychology, formulated the doctrine of 'affective primacy' in the 1890s, holding that we find ourselves liking or disliking something the instant we notice it, sometimes even before we know what it is","Robert Zajonc's 1980 research had people rate Japanese pictograms, words in a made-up language, and geometric shapes, and showed that merely showing a word or image several times makes people like it more - the 'mere exposure effect'","Zajonc said affect has primacy both because it happens first and because it is more powerful, and that affective reactions act like blinders that 'reduce the universe of alternatives' available to later thinking","In affective priming, after the first word hate is flashed, the positive word sunshine takes about 250 milliseconds longer to categorize than cancer, because you have to undo the lean toward negativity","The Implicit Association Test, developed by Tony Greenwald, Mahzarin Banaji, and Brian Nosek, shows that most people turn out to have negative implicit associations with groups such as black people, immigrants, obese people, and the elderly","Jamie Morris measured the brain waves of liberals and conservatives reading words like Clinton, Bush, flag, taxes, welfare, and pro-life: pro-life plus sunshine was affectively incongruous for liberals, just as Clinton plus sunshine was for conservatives","Alexander Todorov found that the candidate judged more competent from photographs won U.S. Senate and House races about two-thirds of the time","When Todorov forced people to judge competence after flashing the pair of pictures for just a tenth of a second, the snap judgments predicted the real outcomes just as well"],"difficulty_level":"Beginner"},{"asset_type":"DYNAMIC_DIALOGUE_SIM","domain":"Moral Psychology | Intuition vs Reason | Political Division","topic":"Dialogue: The Dishes Confession","visual_frame_description":"A sunlit study on a February morning: a desk with a review article about moral psychology, a counter of baby bottles and breakfast dishes, and a courtroom bubble in which an inner lawyer stitches together Max's bottle and Andy's walk into an alibi while a wife stands waiting in the doorway.","player_mission":"Play Haidt at his desk on February 3, 2007, the morning his wife Jayne asks him - politely, but with the postscript 'As I have asked you a hundred times before' - not to leave dirty dishes on the counter where she prepares their baby's food. Feel the flash of negativity at her third word, watch your inner lawyer assemble the baby-and-dog alibi in three seconds, and then switch seats and interrogate yourself: was the brief true, or a historical fiction built after the verdict?","key_concepts":["On February 3, 2007, shortly before lunch, Haidt discovered that he was a chronic liar while at home writing a review article on moral psychology","Jayne asked Haidt not to leave dirty dishes on the counter where she prepared their baby's food, adding the postscript: 'As I have asked you a hundred times before'","Haidt's mouth started moving before hers had stopped, and the words linked themselves into a story about the baby waking up at the same time that their elderly dog barked to ask for a walk","In Haidt's family, caring for a hungry baby and an incontinent dog is a surefire excuse, so he was acquitted","Haidt realized he had felt a flash of negativity by the time Jayne had gotten to her third word - 'Can you not...' - before he even knew what the criticism was","Only when his wife criticized him did Haidt merge separate events - eating breakfast, giving Max his first bottle, letting Andy out for his first walk - into a composite image of a harried father, a fabrication complete by the time she finished her one-sentence criticism","Haidt had long teased his wife for altering stories to make them more dramatic, but it took twenty years of studying moral psychology to see that he altered his stories too","The Buddha: 'It is easy to see the faults of others, but difficult to see one's own faults. One shows the faults of others like chaff winnowed in the wind, but one conceals one's own faults as a cunning gambler conceals his dice.'","The chapter's six major research findings collectively illustrate the first half of the first principle: Intuitions Come First","Elephants rule, although they are sometimes open to persuasion by riders","The rider-and-elephant relationship is more like a lawyer serving a client than a slave serving a master - a dignified partnership","Good lawyers sometimes refuse to go along with requests, as when the elephant wants a third piece of cake and the rider refuses to find an excuse"],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate"},{"asset_type":"DECEPTION_AUDIT_FILE","domain":"Moral Psychology | Intuition vs Reason | Political Division","topic":"Audit: The Six-Finding Registry","visual_frame_description":"A six-drawer evidence registry; findings arrive as case files - the fart spray canister, the hand sanitizer, the puppet stage, the fMRI slices, the psychopath's confession tape - and each is slotted under its numbered drawer before the seal drops.","player_mission":"Audit a season of experiments and file each one under its finding: brains evaluate instantly, social and political judgments are intuitive, bodies guide judgments, psychopaths reason but don't feel, babies feel but don't reason, and affective reactions sit in the right place at the right time in the brain. Reject any filing that can't point to its experiment, and stamp the registry's seal: Intuitions Come First.","key_concepts":["Alex Jordan stood at a pedestrian intersection on the Stanford campus asking passersby to judge four controversial issues while secretly spraying fart spray into a trash can liner; people made harsher judgments when breathing in foul air","Other researchers found the same effect by asking subjects to fill out questionnaires after drinking bitter versus sweet drinks","Haidt's UVA colleague Jerry Clore puts it this way: we use 'affect as information'","Chenbo Zhong at the University of Toronto showed that subjects who wash their hands with soap before filling out questionnaires become more moralistic about issues related to moral purity, such as pornography and drug use","Zhong also showed the reverse: people who recall their own moral transgressions, or copy by hand an account of someone else's, think about cleanliness more and want to cleanse themselves - the Macbeth effect, named for Lady Macbeth's obsession with washing after King Duncan's murder","People who felt morally dirty were more likely to select hand wipes and other cleaning products when given a choice of consumer products to take home after the experiment","Eric Heizer and David Pizarro asked Cornell students to fill out surveys about their political attitudes while standing near or far from a hand sanitizer dispenser: those told to stand near the sanitizer became temporarily more conservative","Robert Hare defines psychopathy by two sets of features: impulsive antisocial behavior beginning in childhood, and the moral emotions psychopaths lack - no compassion, guilt, shame, or even embarrassment","One psychopath told Hare: 'Of course! I'm not a robot. I really get pumped up when I have sex or when I get into a fight' - yet psychopaths show no emotions that indicate they care about other people","Psychopaths seem to live in a world of objects, some of which happen to walk around on two legs","Ted Bundy was a psychology major in college who volunteered on a crisis hotline, where he learned how to speak to women and gain their trust before raping, mutilating, and murdering at least thirty young women","Psychopathy is a genetically heritable condition that creates brains unmoved by the needs, suffering, or dignity of others"],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate"},{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Moral Psychology | Intuition vs Reason | Political Division","topic":"Discrimination Matrix: Feelers or Reasoners","visual_frame_description":"A sorting yard of four clinical bays: in one a brain-damaged patient shops for washing machines, in another a psychopath laughs over a confession tape, in a third an infant stares at a toy car passing through a solid box, and in the last an infant reaches across a tray for the nice puppet.","player_mission":"Sort each profile onto the right track of moral machinery: the vmPFC patient who knows all the rules but feels nothing, the psychopath whose rider reasons perfectly while the elephant never leans, the two-month-old who stares at impossible physics, the six-month-old who reaches for the helper puppet. For every profile, answer one question - what is missing, and what does the rider do about it?","key_concepts":["William James described the world babies enter as 'one great blooming, buzzing confusion'","Infants as young as two months old look longer at an event that surprises them than at an event they were expecting - the trick that lets psychologists see what the infant mind expects","Infants expect objects to move according to Newton's laws of motion and get startled when psychologists show scenes that should be physically impossible, such as a toy car seeming to pass through a solid object","In the puppet experiments, six- and ten-month-old infants watched a 'climber' (a wooden shape with eyes glued to it) struggle up a hill while a helper puppet pushed from below and a hinderer puppet bashed it down","When the climber later cozied up to the hinderer, the infants stared longer than when it cozied up to the helper - the social equivalent of seeing a car pass through a solid box","At the end of the experiment, the infants were much more likely to reach out for the helper puppet than the hinderer","The researchers concluded that 'the capacity to evaluate individuals on the basis of their social interactions is universal and unlearned'","By six months of age, infants are watching how people behave toward other people and developing a preference for those who are nice rather than those who are mean","The elephant begins making something like moral judgments during infancy, long before language and reasoning arrive","When moral reasoning is not accompanied by moral intuition, the results are ugly - as seen in psychopaths","Moral intuitions emerge very early and are necessary for moral development, while the ability to reason emerges much later","Damasio's brain-damaged patients showed that the emotional areas of the brain are the right places to look for the foundations of morality, because losing them interferes with moral competence"],"difficulty_level":"Advanced"},{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Moral Psychology | Intuition vs Reason | Political Division","topic":"Boss Battle: The Puppet Theater of Judgment","visual_frame_description":"A laboratory puppet theater on a hill of felt: the climber trembles on the slope, the helper nudges from below, the hinderer bashes from the top; in the audience, infants in car seats stare, and on a monitor a countdown clock ticks toward a two-minute judgment that may let reason back in.","player_mission":"Run the final gauntlet of the elephant's rule: the hill-climbing puppet show where a climber with glued-on eyes is helped up or bashed down, and the infants stare longer at the climber's wrong choice - then reach for the helper. Follow the two-minute countdown of Paxton and Greene's Harvard study and decide who wins when you can't answer right away: the elephant's lean, or the rider's delayed reasoning.","key_concepts":["Joshua Greene, then a graduate student in philosophy at Princeton, teamed up with neuroscientist Jonathan Cohen in 1999 to study what happens in the brain as people make moral judgments","In the famous 'trolley dilemma,' the only way to stop a runaway trolley from killing five people is to push one person off a bridge onto the track below","Utilitarianism says aim for the greatest total good even if a few people get hurt, while deontology holds that we must not harm people in pursuit of other goals, even moral goals such as saving lives","Greene wrote stories involving personal harm, such as pushing the man off the footbridge, and twenty stories involving impersonal harm, such as flipping a switch that diverts the trolley onto a side track where it kills one person","Some philosophers say the switch and the push are morally equivalent - one life for five - but from an intuitionist perspective there's a world of difference, because the bare-handed push produces an initial flash of horror","Greene brought eighteen subjects into an fMRI scanner and had them press one of two buttons to indicate whether each action was appropriate","When people read stories involving personal harm, they showed greater activity in several brain regions related to emotional processing","Across many stories, the relative strength of these emotional reactions predicted the average moral judgment, and Greene published the now-famous study in the journal Science in 2001","In 'The Secret Joke of Kant's Soul,' Greene argued that deontology is mostly a post hoc rationalization of moral intuitions: we have strong feelings that some things simply cannot be done, and we make up a rationally appealing story about rights","Wilson's 1975 prophecy that ethics would be 'biologicized' came true: in the thirty-three years between the Wilson and Greene quotes, everything changed, and the 'new synthesis' arrived","Joe Paxton and Josh Greene asked Harvard students to judge the Julie-and-Mark story, supplying half with a weak argument ('If Julie and Mark make love, then there is more love in the world') and half with a stronger evolutionary argument about contraception - it made no difference","When the computer forced some subjects to wait two minutes before declaring their judgment, the affective lean diminished and the rider had time to think, so people who reflected on the weak argument condemned it less"],"difficulty_level":"Advanced"}]},{"id":"rm04","title":"Chapter 4: Vote for Me (Here's Why)","pages":"73-94","assets":[{"asset_type":"CUE_SCRUBBER_STATION","domain":"Moral Psychology | Intuition vs Reason | Political Division","topic":"Cue Scrubber: The Press Secretary Watch","visual_frame_description":"A press-briefing podium turned diagnostics lab: triplet sequences flash on the teleprompter, index cards of cake and sickness shuffle across the desk, and every justification glows amber the instant it defends the team instead of testing the claim.","player_mission":"Scan a week of reasoning under the cameras - the 2-4-6 rule hunters, the chocolate-cake card sorters, the school-funding arguers, the enzyme-strip lickers - and catch each one in the act of confirming rather than testing. Ring the moment a subject asks 'Can I believe it?' instead of 'Must I believe it?', and the moment the elephant leans and the press secretary starts spinning.","key_concepts":["In Wason's 1960 '2-4-6 problem,' subjects saw the triplet 2-4-6 and generated test triplets, but what they hardly ever did was test their hypotheses by offering triplets that did not conform to them","Proposing 2-4-5 (yes) and 2-4-3 (no) would have helped subjects zero in on the actual rule - any series of ascending numbers - but instead people kept confirming their guesses","Wason called this phenomenon the confirmation bias: the tendency to seek out and interpret new evidence in ways that confirm what you already think","Deanna Kuhn's sets of eight index cards showed a cartoon child eating chocolate cake or carrot cake and then smiling or looking sick; people usually concluded the evidence proved their hunch that chocolate cake makes kids sick, even when the cards showed a stronger association for carrot cake","Kuhn's subjects seemed to think: 'Here is some evidence I can point to as supporting my theory, and therefore the theory is right'","David Perkins asked people of various ages and education levels whether giving schools more money would improve teaching and learning, and found the more education subjects had, the more reasons they came up with","Perkins found barely any improvement in argument quality within each school from first-year to fourth-year students - schools don't teach people to reason thoroughly, they select the applicants with higher IQs","IQ was by far the biggest predictor of how well people argued, but it predicted only the number of my-side arguments - smart people make good lawyers but are no better at finding reasons on the other side","Perkins concluded that 'people invest their IQ in buttressing their own case rather than in exploring the entire issue more fully and evenhandedly'","Tetlock's research distinguishes exploratory thought, the 'evenhanded consideration of alternative points of view,' from confirmatory thought, 'a one-sided attempt to rationalize a particular point of view'","Accountability increases exploratory thought only when people learn before forming an opinion that they will be accountable to an audience, the audience's views are unknown, and the audience is believed to be well informed and interested in accuracy","The rest of the time, accountability pressures simply increase confirmatory thought - people are trying harder to look right than to be right"],"difficulty_level":"Beginner"},{"asset_type":"DYNAMIC_DIALOGUE_SIM","domain":"Moral Psychology | Intuition vs Reason | Political Division","topic":"Dialogue: The Ring of Gyges Chamber","visual_frame_description":"A marble hall in The Republic: on one finger the gold ring of Gyges glints as a shadow-self raids the marketplace and enters houses, while a scale weighs Being against Seeming and the ghost of Glaucon demands proof that justice itself, not its reputation, brings happiness.","player_mission":"Take the throne Glaucon offers: wear the ring of Gyges and decide, honestly, what you would do with invisibility in the marketplace, in other people's houses, and in prison cells. Then put the ring down and face Socrates' reply - justice in the city is justice in the man - and answer the coin-flip question: would you rather be supremely honest and thought a scoundrel, or a cheater thought a paragon of virtue?","key_concepts":["Plato's Republic, one of the most influential works in the Western canon, is an extended argument that it is better to be than to seem virtuous","Early in The Republic, Glaucon, Plato's brother, challenges Socrates to prove that justice itself - and not merely the reputation for justice - leads to happiness","The mythical ring of Gyges makes its wearer invisible at will, letting him take whatever he wants from the marketplace, go into people's houses and have sex with anyone he wished, and kill or release from prison anyone he wished","Glaucon's thought experiment implies that people are only virtuous because they fear the consequences of getting caught, especially the damage to their reputations","Socrates approaches the challenge with an analogy: justice in a man is like justice in a city, where farmers farm, carpenters build, and rulers rule, all contributing to the common good","In an unjust city one group's gain is another's loss, faction schemes against faction, the powerful exploit the weak, and the city is divided against itself","Socrates argues that philosophers must rule, for only they will pursue what is truly good, not just what is good for themselves - and if philosophers must rule the happy city, reason must rule the happy person","At the core of Plato's beliefs was faith in the perfectibility of reason, given to us by the gods and installed in our spherical heads","Haidt praises Glaucon as the guy who got it right: the most important principle for designing an ethical society is to make sure that everyone's reputation is on the line all the time, so that bad behavior will always bring bad consequences","William James urged psychologists to take a 'functionalist' approach - thinking is for doing - and the question becomes what moral reasoning does within the larger system","Tetlock defines accountability as the 'explicit expectation that one will be called upon to justify one's beliefs, feelings, or actions to others'","Tetlock's metaphor: we act like intuitive politicians striving to maintain appealing moral identities in front of our multiple constituencies"],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate"},{"asset_type":"DECEPTION_AUDIT_FILE","domain":"Moral Psychology | Intuition vs Reason | Political Division","topic":"Audit: The Reputation Ledger","visual_frame_description":"A Parliament-style ledger room where expense claims for moat dredging are stamped 'Ring of Gyges,' slips of overpaid cash are sorted by whether the cashier asked, and a sociometer gauge on the wall dips with every declining number from 4 to 2.","player_mission":"Audit a bank of human behavior under the cameras of Glaucon: the UK MPs who dredged their moats, the cashier overpayments, the Ariely math sheets, and Leary's rigged ratings. For every case, file the behavior under reputation-preserved or reputation-exposed, note whether plausible deniability was intact, and total the column: how much of our virtue survives when nobody is watching?","key_concepts":["UK members of Parliament billed taxpayers for second-home expenses so freely - because the approving office approved nearly every request and the claims were hidden from the public - that they thought they were wearing the ring of Gyges, until a newspaper printed a leaked copy of the claims in 2009","Many MPs declared whichever home was due for major and lavish renovations (including dredging the moats) as their second home, then redesignated and renovated the other one too, sometimes selling it for a huge profit","Mark Leary's sociometer theory: self-esteem is more like an internal gauge that continuously measures your value as a relationship partner, triggering an alarm and a change in behavior whenever the needle drops","In Leary's rigged experiment, subjects talked while numbers flashed showing how much a listener in another room wanted to interact with them, with declining ratings (4...3...2...3...2) or rising ratings (4...5...6)","The self-proclaimed mavericks who denied caring about others' opinions suffered shocks almost as big as people who admitted they cared - their compass tracked public opinion, not true north","Leary concluded that the sociometer operates at a nonconscious and preattentive level, scanning the social environment for signs that one's relational value is low or declining - it is part of the elephant","The only people known to have no sociometer are psychopaths","In the cashier study, when a slip error meant subjects were handed too much money, only 20 percent spoke up and corrected the mistake","When the cashier asked whether the payment was correct, 60 percent said no and returned the extra money - being asked directly removes plausible deniability, making people three times more likely to be honest","There is little correlation between people's high-minded answers on Kohlberg-style moral dilemmas and their actual moral behavior, because the rider is not in charge of ethical behavior","Dan Ariely's studies in Predictably Irrational found that when given the opportunity, many honest people will cheat - the majority of people cheated, and they cheated just a little bit","Ariely's cheaters cheated only up to the point where they could no longer find a justification that would preserve their belief in their own honesty, and most left the experiment as convinced of their own virtue as when they walked in"],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate"},{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Moral Psychology | Intuition vs Reason | Political Division","topic":"Discrimination Matrix: Selfish or Groupish","visual_frame_description":"A voter-sorting machine fed by polling data: cards labelled What's in it for me? versus What's in it for my group? race down the lanes, while bumper stickers - causes, universities, teams - light up the badge wall and a polarization gauge splits the output left and right.","player_mission":"Sort a stream of voters, partisans, and citizens onto the correct lane - selfish calculators or groupish badge-wearers - using the evidence of decades of public-opinion research: school parents, draft-age men, the uninsured, the bumper-stickered commuter. Then feed the death-penalty study and the presidential-debate ratings into the polarizer and watch the liberal and conservative columns separate before your eyes.","key_concepts":["Decades of research on public opinion have led to the conclusion that self-interest is a weak predictor of policy preferences","Men of draft age are not more opposed to military escalation than men too old to be drafted, and people who lack health insurance are not more likely to support government-issued health insurance than people covered by insurance","Political scientist Don Kinder summarizes: 'In matters of public opinion, citizens seem to be asking themselves not \"What's in it for me?\" but rather \"What's in it for my group?\"'","Political opinions function as 'badges of social membership,' like the array of bumper stickers people put on their cars showing the political causes, universities, and sports teams they support","Our politics is groupish, not selfish","Studies document the 'attitude polarization' effect: liberals and conservatives actually move further apart when they read about research on whether the death penalty deters crime, rate the quality of arguments in a presidential debate, or evaluate arguments about affirmative action or gun control","If people can see what they want to see in the ambiguous figure B (letter or number 13), there is room for partisans to see different facts in the social world","In 2004, Drew Westen recruited fifteen highly partisan Democrats and fifteen highly partisan Republicans and brought them into an fMRI scanner to watch eighteen sets of slides","Westen's slides caught partisans in a contradiction: a quote from Bush in 2000 praising Ken Lay, the CEO of Enron, a slide describing Bush's later criticism of Enron, and a resolution slide reporting that Bush felt betrayed and was genuinely shocked that Enron's leadership had been corrupt","The threatening information about their own candidate's hypocrisy immediately activated a network of emotion-related brain areas associated with negative emotion and responses to punishment","During the threat there was no increase in activity in the dorsolateral prefrontal cortex, the main area for cool reasoning tasks","When Westen released partisans from the threat, the ventral striatum - one of the brain's major reward centers, where pulses of dopamine manufacture good feelings - started humming, and extreme partisanship may be literally addictive"],"difficulty_level":"Advanced"},{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Moral Psychology | Intuition vs Reason | Political Division","topic":"Boss Battle: The Philosopher's Audit","visual_frame_description":"A philosopher's seminar where the dean's list of donations, votes, and library overdues scrolls behind the lectern; at center stage the Webster's definition of delusion glows over a stack of stolen ethics books, while a network of neurons on the wall fuses into a single reasoning brain.","player_mission":"Confront the rationalist delusion head-on: audit the moral philosophers themselves with Schwitzgebel's measures - charity, voting, calling mothers, blood and organ donation, conference cleanup, student emails - and read the missing-book lists to see whose ethics books are stolen. Then run Mercier and Sperber's final computation: if each reasoner is a biased neuron, can a well-built group reason better than any individual in it?","key_concepts":["Webster's defines delusion as 'a false conception and persistent belief unconquerable by reason in something that has no existence in fact'","The rationalist delusion is the idea that reasoning is our most noble attribute, making us like the gods (for Plato) or bringing us beyond the delusion of believing in gods (for the New Atheists)","Eric Schwitzgebel used surveys and more surreptitious methods to measure how often moral philosophers give to charity, vote, call their mothers, donate blood, donate organs, clean up after themselves at philosophy conferences, and respond to emails purportedly from students","In none of these ways are moral philosophers better than other philosophers or professors in other fields","Schwitzgebel scrounged up the missing-book lists from dozens of libraries and found that academic books on ethics are more likely to be stolen or just never returned than books in other areas of philosophy","Expertise in moral reasoning does not seem to improve moral behavior, and it might even make it worse, perhaps by making the rider more skilled at post hoc justification","Mercier and Sperber reviewed the research on motivated reasoning and concluded that most of the bizarre findings make perfect sense once you see reasoning as having evolved not to help us find truth but to argue","The confirmation bias is a built-in feature of an argumentative mind, not a bug that can be removed from a platonic mind","A neuron is really good at one thing - summing up the stimulation coming into its dendrites to decide whether to fire - and neurons put together in the right way produce a brain; similarly, individuals put together in the right way can produce good reasoning as an emergent property","Each individual reasoner is really good at finding evidence to support the position he or she already holds, usually for intuitive reasons","Groups whose goal is to find truth, such as an intelligence agency or a community of scientists, need intellectual and ideological diversity so that some individuals can use their reasoning to disconfirm the claims of others","You can hire Glaucon as a consultant and make minor and inexpensive tweaks to the environment that produce big increases in ethical behavior"],"difficulty_level":"Advanced"}]},{"id":"rm05","title":"Chapter 5: Beyond WEIRD Morality","pages":"95-111","assets":[{"asset_type":"CUE_SCRUBBER_STATION","domain":"Moral Psychology | Intuition vs Reason | Political Division","topic":"Cue Scrubber: The WEIRD-O-Meter Scan","visual_frame_description":"A West Philadelphia sidewalk rebuilt as a scanning corridor; each frozen interview plays inside a neon ring while a gauge labeled WEIRD-INDEX swings between Analytic and Holistic, and a rotating stack of interview cards waits for its verdict.","player_mission":"Scan twelve interview clips from Haidt's dissertation study and flag every tell of the WEIRD mind: the smooth harm-principle justification, the 73 percent verdict on the chicken story, the analytic answer to the framed-line task. Judge each subject's moral style - autonomy-saturated Penn student or holistic working-class Philadelphian - before the meter stamps their file.","key_concepts":["Haidt recruited working-class adults standing outside a McDonald's in West Philadelphia for his dissertation research and asked them about a family that ate its dog, a woman who used her flag as a rag, and a man and his chicken","When Haidt asked 'Can you tell me why that was wrong?', the working-class subjects often answered with long pauses and disbelieving stares that seemed to say: 'You mean you don't know why it's wrong to do that to a chicken? What planet are you from?'","The Penn students were the most unusual of all twelve groups in Haidt's study, unique in their unwavering devotion to the harm principle put forth by John Stuart Mill in 1859","Mill's harm principle reads: 'The only purpose for which power can be rightfully exercised over any member of a civilized community, against his will, is to prevent harm to others'","As one Penn student said of the chicken story, 'It's his chicken, he's eating it, nobody is getting hurt,' while another said, 'It's perverted, but if it's done in private, it's his right'","The Penn students were the only group in which a majority (73 percent) tolerated the chicken story, yet they were just as likely as the other eleven groups to say the taboo violations would bother them","In 2010 the cultural psychologists Joe Henrich, Steve Heine, and Ara Norenzayan published 'The Weirdest People in the World?', pointing out that psychology's subjects are nearly all WEIRD: Western, educated, industrialized, rich, and democratic","WEIRD people are statistical outliers - the least typical, least representative people you could study - with Americans more extreme outliers than Europeans and the educated upper middle class the most unusual of all","In the framed-line task, Westerners (particularly Americans) excel at the absolute version because they saw the line as an independent object, while East Asians outperform at the relative version because they perceived the relationship among the parts","Most people think holistically, seeing the whole context and the relationships among parts, whereas WEIRD people think analytically, detaching the focal object from its context and assigning it to a category","WEIRD philosophers since Kant and Mill have generated moral systems that are individualistic, rule-based, and universalist, whereas Confucius's Analects is a collection of aphorisms and anecdotes that cannot be reduced to a single rule","The second principle of moral psychology is that there's more to morality than harm and fairness - true descriptively, as a portrait of the moralities we see when we look around the world"],"difficulty_level":"Beginner"},{"asset_type":"DYNAMIC_DIALOGUE_SIM","domain":"Moral Psychology | Intuition vs Reason | Political Division","topic":"Dialogue: The Bhubaneswar Rickshaw Run","visual_frame_description":"A candlelit Bhubaneswar courtyard where women serve in silence behind a screen; on one wall a morality chart lists rights, liberty, and justice, while a scale tips between the individual and the family unit.","player_mission":"Play Haidt in his first weeks in Bhubaneswar, dining with men whose wives silently serve and retreat to the kitchen, and then play the hosts who urge him to be stricter with his servants. Let your elephant lean toward them and your rider search for moral arguments in their defense - then explain whether honoring elders, gods, and guests outweighs equality and personal autonomy.","key_concepts":["Shweder and his colleagues analyzed six hundred interview transcripts and found three major clusters of moral themes, which they called the ethics of autonomy, community, and divinity","The ethic of autonomy treats people as autonomous individuals with wants, needs, and preferences, so societies develop moral concepts such as rights, liberty, and justice","The ethic of community treats people as members of larger entities such as families, teams, armies, companies, tribes, and nations, developing concepts such as duty, hierarchy, respect, reputation, and patriotism","The ethic of divinity treats people as temporary vessels within which a divine soul has been implanted, developing concepts such as sanctity and sin, purity and pollution, elevation and degradation","Haidt realized his best dissertation stories involved disrespect, which violated the ethics of community (the flag used as a rag), or disgust and carnality, which violated the ethics of divinity (the thing with the chicken)","In September 1993 Haidt flew to Bhubaneswar, the capital of Orissa, for three months of funded research on violations of the ethics of divinity, renting a car and driver for $5 a day and living in an apartment with a full-time cook and servant","Haidt arrived in India as a twenty-nine-year-old liberal atheist carrying two incompatible identities: very definite views about right and wrong, and the wish to be like open-minded anthropologists such as Alan Fiske and Richard Shweder","His dissonance disappeared within a few weeks because the normal human capacity for empathy kicked in: 'My elephant leaned toward them, which made my rider search for moral arguments in their defense'","In India Haidt began to see a moral world in which families, not individuals, are the basic unit of society, and equality and personal autonomy were not sacred values","The dictum of cultural psychology is that 'culture and psyche make each other up' - minds function only once they have been filled out by a particular culture","In Bhubaneswar Haidt developed an intuitive sense that left = dirty and right = clean, eating with his right hand and using his left hand to clean himself after defecation","Haidt and his collaborators theorized a vertical dimension of social space running from God or moral perfection at the top down to the devil or perfect evil at the bottom, where high = good = pure = God and low = bad = dirty = animal"],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate"},{"asset_type":"DECEPTION_AUDIT_FILE","domain":"Moral Psychology | Intuition vs Reason | Political Division","topic":"Audit: The Three-Ethics Filing Cabinet","visual_frame_description":"A clerk's office papered with sacred texts and bumper stickers; three brass filing cabinets labeled AUTONOMY, COMMUNITY, and DIVINITY swallow case cards while a banner above reads 'We are multiple from the start.'","player_mission":"File every moral scene under the ethic it serves: autonomy, community, or divinity. Record the flag used as a rag, the chicken carcass, the crucifix drowned in urine, the UVA dining-hall exclamation - and flag every case where your own matrix cannot see the violation.","key_concepts":["Haidt teamed up with Paul Rozin and Clark McCauley to ask why disgust - which originated to keep us away from dirty, contaminating things - can be triggered by betrayal or child abuse but not by robbing a bank or cheating on one's taxes","In the temple topography of Bhubaneswar the courtyard is higher (more pure) than the street, the antechamber higher still, and the inner sanctum could be entered only by the Brahmin priest, who had followed all the necessary rules of personal purity","Haidt began to feel positive essences emanating from some objects, treating certain books with reverence and finding emotional sense in funeral services and burial","The culture wars are battles over sacrilege: whether a flag is just a piece of cloth that can be burned in protest, and whether the artist who submerges a crucifix in urine or smears elephant dung on an image of the Virgin Mary makes the world dirtier, more profane, and more degraded","Haidt's reversed-politics thought experiment: a conservative artist mocking the left's quasi-deification of black leaders with images of Martin Luther King Jr. and Nelson Mandela would trigger angry demonstrations in New York or Paris museums","The ethic of divinity is sometimes incompatible with compassion, egalitarianism, and basic human rights - majorities such as homosexuals or obese people can be ostracized and treated cruelly","The ethic of divinity gives voice to inchoate feelings of elevation and degradation, allowing us to condemn crass consumerism and trivialized sexuality, which the ethic of autonomy cannot criticize","William Gibson's 1984 novel Neuromancer coined the term cyberspace and described a 'matrix' of 'consensual hallucination,' later developed in the film The Matrix, where the protagonist Neo swallows the red pill","Shweder's writings were Haidt's red pill: he saw that many moral matrices coexist within each nation, each providing a complete, unified, and emotionally compelling worldview nearly impregnable to attack from outsiders","Haidt grew up Jewish in the suburbs of New York City; his grandparents had fled czarist Russia for the garment industry, for whom socialism, labor unions, and Franklin Roosevelt were heroes","At Yale, ranked at the time as the second most liberal of the Ivy League schools, students strongly supported the victims of apartheid, the people of El Salvador, the government of Nicaragua, the environment, and Yale's striking labor unions","Haidt and his fellow liberals looked for psychological explanations of conservatism but not liberalism - seeing conservative policies as self-interest or thinly veiled racism - and never considered that there were alternative moral worlds"],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate"},{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Moral Psychology | Intuition vs Reason | Political Division","topic":"Discrimination Matrix: WEIRD or Sociocentric","visual_frame_description":"A sorting chamber where a square containing a line is projected onto two frames, one absolute and one relative; twin chutes labeled Analytic and Holistic swallow scenes while a banner reads 'Moral matrices bind people together and blind them to the coherence, or even existence, of other matrices.'","player_mission":"Sort a stream of perception data and moral scenes onto the two great modes: the analytic, object-focused WEIRD mind and the holistic, relationship-centered sociocentric mind. Justify every call - the framed-line result, the Analects aphorism, the Penn student's 'It's his right' - before the cases multiply into a matrix war.","key_concepts":["The WEIRDer you are, the more you perceive a world full of separate objects rather than relationships","Moral pluralism is true descriptively: as a simple matter of anthropological fact, the moral domain varies across cultures","The moral domain is unusually narrow in WEIRD cultures, largely limited to the ethic of autonomy - moral concerns about individuals harming, oppressing, or cheating other individuals","The moral domain is broader, including the ethics of community and divinity, in most other societies and within religious and conservative moral matrices inside WEIRD societies","Moral matrices bind people together and blind them to the coherence, or even existence, of other matrices, making it difficult to consider that there might be more than one form of moral truth","The Penn students were the only group that frequently ignored their own feelings of disgust and said that an action that bothered them was nonetheless morally permissible","If you grow up in a WEIRD society you become so well educated in the ethic of autonomy that you can detect oppression and inequality even where the apparent victims see nothing wrong","If you are raised in a more traditional society you become so well educated in the ethics of community and divinity that you can detect disrespect and degradation even where the apparent victims see nothing wrong","In a sociocentric morality, as Shweder described it, you place the needs of groups and institutions first, often ahead of the needs of individuals, so a morality of harm and fairness will not be sufficient","Haidt first read about Shweder's three ethics in 1991, after collecting his data in Brazil but before writing his dissertation","In India Haidt could see the ugly side of the ethic of community: power sometimes leads to pomposity and abuse, and subordinates - particularly women - were often blocked by the whims of their elders","From the vantage point of the ethic of community, the ethic of autonomy seemed overly individualistic and self-focused"],"difficulty_level":"Advanced"},{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Moral Psychology | Intuition vs Reason | Political Division","topic":"Boss Battle: The Red Pill Choice","visual_frame_description":"A vat-lit corridor of green code dissolving into a map of moral matrices; two pills float on a tray while the Declaration, the Analects, and the Koran glow side by side on shelves.","player_mission":"Stand where Neo stood and take the red pill: dissolve the matrix you were raised in - the Jewish New York suburbs, Yale, the jokes about Ronald Reagan - and learn to hear Falwell and Robertson with clinical detachment. Then face the final test: listening to King's 'I Have a Dream' speech, find the latent intuitions of oppression and equality in your own righteous mind.","key_concepts":["In his three months in India Haidt met very few Americans, and it was only in India that he had to stand alone outside his home matrix","When Haidt returned to America, social conservatives no longer seemed so crazy, and he could listen to leaders of the religious right such as Jerry Falwell and Pat Robertson with clinical detachment","Haidt could see why Christian conservatives wanted to 'thicken up' the moral climate of schools and discourage the view that children should be as free as possible to act on their desires","Once he was no longer angry, Haidt was no longer committed to the conclusion that righteous anger demands: we are right, they are wrong","Shweder wrote in 1991: 'There is no homogeneous \"backcloth\" to our world. We are multiple from the start'","Our minds have the potential to become righteous about many different concerns, and only a few of these concerns are activated during childhood","Years later you might find other moral intuitions latent within yourself, responding to dilemmas involving authority, sexuality, or the human body in ways that are hard to explain","If you face discrimination yourself, or simply listen to Martin Luther King Jr.'s 'I Have a Dream' speech, you may find a new resonance in moral arguments about oppression and equality","As an intuitionist, Haidt believes it is a mistake to raise the question of whether alternative moralities are really good, true, or justifiable until we have calmed our elephants and cultivated understanding","Each moral matrix provides a complete, unified, and emotionally compelling worldview, easily justified by observable evidence","The second principle of moral psychology is that there's more to morality than harm and fairness","In Bhubaneswar Haidt interviewed Hindu priests, monks, and laypeople about their concepts of purity and pollution, trying to understand why Hindus place so much emphasis on bathing, food choices, and what a person has touched"],"difficulty_level":"Advanced"}]},{"id":"rm06","title":"Chapter 6: Taste Buds of the Righteous Mind","pages":"112-127","assets":[{"asset_type":"CUE_SCRUBBER_STATION","domain":"Moral Psychology | Intuition vs Reason | Political Division","topic":"Cue Scrubber: The One-Receptor Scanner","visual_frame_description":"A white-walled tasting bar where five small spoons lie at each place setting; a scanner sweeps menus and manuscripts while gauges labeled Welfare, Rights, and Virtues judge how many moral receptors each dish activates.","player_mission":"Scan twelve moral theories and cuisines served at The True Taste and detect the monist signature: a menu of only sweeteners, a single principle of welfare, a single principle of rights. Name each dish - The Utilitarian Grill or The Deontological Diner - and ring the alarm the instant only one taste receptor is being fed.","key_concepts":["The True Taste restaurant had an entirely white interior, each table set only with five small spoons, and a menu divided into sections labeled 'Sugars,' 'Honeys,' 'Tree Saps,' and 'Artificials'","The waiter, who was also the owner and sole employee, was a biologist who specialized in the sense of taste and ran the first tasting bar for sweeteners in the world, with samples from thirty-two countries","The five kinds of taste receptor found in each taste bud on the tongue are sweet, sour, salty, bitter, and savory (also called umami)","The owner's research had shown that activation of the sweet receptor produced the strongest surge of dopamine, indicating humans are hard-wired to seek sweetness above the other four tastes","Business was 'Terrible,' he said, 'but at least I'm doing better than the chemist down the street who opened a salt-tasting bar'","Many authors reduce morality to a single principle, usually welfare maximization, or to justice and notions of fairness, rights, and respect for autonomy - The Utilitarian Grill serves only sweeteners (welfare) and The Deontological Diner serves only salts (rights)","Moral monism - the attempt to ground all of morality on a single principle - leads to societies that are unsatisfying to most people and at high risk of becoming inhumane because they ignore so many other moral principles","The Chinese sage Mencius made the analogy between morality and food 2,300 years ago, writing that 'moral principles please our minds as beef and mutton and pork please our mouths'","The righteous mind is like a tongue with six taste receptors, and morality is like cuisine - a cultural construction influenced by accidents of environment and history, but not so flexible that anything goes","You can't have a cuisine based on tree bark, nor one based primarily on bitter tastes; moral matrices vary, but they all must please righteous minds equipped with the same six social receptors","Just knowing that everyone has sweetness receptors can't tell you why one person prefers Thai food to Mexican or why hardly anyone stirs sugar into beer - connecting the universal receptors to particular judgments takes a lot of additional work","Hume thought morality was based in a variety of sentiments, so he relied on sensory analogies: moral judgment is a kind of perception, and moral science should begin with a careful study of the moral taste receptors"],"difficulty_level":"Beginner"},{"asset_type":"DYNAMIC_DIALOGUE_SIM","domain":"Moral Psychology | Intuition vs Reason | Political Division","topic":"Dialogue: The Philosopher's After-Dinner Table","visual_frame_description":"An Edinburgh candlelit parlor where an argument rages across a table set with ink pots and folios; a subtitle reads 'Being an Attempt to Introduce the Experimental Method of Reasoning into Moral Subjects.'","player_mission":"Take the seat of David Hume at a table with Plato, Kant, and Bentham and argue the three-way battle: is morality deduced by reason or discovered in human nature, like taste? Play the sentimentalist, then the rationalist, and decide whether moral science can begin without studying the moral taste receptors.","key_concepts":["Hume fought a three-way battle: Enlightenment thinkers were united in rejecting divine revelation as the source of moral knowledge, but divided over whether morality transcended human nature (deducible by reasoning, as Plato believed) or was part of human nature, like language or taste","Rationalists and empiricists who thought they could find the way to moral truth without looking at human nature were no better than theologians who found it revealed in sacred texts - both were transcendentalists","The subtitle of Hume's first great work, A Treatise of Human Nature, was 'Being an Attempt to Introduce the Experimental Method of Reasoning into Moral Subjects'","Hume believed that 'moral science' had to begin with careful inquiry into what humans are really like, examining human nature in history, in political affairs, and among his fellow philosophers","Hume saw that 'sentiment' (intuition) is the driving force of our moral lives, whereas reasoning is biased and impotent, fit primarily to be a servant of the passions","Hume rejected attempts by contemporaries to reduce all of morality to a single virtue such as kindness, or to do away with virtues and replace them with a few moral laws","Hume's taste analogy: 'Morality is nothing in the abstract Nature of Things, but is entirely relative to the Sentiment or mental Taste of each particular Being; in the same Manner as the Distinctions of sweet and bitter, hot and cold, arise from the particular feeling of each Sense or Organ'","Hume wrote that moral perceptions ought not to be classed with the operations of the understanding, but with the tastes or sentiments","When Hume died in 1776, he and the other sentimentalists had laid a superb foundation for 'moral science,' one that has been largely vindicated by modern research","In the decades after Hume's death the rationalists claimed victory over religion and took the moral sciences off on a two-hundred-year tangent","You can't possibly deduce the list of five taste receptors by pure reasoning, which is why the moral sciences had to begin with observation of human nature","Because morality is based in sentiments that give us pleasure at virtue and displeasure at vice, Hume relied on sensory analogies such as the distinction between sweet and bitter"],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate"},{"asset_type":"DECEPTION_AUDIT_FILE","domain":"Moral Psychology | Intuition vs Reason | Political Division","topic":"Audit: The Systemizer Case Files","visual_frame_description":"A Victorian consulting room where two dossiers lie open on a desk: one stamped with a felicific calculus ledger, the other with a pocket watch stopped at three-thirty; a two-axis chart of empathizing versus systemizing glows on the wall.","player_mission":"Audit the private lives of the founders of Western ethics and file each piece of evidence against Baron-Cohen's two spectra, empathizing and systemizing. Record the three-thirty walk, the flies-of-a-summer remark, the felicific calculus, the categorical imperative - then rule on the Asperger's diagnosis for Bentham, and for Kant.","key_concepts":["According to Simon Baron-Cohen, empathizing is 'the drive to identify another person's emotions and thoughts, and to respond to these with an appropriate emotion,' while systemizing is 'the drive to analyse the variables in a system, to derive the underlying rules that govern the behaviour of the system'","Autism, including Asperger's syndrome, is better thought of as a region of personality-space - the lower right corner of the lower right quadrant of the empathizing-systemizing grid - than as a discrete disease","At the extreme end of the autism spectrum people are 'mind-blind,' missing the social-cognitive software the rest of us use to guess the intentions and desires of other people","The two leading ethical theories in Western philosophy were founded by men who were as high as could be on systemizing and were rather low on empathizing","Jeremy Bentham was born in England in 1748, went to Oxford at the age of twelve, trained as a lawyer, and devoted his career to reforming the mess of contradictory English law","Bentham's most important work, Introduction to the Principles of Morals and Legislation, defined the principle of utility as approving or disapproving of every action according to its tendency to augment or diminish the happiness of the party whose interest is in question","Bentham systematized the parameters of utility, including the intensity, duration, and certainty of 'hedons' (pleasures) and 'dolors' (pains), offering an algorithm - the felicific calculus - for summing them into a moral verdict on any action, for any person, in any country","In 'Asperger's Syndrome and the Eccentricity and Genius of Jeremy Bentham,' Philip Lucas and Anne Sheeran found a close match with the diagnostic criteria; a contemporary said Bentham regarded the people about him 'no more than the flies of a summer'","John Stuart Mill, a decidedly non-autistic utilitarian, came to despise Bentham, writing that conceiving of a mind that 'differ from itself, and throws itself into the feelings of that other mind, was denied him by his deficiency of Imagination'","Lucas and Sheeran concluded that had Bentham been alive today, 'it is likely he would have received the diagnosis of Asperger's syndrome'","Kant, born in Prussia in 1724, was disturbed by the subjectivity that sentimentalism implied for ethics and, like Plato, wanted to discover the timeless, changeless form of the Good through a priori philosophizing","Kant was famous for his love of routine, setting out for his afternoon walk at precisely three-thirty every day regardless of the weather, yet unlike Bentham he was widely liked - the safest view is that he was an extraordinary systemizer rather low on empathizing, without joining Bentham at the bottom right corner of the grid"],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate"},{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Moral Psychology | Intuition vs Reason | Political Division","topic":"Discrimination Matrix: The Six-Receptor Pantry","visual_frame_description":"A pantry of shelves labeled with the five foundations; cards shaped like taste buds slide into slots while a sixth empty shelf waits, labeled Liberty/oppression, to be filled in chapter 8.","player_mission":"Sort the adaptive challenges, original triggers, current triggers, emotions, and virtues into their proper foundations - Care, Fairness, Loyalty, Authority, Sanctity. Watch for the traps: baby seals are current triggers, broken vending machines trigger fairness, and the hover fly's stripes are a module exploit.","key_concepts":["The prevailing view among anthropologists had long been that evolution got our species to being bipedal, tool-using, large-brained creatures, but once we developed the capacity for culture, biological evolution stopped or became irrelevant","Virtues are social constructions: the virtues taught to children in a warrior culture differ from those of a farming culture or a modern industrialized culture, and Buddha, Christ, and Muhammad all talked about compassion, but in rather different ways","It would be silly to posit separate receptors for mango juice, apple juice, Coca-Cola, and Fanta - there is one main sweetness receptor at work, and each culture has invented various ways to trigger it","If an Eskimo tribe has no sweet beverage, it does not mean they lack the sweetness receptor; it just shows that Eskimo cuisine makes little use of it, for the obvious reason that Eskimos, until recently, had little access to fruit","Primatologists report that chimpanzees and bonobos love fruit and will work hard in a laboratory task to obtain a sip of Coca-Cola, which strengthens the case for an innate sweet receptor","Haidt and Craig Joseph - whose research examined virtue concepts among Muslims in Egypt and the United States - borrowed the idea of 'modularity' from cognitive anthropologists Dan Sperber and Lawrence Hirschfeld","Modules are like little switches in the brains of all animals: many animals react with fear the very first time they see a snake because their brains include neural circuits that function as snake detectors","Sperber and Hirschfeld distinguished original triggers (the objects a module was designed for) from current triggers (everything that happens to trigger it, such as toy snakes, curved sticks, and thick ropes)","The hover fly has evolved yellow and black stripes, making it look like a wasp, which triggers the wasp-avoidance module in some birds that would otherwise enjoy eating hover flies","Cultures can shrink or expand the current triggers of any module in a single generation: in the past fifty years Westerners have come to feel compassion for many more kinds of animal suffering and disgust in response to many fewer kinds of sexual activity","Spanking shows how one action can hook into competing modules: on the left it typically triggers judgments of cruelty and oppression, while on the right it is sometimes linked to the proper enforcement of rules about respect for parents and teachers","Five adaptive challenges stood out most clearly: caring for vulnerable children, forming partnerships with non-kin to reap the benefits of reciprocity, forming coalitions to compete with other coalitions, negotiating status hierarchies, and keeping oneself and one's kin free from parasites and pathogens"],"difficulty_level":"Advanced"},{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Moral Psychology | Intuition vs Reason | Political Division","topic":"Boss Battle: The Operating Theater Trial","visual_frame_description":"An operating theater behind glass where a surgeon's knife glints over a small unconscious child; a screen splits the scene into hedons and dolors while a pair of Muller-Lyer lines proves that the eye cannot help itself.","player_mission":"Stand behind the glass of the operating theater as your anesthetized four-year-old son's appendix is cut open, and judge your own reaction against the hedons and dolors of the felicific calculus. Then face the two nurses and rule as a utilitarian must - no preference - or answer for the module that makes the older nurse a virtuoso of caring.","key_concepts":["Watching your anesthetized four-year-old son's appendix operation, if your 'dolors' outweigh your 'hedons,' your reaction is irrational from a utilitarian point of view but makes perfect sense as the output of a module","We respond emotionally to signs of violence or suffering particularly when a child is involved, even when we know consciously that it's not really violence - just as in the Muller-Lyer illusion we can't help but see one line as longer","If patients under deep anesthetic don't hear or feel anything, then a utilitarian should have no preference between the older nurse who strokes the child's head and the younger nurse who is all business","The older nurse has so fully acquired the virtue of caring that she does it automatically and effortlessly even when it has no effect - she is a virtuoso of caring, a fine and beautiful thing, and 'it tastes good'","Deontology and utilitarianism are 'one-receptor' moralities that are likely to appeal most strongly to people who are high on systemizing and low on empathizing","Hume's pluralist, sentimentalist, and naturalist approach to ethics is more promising than utilitarianism or deontology for modern moral psychology","Five good candidates for being taste receptors of the righteous mind are care, fairness, loyalty, authority, and sanctity","The first draft of the five foundations (figure 6.2) gave each foundation an adaptive challenge, original triggers, current triggers, characteristic emotions, and relevant virtues","The original trigger for Care is the suffering, distress, or neediness expressed by one's child; current triggers expand to baby seals and cute cartoon characters, with compassion as the characteristic emotion","The Sanctity foundation's original triggers are waste products and diseased people, with disgust as its emotion and temperance, chastity, piety, and cleanliness among its virtues","Kohlberg embraced Kant's rationalism, creating a theory in which moral development had one end point - a full understanding of justice - which felt oversystemized and underempathized, like The True Taste restaurant serving up a one-receptor morality","The sixth foundation - Liberty/oppression - is presented in chapter 8"],"difficulty_level":"Advanced"}]},{"id":"rm07","title":"Chapter 7: The Moral Foundations of Politics","pages":"128-154","assets":[{"asset_type":"CUE_SCRUBBER_STATION","domain":"Moral Psychology | Intuition vs Reason | Political Division","topic":"Cue Scrubber: The Price-Tag Scanner","visual_frame_description":"A market stall turned diagnostic booth where ten price tags hang from a wire; a scanner rings each tag with a colored aura while a ledger of zeros through fours sums the verdicts of a human being, not an economist's fantasy.","player_mission":"Read the ten items of figure 7.1 as they flash past and detect which foundation each column-B action fires: the needle in a child's arm, the television bought from a thief, the criticism of your nation on foreign airwaves, the slap of your father, the actors grunting like chimpanzees. Stamp each verdict with its foundation - Care, Fairness, Loyalty, Authority, or Sanctity - before Homo economicus claims the case for self-interest.","key_concepts":["Homo economicus is a simple creature who makes all of life's choices like a shopper in a supermarket with plenty of time to compare jars of applesauce, and there is really just one principle at work: self-interest","The ten questions of figure 7.1 ask how much someone would have to pay you to perform each action, paid secretly with no social, legal, or other harmful consequences: 0 = $0, I'd do it for free; 1 = $100; 2 = $10,000; 3 = $1,000,000; 4 = I would not do this for any amount of money","Homo economicus would put a price on sticking a needle into his own arm and a lower price - perhaps zero - on the other nine actions, none of which hurts him directly or costs him anything","Each column-B item was written to give an intuitive flash from a foundation: hurting a child (Care), profiting from someone else's undeserved loss (Fairness), criticizing your nation to outsiders (Loyalty), disrespecting your father (Authority), and acting in a degrading or disgusting way (Sanctity)","If you found any of the actions in column B worse than their counterparts in column A, then congratulations - you are a human being, not an economist's fantasy, with concerns beyond narrow self-interest","The neuroscientist Gary Marcus: 'Nature bestows upon the newborn a considerably complex brain, but one that is best seen as prewired - flexible and subject to change - rather than hardwired, fixed, and immutable'","Marcus's analogy: the brain is like a book, the first draft of which is written by the genes during fetal development - no chapters are complete at birth, but not a single chapter can be inscribed by experience in any conceivable set of words","The best definition of innateness Haidt has ever seen: organized in advance of experience - 'built-in' does not mean unmalleable","It used to be risky for a scientist to assert that anything about human behavior was innate: you had to show the trait was hardwired, unchangeable by experience, and found in all cultures, a standard that left little innate besides a few infant reflexes","We have advanced a lot since the 1970s in our understanding of the brain, and now we know that traits can be innate without being either hardwired or universal","The list of five moral foundations was Haidt's first attempt to specify how the righteous mind was 'organized in advance of experience'","Moral Foundations Theory also tries to explain how that first draft gets revised during childhood to produce the diversity of moralities we find across cultures - and across the political spectrum"],"difficulty_level":"Beginner"},{"asset_type":"DYNAMIC_DIALOGUE_SIM","domain":"Moral Psychology | Intuition vs Reason | Political Division","topic":"Dialogue: The First Draft Interview","visual_frame_description":"A library where a book's unbound pages glow as they fall from a press; one page shows a mother's face bending over a newborn, another shows a coworker handing over a shift, while a marginal note reads 'organized in advance of experience.'","player_mission":"Interview Gary Marcus about the brain as a book whose first draft is written by the genes, then question a mother about the enormous bet that is a human baby and the adaptive challenge of the Care foundation. Ask what is organized in advance of experience, whether the chapter on mothering could be entirely blank, and how Robert Trivers's tit for tat turns selfish genes into generous creatures.","key_concepts":["When the first mammals began suckling their young, they raised the cost of motherhood - no longer turning out dozens of babies, they made fewer bets and invested a lot more in each one","Human babies, whose brains are so enormous that a child must be pushed out through the birth canal a year before he or she can walk, are bets so huge that a woman needs help in the last months of pregnancy, at the delivery, and for years after the birth","Mothers who were innately sensitive to signs of suffering, distress, or neediness improved their odds relative to their less sensitive sisters","The suffering of your own children is the original trigger of one of the key modules of the Care foundation","The Care foundation's origin story is a retelling of the beginning of attachment theory, which describes the system by which mothers and children regulate each other's behavior so the child gets a good mix of protection and opportunities for independent exploration","The set of current triggers for a module is often much larger than its original triggers: cuteness primes us to care, nurture, protect, and interact - it gets the elephant leaning","Max's companions Gogo and Baby Gogo were designed by a toy company to trigger the Care foundation, and Max screams when Haidt accidentally sits on Gogo and often says, 'I am Gogo's mommy'","Darwin doesn't have to explain why you shed any particular tear over a hungry child in a faraway country or a baby seal - he just has to explain why you have tear ducts and why those ducts can sometimes be activated by suffering that is not your own","Political parties and interest groups strive to make their concerns become current triggers of your moral modules - to get your vote, your money, or your time, they must activate at least one of your moral foundations","The driver of the 'Save Darfur' car in Charlottesville is announcing liberal team membership, because the liberal matrix rests more heavily on the Care foundation, while the 'wounded warrior' car shows conservative caring that is aimed not at animals or foreigners but at those who've sacrificed for the group - local, and blended with loyalty","A coworker who offers to take on your workload for five days so you can add a second week to your Caribbean vacation has given you a favor you can't repay with a bottle of rum - you accept it gushing gratitude, praise, and a promise to do the same","Robert Trivers's 1971 theory of reciprocal altruism shows that 'selfish' genes can give rise to generous creatures, as long as those creatures are selective in their generosity"],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate"},{"asset_type":"DECEPTION_AUDIT_FILE","domain":"Moral Psychology | Intuition vs Reason | Political Division","topic":"Audit: The Robbers Cave Field Log","visual_frame_description":"A state-park ranger station turned evidence room: a topographical map of Robbers Cave glows with two colored territories, Rattlers and Eagles, while photographs of bunks and flags drop into a logbook stamped TRIBAL BEHAVIOR.","player_mission":"File the summer of 1954 event by event: the Rattlers' rock path, the paper cups, the claim on the baseball field, the flags hung in contested territory, the socks filled with rocks. Log every entry under the Loyalty foundation's account - tribal identity, territory, trophies, and treachery - and mark the exact moment Sherif's tournament ignited tribal behavior.","key_concepts":["In the summer of 1954, Muzafer Sherif convinced twenty-two sets of working-class parents to let him take their twelve-year-old boys to a summer camp he had rented in Robbers Cave State Park, Oklahoma","Sherif brought the boys to the camp in two groups of eleven on two consecutive days and housed them in different parts of the park; for the first five days each group thought it was alone","The Rattlers discovered a good swimming hole upstream from the main camp and claimed it as their own special hideout, laying a rock path down to the water, and were disturbed to discover paper cups there","A leader emerged in each group by consensus, and norms, songs, rituals, and distinctive identities began to form: Rattlers are tough and never cry; Eagles never curse","On day 6 Sherif let the Rattlers get close enough to the baseball field to hear that the Eagles were using it, even though the Rattlers had claimed it - and then, as planned, he arranged a weeklong tournament of sports competitions and camping skills","'Tribal behavior increased dramatically': both sides created flags and hung them in contested territory, destroyed each other's flags, raided and vandalized each other's bunks, called each other nasty names, and made weapons such as socks filled with rocks","The male mind appears to be innately tribal - structured in advance of experience so that boys and men enjoy doing the sorts of things that lead to group cohesion and success in conflicts between groups, including warfare","The original trigger for the Loyalty foundation is anything that tells you who is a team player and who is a traitor, particularly when your team is fighting with other teams","A trophy is evidence of victory, and the urge to take trophies - including body parts from slain foes - is widespread in warfare, occurring even during modern times","In The Inferno, Dante reserves the innermost circle of hell, and the most excruciating suffering, for the crime of treachery - far worse than lust, gluttony, violence, or even heresy","The left tends toward universalism and away from nationalism, so it often has trouble connecting to voters who rely on the Loyalty foundation, and because of its strong reliance on the Care foundation, American liberals are often hostile to American foreign policy","Ann Coulter's 2003 book Treason: Liberal Treachery from the Cold War to the War on Terrorism made it easy for conservatives to connect liberalism to the Loyalty foundation - and not in a good way"],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate"},{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Moral Psychology | Intuition vs Reason | Political Division","topic":"Discrimination Matrix: Authority or Sanctity","visual_frame_description":"A great hall with two sorting racks, one topped with a gavel and one with a censer; kings, priests, fathers, and filth slide down chutes while a banner reads 'the reaction to desecration is sure to be swift, emotional, collective, and punitive.'","player_mission":"Sort the gallery of scenes onto their proper foundation: the Jordanian taxi driver's vow, the vous and tu of French, the Code of Hammurabi, the alpha chimp's control role, the Meiwes house stain, the Allegory of Chastity. Justify each call - legitimate asymmetry or pathogen avoidance - before the sacred pillars of the community shake.","key_concepts":["The Jordanian taxi driver told Haidt: 'We will return to Jordan because I never want to hear my son say \"fuck you\" to me'","The urge to respect hierarchical relationships is so deep that many languages encode it directly: French speakers must choose between the respectful form (vous) and the familiar form (tu), and Americans until recently addressed strangers and superiors by title plus last name","The displays made by low-ranking individuals are often similar across species because their function is always the same - to appear submissive, which means small and nonthreatening","Even among chimpanzees, where dominance hierarchies are about raw power, the alpha male performs socially beneficial functions such as taking on the 'control role,' resolving disputes and suppressing the violent conflict that erupts when there is no clear alpha male","Frans de Waal: 'Without agreement on rank and a certain respect for authority there can be no great sensitivity to social rules, as anyone who has tried to teach simple house rules to a cat will agree'","The Code of Hammurabi (eighteenth century BCE) opens with Anu and Bel calling Hammurabi 'to bring about the rule of righteousness in the land, to destroy the wicked and the evil-doers; so that the strong should not harm the weak'","Haidt began graduate school subscribing to the common liberal belief that hierarchy = power = exploitation = evil, until Alan Fiske's theory of Authority Ranking showed relationships based on perceptions of legitimate asymmetries, not coercive power, that are not inherently exploitative","Fiske's Authority Ranking examples include military hierarchies, ancestor worship (including offerings of filial piety), and monotheistic religious moralities","The Authority foundation is more complex than the others because its modules must look in two directions - up toward superiors and down toward subordinates - to meet the adaptive challenge of forging beneficial relationships within hierarchies","Current triggers of the Authority foundation include anything construed as an act of obedience, disobedience, respect, disrespect, submission, or rebellion with regard to legitimate authorities, plus acts that subvert the traditions, institutions, or values perceived to provide stability","Figure 7.7 contrasts an advertisement for the liberal magazine The Nation with the sign of Aldersgate United Methodist Church in Charlottesville - while Methodists are not necessarily conservative, 'the sign in front of their church tells you they ain't no Unitarians'","The Sanctity foundation makes it easy to regard some things as 'untouchable' both in a bad way (because something is so dirty or polluted we want to stay away) and in a good way (because something is so hallowed, so sacred, that we want to protect it from desecration)"],"difficulty_level":"Advanced"},{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Moral Psychology | Intuition vs Reason | Political Division","topic":"Boss Battle: The Meiwes Verdict","visual_frame_description":"A courtroom where the evidence table holds a frying pan, a meat hook, and a video camera; behind the judge the vertical dimension of divinity rises from worms and demons up to God, with a lone house on the ground awaiting its verdict.","player_mission":"Face the Meiwes case head-on: read the advertisement, review the video of consent, weigh the manslaughter verdict, and then rule on what the ethic of autonomy cannot see. Decide what you would pay to live in that house, whether the stain could be expunged only by fire, and whether repugnance is a warning or a prejudice - and thread the neophilia and neophobia of the omnivore's dilemma.","key_concepts":["In early 2001, Armin Meiwes, a German computer technician, posted an advertisement on the Web - 'Looking for a well-built 21-to-30-year-old to be slaughtered and then consumed' - and hundreds of men responded by email","Bernd Brandes, a forty-three-year-old computer engineer, was the first respondent who didn't change his mind when he realized that Meiwes was not engaging in mere fantasy","On the evening of March 9, the two men made a video to prove that Brandes fully consented; Brandes took sleeping pills and alcohol, but was still alert when Meiwes cut off his penis, after being unable to bite it off as Brandes had requested","Meiwes sautéed the penis in a frying pan with wine and garlic; Brandes took a bite, then went off to a bathtub to bleed to death, and a few hours later Meiwes kissed him, stabbed him in the throat, hung the body on a meat hook, and ate the flesh gradually over the next ten months","Because Brandes's participation was fully voluntary, Meiwes was convicted only of manslaughter, not murder, the first time the case went to trial","Any attempt to condemn Meiwes or Brandes runs smack into John Stuart Mill's harm principle - from within the ethic of autonomy, adults have a right to live their lives as they please, and to end them how and when they please, as long as they harm nobody","Haidt's questions: would you find Meiwes's return to a house a block from yours unsettling, might you feel some relief if social pressure forced him out of town, and how much would someone have to pay you to live in the house where the atrocity happened for a week?","Feelings of stain, pollution, and purification are irrational from a utilitarian point of view but make perfect sense in Shweder's ethic of divinity - Meiwes and Brandes behaved as low as any humans can go on the vertical dimension, where only worms and demons eat human flesh","A koala bear's sensory systems are 'structured in advance of experience' to guide it to eucalyptus leaves, but omnivores must solve the 'omnivore's dilemma' (a term coined by Paul Rozin), torn between neophilia and neophobia","Liberals score higher on measures of neophilia (also known as 'openness to experience') - not just for new foods but for new people, music, and ideas - while conservatives are higher on neophobia, preferring what's tried and true and caring more about guarding borders, boundaries, and traditions","The psychologist Mark Schaller has shown that disgust is part of what he calls the 'behavioral immune system' - a set of cognitive modules triggered by signs of infection or disease in other people that make you want to get away from those people","Leon Kass's 1997 essay 'The Wisdom of Repugnance,' written the year after Dolly the sheep became the first cloned mammal, argued that disgust can warn us that we are close to a line - 'Shallow are the souls that have forgotten how to shudder'"],"difficulty_level":"Advanced"}]},{"id":"rm08","title":"Chapter 8: The Conservative Advantage","pages":"155-188","assets":[{"asset_type":"CUE_SCRUBBER_STATION","domain":"Moral Psychology | Intuition vs Reason | Political Division","topic":"Cue Scrubber: The Slogan Scrubber","visual_frame_description":"A campaign war room walled with slogan cards; each card is scanned by a device that lights up one to five receptors - sugar for Care, salt for Fairness as equality - while a chyron reads 'Republicans understand moral psychology. Democrats don't.'","player_mission":"Scan the political slogans, ads, and stump lines and score each against the five taste receptors: 'America can do better,' 'Help is on the way,' 'You gonna pay for that, George?,' the Willie Horton mug shot, 'Country First.' Rule which foundations each one triggers before the elephant casts its vote.","key_concepts":["In January 2005 Haidt was invited to speak to the Charlottesville Democratic Party, having spent much of 2004 as an unpaid speechwriter for John Kerry's presidential campaign, mentally rewriting Kerry's ineffectual appeals while walking his dog","In Kerry's acceptance speech at the Democratic National Convention, 'America can do better' connected to no moral foundation at all, while 'Help is on the way' connected weakly to the Care/harm foundation","Haidt's rewrite - 'You gonna pay for that, George?' - would have made Bush's many new programs, on top of tax cuts and two wars, look like shoplifting rather than generosity, and would have activated the cheater detection modules of the Fairness/cheating foundation","The message of the talk was simple: Republicans understand moral psychology; Democrats don't - Republicans have long understood that the elephant is in charge of political behavior, not the rider, and they know how elephants work","The infamous 1988 ad showed a mug shot of a black man, Willie Horton, who committed a brutal murder after being released from prison on a weekend furlough by the 'soft-on-crime' Democratic candidate, Governor Michael Dukakis","Democrats have often aimed their appeals more squarely at the rider, emphasizing specific policies and the benefits they'll bring to you, the voter","Bill Clinton, the only Democrat since Franklin Roosevelt to win election and then reelection, combined gregariousness and oratorical skill with an almost musical emotionality - he knew how to charm elephants","Republicans since Nixon have had a near-monopoly on appeals to loyalty (particularly patriotism and military virtues) and authority (including respect for parents, teachers, elders, and the police, as well as traditions)","After Ronald Reagan's 1980 campaign embraced Christian conservatives and the party became the party of 'family values,' Republicans inherited a powerful network of Christian ideas about sanctity and sexuality","In the story Haidt told the Charlottesville Democrats, Democratic morality was just sugar (Care) and salt (Fairness as equality), whereas Republican morality appealed to all five taste receptors","Haidt didn't blame the Republicans for trickery - he blamed the Democrats for psychological naiveté, and after two consecutive losses to George W. Bush the audience seemed willing to consider his explanation","Back then Haidt's explanation was just speculation: he had not yet collected any data to support his claim that conservatives responded to a broader set of moral tastes than did liberals"],"difficulty_level":"Beginner"},{"asset_type":"DYNAMIC_DIALOGUE_SIM","domain":"Moral Psychology | Intuition vs Reason | Political Division","topic":"Dialogue: The YourMorals.org Briefing","visual_frame_description":"A bare UVA office where a questionnaire scrolls across a whiteboard; four researchers argue over items while a website counter clicks past 132,000 respondents and the five graph lines of figure 8.2 rise and fall.","player_mission":"Join Jesse Graham, Brian Nosek, Ravi Iyer, and Sena Koleva in the room where the MFQ was born and write the fifteen statements - three per foundation - from 'whether or not someone was cruel' to 'whether or not someone showed a lack of respect for authority.' Then defend the scale from 0 to 5 before the first 1,600 subjects answer.","key_concepts":["Jesse Graham arrived at UVA that year - University of Chicago graduate, a master's at the Harvard Divinity School, a year teaching English in Japan - and Haidt felt that if Match.com had offered a way to pair up advisors and grad students, he couldn't have found a better partner","For Jesse's first-year research project he created a questionnaire to measure people's scores on the five moral foundations, working with Haidt's colleague Brian Nosek","The first version of the Moral Foundations Questionnaire (MFQ) began: 'When you decide whether something is right or wrong, to what extent are the following considerations relevant to your thinking?'","The MFQ response scale ran from 0 ('not at all relevant - this has nothing to do with my judgments of right and wrong') to 5 ('extremely relevant - this is one of the most important factors when I judge right and wrong')","The first MFQ listed fifteen statements, three for each of the five foundations, such as 'whether or not someone was cruel' (Care) and 'whether or not someone showed a lack of respect for authority' (Authority)","Using research sites on the Internet, the team was able to recruit 1,600 subjects to fill out the MFQ within a week","Figure 8.1 (adapted from Graham, Haidt, and Nosek 2009) shows responses from people who said they were 'very liberal' on the far left, through moderates in the middle, to people who self-identified as 'very conservative' on the far right","The lines for Care and Fairness are moderately high across the board - everyone, left, right, and center, says compassion, cruelty, fairness, and injustice are relevant - yet the lines still slope downward, because liberals say these issues are a bit more relevant than conservatives do","Liberals largely reject Loyalty, Authority, and Sanctity, showing such a gap that we might say, as shorthand, that liberals have a two-foundation morality, while by the time we reach 'very conservative,' all five lines have converged - a five-foundation morality","Refined MFQ items included the Care item 'One of the worst things a person can do is to hurt a defenseless animal' and the Loyalty item 'It is more important to be a team player than to express oneself,' and Jesse's original findings replicated in many countries besides the United States","Ravi Iyer, a graduate student at the University of Southern California, heard Haidt speak in the fall of 2006 and offered to help create a website, while Sena Koleva, a UC Irvine graduate student studying with Pete Ditto, asked to use the MFQ","All five collaborators were politically liberal, yet they shared a concern that so much research aimed to explain what was wrong with conservatives - that very day, in a session on political psychology, speakers had joked about conservatives and the cognitive limitations of President Bush"],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate"},{"asset_type":"DECEPTION_AUDIT_FILE","domain":"Moral Psychology | Intuition vs Reason | Political Division","topic":"Audit: The Five-Foundation Evidence Vault","visual_frame_description":"A vault lined with evidence drawers: one holds dog photographs, one holds sermon manuscripts, one holds EEG readouts; a clerk stamps each file while a monitor replays liberal and conservative brains flashing surprise in the first half second.","player_mission":"Audit the evidence chain that confirmed Moral Foundations Theory: the Internet graphs, the dog breeds, the Unitarian and Baptist sermons, the EEG waveforms. Certify each exhibit - the gentle dog versus the obedient dog, peace and care versus obey and honor - before filing it under the claim that partisan brains differ within the first half second.","key_concepts":["Figure 8.2 shows MFQ scores from 132,000 subjects in 2011, from YourMorals.org: the lines for Care and Fairness slant downward while the lines for Loyalty, Authority, and Sanctity slant upward, and conservatives endorse all five foundations more or less equally","In the dog-breed study, liberals want dogs that are extremely gentle (fitting the values of the Care foundation) and relate to their owners as friends and equals (Fairness as equality), while conservatives want dogs that are extremely loyal (Loyalty) and very obedient (Authority)","The Sanctity item in the dog study showed no partisan tilt - both sides prefer clean dogs","Jesse obtained the text of dozens of sermons delivered in Unitarian (liberal) churches and dozens more delivered in Southern Baptist (conservative) churches, and used a computer program called LIWC to count foundation-related words","The sermon study used word families such as peace, care, and compassion (positive Care), suffer, cruel, and brutal (negative Care), obey, duty, and honor (positive Authority), and defy, disrespect, and rebel (negative Authority)","Unitarian preachers made greater use of Care and Fairness words, while Baptist preachers made greater use of Loyalty, Authority, and Sanctity words","With UVA social neuroscientist Jamie Morris, the team presented liberal and conservative students with sixty sentences, each in two versions - one endorsing and one rejecting an idea consistent with a particular foundation - flashed on a screen one word at a time","Liberal brains showed more EEG surprise at sentences that rejected Care and Fairness concerns, and also more surprise at sentences that endorsed Loyalty, Authority, and Sanctity concerns (for example, 'In the teenage years, parental advice should be heeded')","Within the first half second after hearing a statement, partisan brains are already reacting differently - these initial flashes of neural activity are the elephant, leaning slightly, which then causes riders to reason differently: intuitions come first, strategic reasoning second","In The Audacity of Hope, Obama showed himself a liberal who understood conservative arguments about the need for order and the value of tradition; on Father's Day he praised marriage and the traditional two-parent family and called on black men to take more responsibility for their children","In Berlin Obama introduced himself as 'a fellow citizen of the world' and spoke of 'global citizenship,' and his refusal to wear an American flag pin fed the narrative that he was a liberal universalist; John McCain took advantage with his campaign motto, 'Country First'","Haidt's Edge essay 'What Makes People Vote Republican?' rejected the standard explanations - overly strict parents, inordinate fear of change, novelty, and complexity, existential fears clinging to a simple worldview - because they used psychology to explain away conservatism"],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate"},{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Moral Psychology | Intuition vs Reason | Political Division","topic":"Discrimination Matrix: Mill or Durkheim","visual_frame_description":"A law library split by a velvet rope: on one side a portrait of Mill above a shelf of contracts, on the other a portrait of Durkheim above a family tree; a scale weighs self-expression against duty, and rights against loyalty.","player_mission":"Sort each social vision onto the correct patron saint: the contract of autonomous individuals under John Stuart Mill, or the organic, hierarchically structured family under Emile Durkheim. File the harm principle, the anomie warning, E pluribus unum, and the American civil religion - and show which foundations each society must rest upon.","key_concepts":["A Millian society is a social contract invented for our mutual benefit: all individuals are equal, and all should be left as free as possible to move, develop talents, and form relationships as they please","The patron saint of the contractual society is John Stuart Mill, who wrote in On Liberty that 'the only purpose for which power can be rightfully exercised over any member of a civilized community, against his will, is to prevent harm to others'","A Millian society at its best would be a peaceful, open, and creative place where diverse individuals respect each other's rights - a vision that rests exclusively on the Care and Fairness foundations","A Durkheimian society emerged organically over time as people found ways of living together, binding themselves to each other, suppressing each other's selfishness, and punishing the deviants and free riders who eternally threaten to undermine cooperative groups - its basic social unit is the hierarchically structured family","The patron saint of the more binding moral system is the sociologist Emile Durkheim, who warned of the dangers of anomie and wrote in 1897 that 'man cannot become attached to higher aims and submit to a rule if he sees nothing above him to which he belongs. To free himself from all social pressure is to abandon himself and demoralize him'","A Durkheimian society would value self-control over self-expression, duty over rights, and loyalty to one's groups over concerns for out-groups - and it cannot be supported by the Care and Fairness foundations alone","The American left fails to understand social conservatives and the religious right because it cannot see a Durkheimian world as anything other than a moral abomination: usually hierarchical, punitive, and religious, and endorsing traditions including traditional gender roles","If your moral matrix rests entirely on the Care and Fairness foundations, it's hard to hear the sacred overtones in America's unofficial motto: E pluribus unum (from many, one)","Sacredness is the ability to endow ideas, objects, and events with infinite value, particularly those that bind a group together into a single entity - the process of converting pluribus (diverse people) into unum (a nation) is a miracle that occurs in every successful nation on Earth","In the 1960s the Democrats became the party of pluribus: they celebrate diversity, support immigration without assimilation, and oppose making English the national language","Robert Bellah calls the presidential performance of unity the 'American civil religion': the president must invoke the name of God (though not Jesus), glorify America's heroes and history, quote its sacred texts - the Declaration of Independence and the Constitution - and perform the transubstantiation of pluribus into unum","One angry reader's email with the subject line 'Head up ass' explained: 'I vote republican because I'm against other people (authority figures) taking my money (that I work hard for) and giving it to a non-producing, welfare collecting, single mother, crack baby producing future democrat'"],"difficulty_level":"Advanced"},{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Moral Psychology | Intuition vs Reason | Political Division","topic":"Boss Battle: The Sixth Foundation Showdown","visual_frame_description":"A coliseum of moral matrices where a sixth pillar rises beside the five; overhead, spears and gossip bind a tribe of equals while a banner revises the final equation - three versus six - and the puzzle of why rural and working-class Americans vote Republican.","player_mission":"Face the final synthesis: add the Liberty/oppression foundation to the theory and re-divide fairness. Rule on Boehm's political transition, the !Kung ambush of Twi, Santelli's tirade against the $75 billion bailout, and the 84 percent who paid to punish in Fehr and Gachter's game - then deliver the revised verdict: three foundations for liberals, six for conservatives.","key_concepts":["Christopher Boehm, in his book Hierarchy in the Forest, concluded that human beings are innately hierarchical, but that at some point during the last million years our ancestors underwent a 'political transition' that allowed them to live as egalitarians by banding together to rein in, punish, or kill any would-be alpha males who tried to dominate the group","Beginning around five hundred thousand years ago, the archaeological record shows a flowering of tool and weapon types - once early humans had developed spears, anyone could kill a bullying alpha male, and the balance of power shifted away from physical strength","Every human society uses language to gossip about moral violations, and Boehm claims that at some point during the last half-million years our ancestors created the first true moral communities","Boehm's !Kung example: a man named Twi had killed three other people when the community, in a rare move of unanimity, ambushed and fatally wounded him in full daylight - as he lay dying, all the men approached his body and stabbed him with spears, symbolically sharing responsibility for his death","People armed with weapons and gossip created what Boehm calls 'reverse dominance hierarchies,' in which the rank and file band together to dominate and restrain would-be alpha males - uncannily similar to Marx's dream of the 'dictatorship of the proletariat'","The end result, says Boehm, was a process sometimes called 'self-domestication': just as animal breeders create tamer creatures, our ancestors began to selectively breed themselves, unintentionally, for the ability to construct shared moral matrices and then live cooperatively within them","The Liberty/oppression foundation's righteous anger at domination is sometimes called reactance - the feeling you get when an authority tells you that you can't do something and you feel yourself wanting to do it even more strongly","The Declaration of Independence is a long enumeration of 'repeated injuries and usurpations, all having in direct object the establishment of absolute Tyranny,' and ends with the pledge of 'our Lives, our Fortunes and our sacred Honor'","Timothy McVeigh, arrested a few hours after he blew up a federal office building in Oklahoma City and killed 168 people in 1993, was wearing a T-shirt that said Sic semper tyrannis, and the Tea Party's unofficial flag says 'Don't tread on me'","The Tea Party began in earnest on February 19, 2009, when Rick Santelli, broadcasting live from the floor of the Chicago Mercantile Exchange, launched a tirade against a new $75 billion program to help homeowners - 'The government is promoting bad behavior'","In Fehr and Gachter's public goods game, Swiss students contributed about ten tokens on the first round, dropping to about six tokens by the sixth round; after a new rule allowed players to pay tokens to punish (each token costing the punished player three), 84 percent of subjects paid to punish, at least once","With the revisions, liberals have a three-foundation morality (Care/harm, Liberty/oppression, and Fairness/cheating) whereas conservatives use all six - and rural and working-class Americans vote Republican because they are voting for their moral interests, not against their economic ones"],"difficulty_level":"Advanced"}]},{"id":"rm09","title":"Chapter 9: Why Are We So Groupish?","pages":"189-220","assets":[{"asset_type":"CUE_SCRUBBER_STATION","domain":"Moral Psychology | Intuition vs Reason | Political Division","topic":"Cue Scrubber: The Multilevel Scanner","visual_frame_description":"A dim evidence chamber where each scene is nested inside the next like Russian dolls - genes within chromosomes within cells within organisms within hives - while a gauge labeled Selection Level swings between Individual and Group and a red lamp marks every lurking free rider.","player_mission":"Scan a gallery of evolutionary tableaux frozen at their decisive instant and judge which level of selection is doing the work in each: the deer fleeing the predator, the termite nest, the fox pup nuzzling its breeder, the hens stacked in their cages. Name every scene individual or group, flag the free riders who hang back to survive the fight, and stamp the verdict before the exhibit seals itself.","key_concepts":["Haidt felt a primitive urge to put an American flag decal on his car after the terrorist attacks of September 11, 2001, and resolved his dilemma by placing an American flag in one corner of his rear windshield and a United Nations flag in the opposite corner","Selfishness means our minds contain mechanisms that promote our own interests in competition with our peers, while groupishness means mechanisms that promote our group's interests in competition with other groups","Group selection was banished as a heresy from scientific circles in the 1970s, but Haidt argues it was falsely convicted and unfairly banished, and presents four exhibits in its defense","In The Descent of Man, Darwin argued that a tribe rich in courageous, sympathetic, and faithful members would succeed and conquer other tribes, but he raised the free rider problem in the very next paragraph","Multilevel selection treats life as a hierarchy of nested levels, like Russian dolls: genes within chromosomes within cells within individual organisms within hives, societies, and other groups","The free rider problem says that within the bravest army, the few cowards who hang back are the most likely of all to survive the fight, go home alive, and become fathers","Darwin's probable steps toward team playing were the social instincts, reciprocity, passionate concern with the praise and blame of our fellow-men, and the capacity to treat duties and principles as sacred","Darwin summarized morality as a highly complex sentiment originating in the social instincts, largely guided by the approbation of our fellow-men, ruled by reason, self-interest, and in later times deep religious feelings, and confirmed by instruction and habit","In Adaptation and Natural Selection (1966), George Williams argued that a fast herd of deer is nothing more than a herd of fast deer - the selection process operated on the level of individuals, not the herd","Williams conceded that only by a theory of between-group selection could we achieve a scientific explanation of group-related adaptations","By the late 1970s there was a strong consensus that anyone who said a behavior occurred for the good of the group was a fool who could be safely ignored","Williams called morality an accidental capability produced, in its boundless stupidity, by a biological process normally opposed to its expression, while Haidt answers that human beings are the giraffes of altruism - one-of-a-kind freaks of nature occasionally as team-spirited as bees"],"difficulty_level":"Beginner"},{"asset_type":"DYNAMIC_DIALOGUE_SIM","domain":"Moral Psychology | Intuition vs Reason | Political Division","topic":"Dialogue: The Retrial of Group Selection","visual_frame_description":"A wood-paneled courtroom where an exhibit table holds the rowboat race of life: a lone rower, a two-person boat, a train of three boats crewed by identical septuplets, and a motorboat of twenty-four sisters, all frozen mid-stroke before the bench.","player_mission":"Take the stand at the retrial of group selection and play the witnesses in turn: Darwin, who described the tribe that conquers other tribes; George Williams, who set out to purge biology of Panglossian mushiness; Richard Dawkins, who taught generosity because we are born selfish; Michael Tomasello, who never saw two chimps carry a log; and Lesley Newson, who insisted that groups competed to turn resources into offspring rather than to make war. Question each about the free rider problem, then render your verdict on the banished heresy.","key_concepts":["The boat race is a metaphorical history of life on Earth: a lone rower is passed by a boat with two rowers, then by a train of three rowboats tied together, then by an enterprising group of twenty-four sisters who rented a motorboat","Around 2 billion years ago two bacteria somehow joined together inside a single membrane, which is why mitochondria have their own DNA, unrelated to the DNA in the nucleus","The biologists John Maynard Smith and Eors Szathmary count just eight clear major transitions over the last 4 billion years, the last of which is human societies","A superorganism is an organism made out of smaller organisms, and competition among superorganisms is one form of group selection","More than 100 million years ago some wasps developed the trick of a queen who lays all the eggs plus workers who maintain the nest, a trick discovered independently by the ancestors of termites, naked mole rats, shrimp, aphids, beetles, and spiders","The colonial insects represent just 2 percent of all insect species, yet they are now the majority, by weight, of all insects on Earth","Bert Holldobler and E. O. Wilson find that the resource for nonhuman ultrasocials is invariably a nest plus dependable food within foraging range, and beehives have been described as a factory inside a fortress","The three factors that pulled for human ultrasociality were territorial creatures fond of defensible nests, needy offspring requiring enormous care, and threat from neighboring groups - making humans the only ultrasocial primate","Tomasello's sentence that it is inconceivable you would ever see two chimpanzees carrying a log together convinced Haidt that shared intentionality was the Rubicon crossing of moral evolution","Across ten tasks, chimps and two-year-olds solved the physical problems equally well, about 68 percent of the time, but children aced the social challenges 74 percent of the time while chimps solved them just 35 percent of the time","When everyone in a group shared a common understanding of how things were supposed to be done and felt a flash of negativity when anyone violated those expectations, the first moral matrix was born","Tomasello notes that a word is not a relationship between a sound and an object but an agreement among people who share a joint representation of the things in their world and a set of conventions for communicating about them"],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate"},{"asset_type":"DECEPTION_AUDIT_FILE","domain":"Moral Psychology | Intuition vs Reason | Political Division","topic":"Audit: The Four Exhibits Dossier","visual_frame_description":"A clerk's office stacked with four brown dossier binders labeled A, B, C, and D; artifacts fly in to be filed while a timeline along the wall runs from the first bacteria to Facebook linking half a billion people, and an alarm box reads: In case of foreign attack, break glass and push button.","player_mission":"File every piece of evidence into the four exhibits of Haidt's defense: Exhibit A, major transitions producing superorganisms; Exhibit B, shared intentionality generating moral matrices; Exhibit C, genes and cultures coevolving; and Exhibit D, evolution that can be fast. For each artifact - the hand axe, the lactase gene, the tamed fox, the healed chicken - record the selection level that produced it before the timeline closes.","key_concepts":["Acheulean hand axes are nearly identical everywhere, from Africa to Europe to Asia, for more than a million years, which suggests the knowledge of how to make them may have become innate rather than passed on culturally","Homo heidelbergensis, the ancestor of Neanderthals as well as us, left the first clear evidence of hearths and of spears and is Haidt's best candidate for the Rubicon crosser","Among the first cattle keepers of northern Europe and parts of Africa, any individual whose mutated genes delayed the shutdown of lactase production had an advantage, and groups with the new gene kept larger herds and found more ways to use milk, such as turning it into cheese","Richerson and Boyd's tribal instincts hypothesis holds that gene-culture coevolution moved humanity from the small-group sociability of other primates to the tribal ultrasociality found today in all human societies","Human beings take extraordinary, costly, and sometimes painful steps to make their bodies advertise group membership, from Amazonian tattoos and the male circumcision required of Jews to the tattoos and facial piercings of punks in the United Kingdom","Early humans domesticated themselves by selecting friends and partners who could live within the tribe's moral matrix, and our brains, bodies, and behavior show domesticated signs: smaller teeth, smaller body, reduced aggression, and greater playfulness carried into adulthood","Stephen Jay Gould asserted in a 2000 interview that natural selection has almost become irrelevant in human evolution and that there has been no biological change in humans in 40,000 or 50,000 years","Dmitri Belyaev, demoted in 1948 for his belief in Mendelian genetics, bred foxes for tameness rather than pelt quality, and after nine generations novel traits began to appear - largely the ones that distinguish dogs from wolves","William Muir used group selection on hens kept twelve to a cage: within three generations aggression levels plummeted, by the sixth generation the death rate fell from 67 percent to 8 percent, and eggs per hen jumped from 91 to 237","Data from the Human Genome Project show that genetic evolution greatly accelerated during the last 50,000 years, the curve getting steeper after 20,000 years ago and reaching a crescendo during the Holocene era in Africa as well as Eurasia","Genes for recent traits, such as those that changed the blood of Tibetans so they could live at high altitudes, have already been identified","The climate in Africa fluctuated wildly between 70,000 and 140,000 years ago, and a catastrophic volcanic eruption from the Toba volcano in Indonesia occurred 74,000 years ago"],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate"},{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Moral Psychology | Intuition vs Reason | Political Division","topic":"Discrimination Matrix: Chimp or Bee, Self or Team","visual_frame_description":"A sorting chamber with twin scales, one stamped with a chimp and one with a bee; between them a glowing banner reads 'We are 90 percent chimp and 10 percent bee,' and each sorted card drops onto its level of the hierarchy of life.","player_mission":"Sort a wall of behaviors and scenes into the two natures Haidt describes: the chimp-like pursuit of individual advantage and the bee-like promotion of the group. Weigh each case - the lone forager's fig, the warrior's self-sacrifice, the group-selected hen, the reputation hunter - and justify every call by naming the selection level that shaped it before the scenes multiply.","key_concepts":["Haidt's thought experiment asks: if 95 percent of the food on Earth disappeared tonight, would the survivors be the biggest, strongest, and most violent individuals, or the people who manage to work together to monopolize, hide, and share the remaining food?","Lesley Newson warned that groups were not necessarily at war: they competed to be the most efficient at turning resources into offspring, and women and children were very important members of these groups","Group selection pulls for cooperation and for the ability to suppress antisocial behavior, but groupishness is generally focused on improving the welfare of the in-group, not on harming an out-group","Darwin wrote that selfish and contentious people will not cohere, and without coherence nothing can be effected","In a real army that sacralizes honor, loyalty, and country, the coward is not the most likely to make it home and father children - he risks being beaten up, left behind, or shot in the back for committing sacrilege, and his reputation will repel women and employers","The term me decade was first applied to the growing individualism of American society, and it described broad changes in the social sciences, where the idea of people as Homo economicus spread far and wide","Equity theory, the leading explanation of fairness in social psychology, rested on the first axiom that individuals will try to maximize their outcomes","Dawkins wrote, let us try to teach generosity and altruism because we are born selfish","People who devote their lives to helping strangers are so rare that we send film crews out to record them for the evening news, yet human ability to work together, divide labor, and function as a team is so all-pervasive we do not even notice it","The 9/11 attacks turned Haidt into a team player and produced what he calls the rally-round-the-flag reflex, exactly the sort of groupish mechanism you would expect if humans were shaped by group selection as Darwin described","By Williams's own criterion, if people in every society readily organize themselves into cooperative groups with a clear division of labor, then this ability is an excellent candidate for being a group-related adaptation","Most of human nature was shaped by natural selection operating at the level of the individual, but not all of it - the groupish overlay makes up a crucial part of our righteous minds"],"difficulty_level":"Advanced"},{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Moral Psychology | Intuition vs Reason | Political Division","topic":"Boss Battle: The Final Verdict on the Tribe","visual_frame_description":"A vaulted hall where Darwin's two warring tribes flicker on a screen above the bench; as each exhibit is entered, the chamber fills with the symbols of the transition - the beehive, the hand axe, the hearth, the city-state raising walls and armies.","player_mission":"Stand before the tribunal that banished group selection in the 1970s and deliver the closing argument: walk the bench through all four exhibits, from the bacterial merger and the hymenopteran revolution to the moral matrix born of shared intentionality, the lactase gene, the tamed foxes, and the hens' falling death rate. Convince the court that the wholesale dismissal was premature before the gavel falls on human groupishness itself.","key_concepts":["Exhibit A: major transitions produce superorganisms - when the free rider problem is muted at one level of the biological hierarchy, large and cohesive units become the vehicle for selection at the next level up","Exhibit B: shared intentionality generates moral matrices - the Rubicon crossing was the uniquely human ability to share intentions and other mental representations, the beginning of the moral matrices that govern our social lives today","Exhibit C: genes and cultures coevolve - once our ancestors crossed the Rubicon, evolution became a two-stranded affair, giving us tribal instincts to mark group membership and cooperate preferentially with group members","Exhibit D: evolution can be fast - gene-culture coevolution reached a fever pitch during the last 12,000 years, so we cannot assume that modern-day hunter-gatherers represent universal human nature locked into place 50,000 years ago","Even if group selection played no role in the evolution of any other mammal, human evolution has been so different since shared intentionality and gene-culture coevolution that humans may well be a special case","Bees, ants, and termites are the ultimate team players - one for all, all for one - because in their hives competition within the group has been nearly eliminated and almost all selection is group selection","Natural selection favored increasing levels of Tomasello's group-mindedness: the ability to learn and conform to social norms, feel and share group-related emotions, and create and obey social institutions, including religion","Humans construct moral communities out of shared norms, institutions, and gods that they fight, kill, and die to defend, just as bees construct hives out of wax and wood fibers","Shared intentionality allowed humans to construct nests that were vast and ornate yet weightless and portable","The coevolution of tribal minds and tribal cultures prepared us not just for war but for far more peaceful coexistence within our groups and, in modern times, for cooperation on a vast scale","Haidt concludes that we have a dual nature - selfish primates who long to be a part of something larger and nobler than ourselves: we are 90 percent chimp and 10 percent bee","The wholesale dismissal of group selection in the 1960s and 1970s, based mostly on arguments and examples from other species, was premature"],"difficulty_level":"Advanced"}]},{"id":"rm10","title":"Chapter 10: The Hive Switch","pages":"221-245","assets":[{"asset_type":"CUE_SCRUBBER_STATION","domain":"Moral Psychology | Intuition vs Reason | Political Division","topic":"Cue Scrubber: The Switch Flipper","visual_frame_description":"A diagnostic arch over a darkened hall where every scene glows with a slider labeled Hive Switch; when the crowd moves as one, a golden current - a sort of electricity generated from their closeness - arcs between the figures.","player_mission":"Scan a montage of collective scenes frozen mid-transition - the drill field at morning march, the fire-lit dance, the rave's strobed crowd, the stadium swaying in unison - and judge each one: has the hive switch been flipped, and is this muscular bonding, collective effervescence, or awe? Ring the instant the self shuts down and the gauge hits 'simply a part of a whole.'","key_concepts":["William McNeill, drafted into the U.S. Army in September 1941, found that prolonged movement in unison during drilling produced a strange sense of personal enlargement, a sort of swelling out, becoming bigger than life","McNeill hypothesized that muscular bonding - moving together in time - evolved long before the beginning of recorded history for shutting down the self and creating a temporary superorganism","McNeill concluded that the key innovation of Greek, Roman, and later European armies was synchronous drilling and marching, and the Macedonian phalanx was the superorganism Alexander the Great used to defeat much larger armies","McNeill studied accounts of men in battle and found that men risk their lives not so much for their country or their ideals as for their comrades-in-arms","One veteran quoted by McNeill said that when their 'I' passes insensibly into a 'we,' self-sacrifice becomes easy, for 'I may fall, but I do not die, for that which is real in me goes forward and lives on in the comrades for whom I gave up my life'","The hive hypothesis states that human beings are conditional hive creatures who have the ability, under special conditions, to transcend self-interest and lose themselves temporarily and ecstatically in something larger than themselves","The hive switch is a group-related adaptation that can only be explained by a theory of between-group selection, as Williams said - it is an adaptation for making groups more cohesive","Barbara Ehrenreich's Dancing in the Streets argues that collective and ecstatic dancing is a nearly universal biotechnology for binding groups together and a form of muscular bonding that fosters love, trust, and equality","Early Christianity was a danced religion until dancing in church was suppressed in the Middle Ages, and Ehrenreich traces Europe's abandonment of ecstatic dancing to the rise of individualism beginning in the sixteenth century","Ehrenreich noted that if homosexual attraction is the love that dares not speak its name, the love that binds people to the collective has no name at all to speak","Durkheim insisted there were social facts - such as the suicide rate or norms about patriotism - that are not reducible to facts about individuals, and he described Homo sapiens as Homo duplex, existing as an individual and as part of the larger society","Durkheim's second set of social sentiments makes the person 'simply a part of a whole, whose actions I follow, and whose influence I am subject to'"],"difficulty_level":"Beginner"},{"asset_type":"DYNAMIC_DIALOGUE_SIM","domain":"Moral Psychology | Intuition vs Reason | Political Division","topic":"Dialogue: The Effervescence Witness Stand","visual_frame_description":"A dim witness room where each speaker's face floats above a scene: a transparent eye-ball dissolving into the sky, a forest of grandeur, a blinding altar light, and a crowd of thousands facing an elevated DJ while wordless electronic beats pulse beneath them all.","player_mission":"Interview the witnesses who crossed into the sacred realm and described it: Ralph Waldo Emerson on the bare ground, Darwin in the Brazilian forest, the Boston divinity student caught up in the vastness of Creation, and Tony Hsieh surrendering his 'I' on the rave floor. Then take the stand yourself and testify whether awe, drugs, or drumbeats flipped your switch - or whether you only ever saw the profane world.","key_concepts":["Emerson's Transcendentalism rejected the analytic hyperintellectualism of America's top universities and argued that the deepest truths must be known by intuition, not reason","Emerson described standing on the bare ground, head bathed by the blithe air: 'I become a transparent eye-ball; I am nothing; I see all; the currents of the Universal Being circulate through me; I am part or particle of God'","Darwin recorded in his journal that standing in the midst of the grandeur of a Brazilian forest, it was not possible to give an adequate idea of the higher feelings of wonder, admiration, and devotion which fill and elevate the mind","The emotion of awe is most often triggered by situations with two features: vastness, which overwhelms us and makes us feel small, and a need for accommodation, when the experience cannot be assimilated into our existing mental structures","Awe acts like a kind of reset button - it makes people forget themselves and their petty concerns and opens them to new possibilities, values, and directions in life","When Cortes captured Mexico in 1519 he found the Aztecs using mushrooms containing psilocybin called teonanacatl, literally 'God's flesh,' which took people directly from the profane to the sacred realm in about thirty minutes","Religious practices north of the Aztecs focused on peyote, a cactus containing mescaline, while practices to the south used ayahuasca, a brew of vines and leaves containing DMT - drugs that could just as well be called Durkheimogens","Traditional rites for transforming boys into men and girls into women involve fear, pain, symbolism of death and rebirth, and many societies used hallucinogenic drugs to catalyze the transformation of the selfish child into a morally responsible adult","In a controlled experiment in the basement chapel of a Boston church, twenty divinity students took either psilocybin or niacin, and within forty minutes it was clear to all who had taken which","The psilocybin subjects reported statistically significant effects on nine kinds of experiences, including unity with a loss of sense of self, transcendence of time and space, sacredness, and persisting positive changes in attitude and behavior","Twenty-five years later Rick Doblin tracked down nineteen of the twenty original subjects and concluded that the psilocybin experiences were among the most meaningful of their lives","Tony Hsieh of Zappos.com described the rave as one massive, united tribe of thousands with the DJ as tribal leader, where the steady wordless electronic beats were the unifying heartbeats that synchronized the crowd and individual consciousness was replaced by a single unifying group consciousness"],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate"},{"asset_type":"DECEPTION_AUDIT_FILE","domain":"Moral Psychology | Intuition vs Reason | Political Division","topic":"Audit: The Neurochemistry Ledger","visual_frame_description":"A laboratory ledger with two columns, Oxytocin and Mirror Neurons; beside them a warning gauge labeled Parochial Altruism ticks upward with every entry while a banner asks whether putting oxytocin into the world's drinking water would end war and cruelty - and answers: unfortunately, no.","player_mission":"Audit the biology of the hive switch and file each finding under its building material: oxytocin, the glue of within-group bonding, and the mirror neuron system, the machinery of shared feeling. For every entry, record whether it bonds us to our group or to humanity in general, and flag any study that shows parochial altruism rather than universal love.","key_concepts":["Oxytocin is a hormone and neurotransmitter produced by the hypothalamus that causes uterine contractions and milk letdown, and evolution has often reused it to forge other kinds of bonds, such as between mates and between parents and offspring","The press has dubbed oxytocin the love drug and the cuddle hormone because your brain secretes more of it with intimate contact, even if that contact is just a back rub from a stranger","If the hive switch is a product of group selection, it should show the signature feature of group selection: parochial altruism - bonding us to our partners and groups, not to humanity in general","In studies of Dutch men playing economic games in cubicles, those given a nasal spray of oxytocin made less selfish decisions and cared more about helping their group but showed no concern at all for improving the outcomes of men in the other groups","In one of the Dutch studies, oxytocin made men more willing to hurt other teams in a prisoner's dilemma game because doing so was the best way to protect their own group","In follow-up studies, oxytocin caused Dutch men to like Dutch names more and to value saving Dutch lives more in trolley-type dilemmas, yet researchers failed to find increased out-group hate toward Muslims","Mirror neurons were discovered accidentally in the 1980s when Italian scientists inserting electrodes into the brains of Macaque monkeys heard firing noises at odd times - such as when a still monkey watched the researcher pick something up with his thumb and forefinger","Most mirror neurons fire not when they see a specific physical movement but when they see an action indicating a more general goal or intention - watching a hand lift a cup from a clean table triggers an eating neuron, while the same movement from a messy table triggers a different one","Just seeing someone else smile activates some of the same neurons as when you smile, so the other person is effectively smiling in your brain, which makes you happy and likely to smile back","In Tania Singer's study, subjects' brains responded the same way when a nice player received an electric shock as when they themselves were shocked, but when the selfish player was shocked people showed less empathy and some even showed neural evidence of pleasure","We are conditional hive creatures: we are more likely to mirror and empathize with others when they have conformed to our moral matrix than when they have violated it","Mirror neurons send signals from motor areas first to the insular cortex and from there to the amygdala and other limbic areas, letting people feel each other's pain and joy to a much greater degree than any other primates"],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate"},{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Moral Psychology | Intuition vs Reason | Political Division","topic":"Discrimination Matrix: The Glauconian or the Hivish Firm","visual_frame_description":"A corporate boardroom split down the middle: on one side a flowchart of incentives under surveillance cameras, on the other a floor of employees exercising in synchrony; a single clapping hand hovers above both, captioned: leadership is the complement of followership.","player_mission":"Sort each organizational practice into the nature it appeals to: the transactional firm staffed by Homo economicus and monitored with carrots and sticks, versus the hivish organization that builds similarity, synchrony, and team competition. Justify every placement with the research - the bonus pool that destroys morale, the Toyota morning exercises, the All Blacks Haka - before the org chart multiplies.","key_concepts":["The word corporation comes from corpus, Latin for body, and legally a corporation is a collection of many individuals united into one body, having perpetual succession under an artificial form and the capacity of acting, in several respects, as an individual","The corporate legal fiction let people place themselves in a new kind of boat within which they could divide labor, suppress free riding, and take on gigantic tasks, and corporate law helped England pull ahead in the early days of the industrial revolution","It is possible to build a corporation staffed entirely by Homo economicus, but this approach, sometimes called transactional leadership, has its limits because self-interested employees are Glauconians, far more interested in looking good and getting promoted than in helping the company","Hivish employees work harder, have more fun, and are less likely to quit or to sue the company, unlike Homo economicus, who is no team player","Robert Hogan, Robert Kaiser, and Mark van Vugt argue that leadership can only be understood as the complement of followership - focusing on leadership alone is like trying to understand clapping by studying only the left hand","People evolved to live in groups of up to 150 that were relatively egalitarian and wary of alpha males, yet we also evolved to rally around leaders when our group is under threat or competing with other groups","Research shows that strangers will spontaneously organize themselves into leaders and followers when natural disasters strike","A leader must construct a moral matrix based on the Authority foundation, the Liberty foundation, and above all the Loyalty foundation, without activating followers' hypersensitive oppression detectors","To make a human hive, increase similarity, not diversity: people are warmer and more trusting toward those who look like them, dress like them, or even just share their first name or birthday, and there is nothing special about race","Synchrony builds trust - Japanese corporations such as Toyota begin their days with synchronous companywide exercises, groups prepare for battle with chants and ritualized movements, and if you want an impressive rugby example, Google 'All Blacks Haka'","Studies show that intergroup competition increases love of the in-group far more than it increases dislike of the out-group, while pitting individuals against each other for scarce bonuses destroys hivishness, trust, and morale","Kaiser and Hogan summarize the literature: transactional leadership appeals to followers' self-interest, but transformational leadership changes the way followers see themselves - from isolated individuals to members of a larger group - by modeling collective commitment through self-sacrifice and the use of 'we' rather than 'I'"],"difficulty_level":"Advanced"},{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Moral Psychology | Intuition vs Reason | Political Division","topic":"Boss Battle: The Fascism Line and the Hivish Nation","visual_frame_description":"A grand hall where one banner reads 'Ask not what your country can do for you' and a second, in heavy black type, preaches renunciation of self-interest for a purely spiritual existence; below, two glass nations stand side by side, one swarming with little glowing hives and one flat and dark.","player_mission":"Face the charge that hiving is fascism: hold the two texts in your hands - Kennedy's inaugural address and Mussolini's Doctrine of Fascism - and tell the court where the line falls between the festival that mocks its leaders and the spectacle that worships them. Then govern the thought experiment of two nations, one full of cross-cutting hives and one devoid of them, and argue why the hivish nation is not a promising target for a demagogue.","key_concepts":["For Americans born before 1950, the words 'Ask not' activate their Durkheimian higher nature through John F. Kennedy's 1961 inaugural address, which called on Americans to bear the burden of a long twilight struggle against the Soviet Union","Mussolini's The Doctrine of Fascism rejects man as a self-centered individual and builds a higher life founded on duty in which the individual, by self-sacrifice and the renunciation of self-interest, achieves a purely spiritual existence - fascism is hive psychology scaled up to grotesque heights","Ehrenreich refutes the fascism charge by noting that ecstatic dancing, festivals, and carnivals invariably erase or invert the hierarchies of everyday life: men dress as women, peasants pretend to be nobles, and leaders can be safely mocked","Fascist rallies were spectacles, not festivals: people stood around passively for hours, applauding soldiers and cheering the leader, and the rallies used awe to strengthen hierarchy and bond people to the godlike figure of the leader","Hiving comes naturally, easily, and joyfully to us, and its normal function is to bond dozens or at most hundreds of people into communities of trust, cooperation, and even love","Creating a nation of multiple competing groups and parties was seen by America's founding fathers as a way of preventing tyranny","Robert Putnam's research on social capital shows that bowling leagues, churches, and other kinds of groups, teams, and clubs are crucial for the health of individuals and of a nation","Haidt changed his mind since The Happiness Hypothesis: happiness comes from between - from getting the right relationships between yourself and others, yourself and your work, and yourself and something larger than yourself","The hive switch is another way of stating Durkheim's idea that we are Homo duplex: we live most of our lives in the ordinary profane world but achieve our greatest joys in brief moments of transit to the sacred world, in which we become simply a part of a whole","The hive switch is made of oxytocin, which bonds people to their groups rather than to all of humanity, and mirror neurons, which help people empathize particularly with those who share their moral matrix","When a single hive is scaled up to the size of a nation and led by a dictator with an army at his disposal, the results are invariably disastrous - but that is no argument for removing or suppressing hives at lower levels","Parochial love - love within groups, amplified by similarity, a sense of shared fate, and the suppression of free riders - may be the most we can accomplish"],"difficulty_level":"Advanced"}]},{"id":"rm11","title":"Chapter 11: Religion Is a Team Sport","pages":"246-273","assets":[{"asset_type":"CUE_SCRUBBER_STATION","domain":"Moral Psychology | Intuition vs Reason | Political Division","topic":"Cue Scrubber: The Sacred Things Scanner","visual_frame_description":"A stadium transformed into a scanner bay where 50,000 fans glow in a single field; as arms lock and sway, the overlay traces the century-old song and labels each chant 'muscular bonding warming up for coordinated military action.'","player_mission":"Scan the game-day rituals at UVA and detect the sacred machinery inside each gesture: the neckties and pearls, the painted Cavaliers logo, the locked arms swaying to 'Wah-hoo-wah,' the fist-pumping battle chant. Judge each behavior against Durkheim's definition of religion and stamp which ones create a community before the scanner seals the frame.","key_concepts":["Every Saturday in the fall, millions of people pack themselves into stadiums at colleges across the United States to participate in a ritual that can only be described as tribal","At UVA the ritual begins with costumes: men wear dress shirts with UVA neckties, women wear skirts or dresses sometimes with pearl necklaces, and some students paint the logo of the Cavaliers - a V crossed by two swords - on their faces","The students sing the same song UVA students have sung together for over a century, to the tune of 'Auld Lang Syne,' beginning 'That good old song of Wah-hoo-wah'","After locking arms and swaying as a single mass, the students illustrate McNeill's thesis that muscular bonding warms people up for coordinated military action, with aggressive fist-pumping in sync with a nonsensical battle chant","Collective effervescence is guaranteed at the game, along with collective outrage at questionable calls by the referees, collective triumph if the team wins, and collective grief if the team loses","From a Durkheimian perspective the function of the cheering and chanting is not victory but the creation of a community - a college football game is a superb analogy for religion","From a naive perspective college football is an extravagant, costly, wasteful institution that impairs rational thinking, but from a sociologically informed perspective it pulls people up from the profane level to the sacred level and flips the hive switch","The school spirit it augments attracts better students and more alumni donations, which improves the experience for the entire community, including professors with no interest in sports","Durkheim defined a religion as a unified system of beliefs and practices relative to sacred things, things set apart and forbidden, which unite into one single moral community called a Church all those who adhere to them","Religion cannot be studied in lone individuals any more than hivishness can be studied in lone bees - religions are social facts","Many scientists misunderstand religion because they examine only what is most visible - individuals and their supernatural beliefs - and conclude it is an extravagant, costly, wasteful institution that leaves a long trail of victims","The third principle of moral psychology, that morality binds and blinds, must be honored before rendering a fair judgment about religion and its relationship to morality and politics"],"difficulty_level":"Beginner"},{"asset_type":"DYNAMIC_DIALOGUE_SIM","domain":"Moral Psychology | Intuition vs Reason | Political Division","topic":"Dialogue: The Four Horsemen Debate","visual_frame_description":"A panel room where three books float above the table - The End of Faith, The God Delusion, Breaking the Spell - while behind them a football arcs across a stadium, and a caption reads: understanding religion by studying beliefs about God is like understanding football by studying the movements of the ball.","player_mission":"Take the interviewer's seat at a debate among the three men of science - Harris, Dawkins, and Dennett - and the Durkheimian challenger. Ask each what religion is: a set of beliefs that cause harmful deeds, or believing, doing, and belonging woven into a community? Then switch seats and answer as a believer whose gods bind a village together, and defend the model that the beliefs exist to serve.","key_concepts":["Between 2004 and 2007 so many books attacking religion were published that a movement was born: the New Atheism, whose four authors are known as the four horsemen","The titles were combative: Sam Harris's The End of Faith, Richard Dawkins's The God Delusion, Daniel Dennett's Breaking the Spell, and Christopher Hitchens's God Is Not Great: How Religion Poisons Everything","Haidt sets Hitchens aside because his book made no pretense to be anything other than a polemical diatribe, while Harris was a graduate student in neuroscience, Dawkins is a biologist, and Dennett is a philosopher","For Harris, beliefs are the key to the psychology of religion because believing a falsehood - such as martyrs being rewarded with seventy-two virgins in heaven - makes religious people do harmful things such as suicide bombing","Dawkins defines the God Hypothesis as the proposition that there exists a superhuman, supernatural intelligence who deliberately designed and created the universe and everything in it, including us","Dawkins argues that God, in the sense defined, is a delusion - and, as later chapters will show, a pernicious delusion","Trying to understand the persistence and passion of religion by studying beliefs about God is like trying to understand the persistence and passion of college football by studying the movements of the ball","Believing, doing, and belonging are three complementary yet distinct aspects of religiosity according to many scholars, and looking at all three at once gives a view very different from the New Atheists'","The Durkheimian model holds that the function of those beliefs and practices is ultimately to create a community, and that our beliefs are often post hoc constructions designed to justify what we've just done or support the groups we belong to","The New Atheist model is based on the Platonic rationalist view that reason is the charioteer guiding the passions, the view Haidt rejected in favor of the Humean account in which reason, the rider, is a servant of the intuitions, the elephant","To understand the psychology of religion, the question is whether to focus on the false beliefs and faulty reasoning of individual believers or on the automatic intuitive processes of people embedded in social groups striving to create a moral community","Supernatural agents play a central role in religion, just as the actual football is at the center of the whirl of activity on game day at UVA, but you've got to broaden the inquiry to the ways beliefs work with practices to create a religious community"],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate"},{"asset_type":"DECEPTION_AUDIT_FILE","domain":"Moral Psychology | Intuition vs Reason | Political Division","topic":"Audit: The By-Product Debrief","visual_frame_description":"A pathology lab where slides of mental machinery are projected, each misfiring into a god-shaped glow; on one bench a parasite-controlled ant climbs a blade of grass while the caption reads 'suicide for the ant, adaptive for the parasite.'","player_mission":"Work the audit trail of the New Atheist story and file each mechanism under its misfire: the hypersensitive agency detector that sees agents in the wind, the gullible learning module that believes whatever grown-ups say, the love circuitry commandeered for God, and the dualist mind that parks immortal souls in temporary bodies. Then close the file on the parasites - the ant at the blade of grass, the virus that makes you sneeze.","key_concepts":["To an evolutionist, religious behaviors stand out like peacocks in a sunlit glade, as Dennett put it, because evolution ruthlessly eliminates costly and wasteful behaviors from an animal's repertoire","Dawkins notes that no known culture lacks some version of the time-consuming, wealth-consuming, hostility-provoking rituals, the anti-factual, counterproductive fantasies of religion","The first step of the New Atheist story - one Haidt does not challenge - is the hypersensitive agency detection device, which makes almost all of its mistakes in one direction: false positives rather than false negatives","Just as we see faces in the clouds but never clouds in faces, the face detector is on a hair trigger, and similarly the agency detector makes thunder and lightning seem as though somebody up in the sky is angry at us","The bridge of the nose is a mundane by-product example: it evolved for other reasons, but we humans reuse it for the entirely new purpose of holding up eyeglasses","Dawkins proposes a gullible learning module: there will be a selective advantage to child brains that possess the rule of thumb to believe, without question, whatever your grown-ups tell you","Dennett suggests that the circuitry for falling in love has been commandeered by some religions to make people fall in love with God","Paul Bloom has shown that our minds were designed for dualism - we think minds and bodies are different but equally real sorts of things - and so we readily believe we have immortal souls housed in our temporary bodies","Dennett opens Breaking the Spell with a tiny parasite that commandeers the brains of ants, making them climb to the tops of blades of grass where grazing animals can eat them - suicide for the ant but adaptive for the parasite","Dawkins describes religions as viruses that spread from host to host, and both he and Dennett treat religions as sets of memes that have undergone Darwinian selection for their own ability to survive and reproduce","Dennett describes the nymphs, fairies, goblins, and demons of mythology as the imaginative offspring of a hyperactive habit of finding agency, with only the souped-up winners of billions of competitions for rehearsal time surviving to be shared","If religion is a virus or parasite that exploits a set of cognitive by-products for its benefit, then scientists, humanists, and the few who have escaped infection must work together to break the spell, lift the delusion, and bring about the end of faith"],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate"},{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Moral Psychology | Intuition vs Reason | Political Division","topic":"Discrimination Matrix: The Sacrifice and Sanctity Sorter","visual_frame_description":"A sorting machine fed by two columns, Memes and Moralistic Gods; each god arrives thundering against adultery and oath-breaking, while a balance scale weighs costly sacrifices against commune longevity and a graph rises perfectly linearly for the religious communes.","player_mission":"Sort a stream of evidence into the two evolutionary stories: the New Atheists' parasitic memes and Atran and Henrich's cultural group selection. Weigh each artifact - the cheating lab lit dimly, the image of the eye, the jealous gods, the communes' costly sacrifices - and decide what actually binds groups together before the ledger closes.","key_concepts":["Atran and Henrich tell a two-step story: diverse cognitive modules evolved as adaptations but misfired, producing supernatural beliefs as by-products, followed by cultural evolution in which religions spread to the extent that they make groups more cohesive and cooperative","The gods of hunter-gatherers are often capricious and malevolent, but as groups take up agriculture and grow larger, their gods become far more moralistic, concerned about actions that foment conflict and division such as murder, adultery, false witness, and the breaking of oaths","People cheat more on a test when the lights are dimmed, but they cheat less when a cartoonlike image of an eye is nearby or when the concept of God is activated in memory by unscrambling sentences containing God-related words","Gods who administer collective punishment - bringing drought or pestilence on the whole village for the adultery of two people - make shame more effective as a means of social control and keep villagers gossipy about extramarital liaisons","Richard Sosis compared nineteenth-century communes and found that just 6 percent of secular communes were still functioning twenty years after their founding, compared to 39 percent of the religious communes","The master variable explaining commune longevity was the number of costly sacrifices each commune demanded: giving up alcohol and tobacco, fasting for days at a time, conforming to a dress code or hairstyle, or cutting ties with outsiders","For religious communes the effect was perfectly linear - the more sacrifice a commune demanded, the longer it lasted - but demands for sacrifice did not help secular communes, most of which failed within eight years","Sosis quotes Roy Rappaport: to invest social conventions with sanctity is to hide their arbitrariness in a cloak of seeming necessity","When secular organizations demand sacrifice, every member has a right to ask for a cost-benefit analysis, and many refuse to do things that don't make logical sense","Religions can spread far faster than genes, as in the case of Islam in the seventh and eighth centuries or Mormonism in the nineteenth century, and a successful religion can be adopted by neighboring or vanquished populations","Atran and Henrich argue that moralistic high gods are just too recent to have driven genetic evolution for religiosity, having emerged along with agriculture in the last 10,000 years","Haidt finds it hard to imagine that the genes stood still for more than 50,000 years in the swirling waltz of gene-culture coevolution while the cultural partner began dancing to religious music"],"difficulty_level":"Advanced"},{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Moral Psychology | Intuition vs Reason | Political Division","topic":"Boss Battle: The Water Temple Summit","visual_frame_description":"A mountainside of terraced pools under the immense temple of the Goddess of the Waters, where twenty-four priests file past and a high priest speaks for the goddess; below, a maypole's ribbons weave a tubular cloth while a banner reads 'Religions exist primarily for people to achieve together what they cannot achieve on their own.'","player_mission":"Take command of Wilson's case: walk the river from the Balinese volcano's Goddess of the Waters down through the subaks and the temple at every fork, and keep the rice flowing without central government, inspectors, or courts. Then rule on the two great questions - do the gods make people good, and can those who forsake the moral exoskeleton escape anomie? - before the maypole weaves its final ribbon.","key_concepts":["David Sloan Wilson of Binghamton University watched group selection convicted in the 1970s, spent thirty years trying to prove it innocent, and merged the ideas of Darwin and Durkheim in Darwin's Cathedral","Wilson shows how John Calvin developed a strict and demanding form of Christianity in sixteenth-century Geneva that suppressed free riding and facilitated trust and commerce, and how medieval Judaism created cultural fortresses that kept outsiders out and insiders in","On Bali, hundreds of terraced pools are irrigated by aqueducts and tunnels, some running underground for more than a kilometer, overseen by an immense temple to the Goddess of the Waters staffed by twenty-four full-time priests selected in childhood","The lowest level of Balinese organization was the subak, a group of several extended families that made decisions democratically, and a small temple was placed at every fork in the irrigation system to unite all the subaks downstream of it","The temple system minimized the cheating that would flourish in a zero-sum division of water and let thousands of farmers cooperate without central government, inspectors, and courts - so efficiently that the Dutch, expert hydrologists, could find little to improve","In the maypole dance, men and women circle the pole in opposite directions weaving their ribbons into a tubular cloth, symbolically enacting the central miracle of social life: e pluribus unum","The !Kung believe in an omnipotent sky god named //Gauwa and spirits of the dead called //gauwasi who offer no moral guidance but simply cause things to happen - perfect examples of the hypersensitive agency detector in action","Lorna Marshall describes the !Kung healing dances as people binding together to become a unit, singing, clapping, and stamping in an extraordinary unison, with no words dividing them","Nicholas Wade's The Faith Instinct endorses Wilson's theory: people belonging to a religiously cohesive society are more likely to survive and reproduce than those in less cohesive groups, who may be vanquished by their enemies","Gods and religions, in sum, are group-level adaptations for producing cohesiveness and trust, created by the members of the group and then organizing the activity of the group","In the United States, the least religious fifth of the population gives just 1.5 percent of its money to charity, while the most religious fifth - measured by church attendance, not belief - gives 7 percent, mostly to religious organizations","In a German trust game, trusters who learned a trustee was religious transferred more money, and the religious trustees really did transfer back more than the nonreligious trustees - the highest wealth was created when religious people played with other religious people"],"difficulty_level":"Advanced"}]},{"id":"rm12","title":"Chapter 12: Can't We All Disagree More Constructively?","pages":"274-303","assets":[{"asset_type":"CUE_SCRUBBER_STATION","domain":"Moral Psychology | Intuition vs Reason | Political Division","topic":"Cue Scrubber: The First Draft Scanner","visual_frame_description":"A scanning arch over a crib and then a nursery where teachers rate children curious, verbal, and self-reliant or obedient and neat; a gauge labeled Threat Sensitivity spikes with a sudden blast of white noise while twin strands of DNA glow in the corner.","player_mission":"Scan the developmental timeline of a life and detect the settings written into the first draft of the brain: threat sensitivity, novelty-seeking, openness to experience. Judge whether each scene - the nursery school report, the strict school, the blast of white noise - pushes a child toward the left or right team, and stamp the heritability verdict before the case file seals itself.","key_concepts":["Haidt defines ideology simply as a set of beliefs about the proper order of society and how it can be achieved","At the French Assembly of 1789, delegates who favored preservation sat on the right side of the chamber while those who favored change sat on the left, and the terms right and left have stood for conservatism and liberalism ever since","Political theorists since Marx assumed people chose ideologies to further their self-interest, but when political scientists looked into it they found that self-interest does a remarkably poor job of predicting political attitudes","Studies of twins in the 1980s showed that identical twins are more similar than fraternal twins on just about everything, and identical twins reared in separate households usually turn out very similar","Genetics explains between a third and a half of the variability among people on their political attitudes, while being raised in a liberal or conservative household accounts for much less","Innate does not mean unmalleable; it means organized in advance of experience, with the genes guiding the construction of the brain in the uterus as a first draft that gets revised by childhood experiences","Genes related to glutamate and serotonin, both involved in the brain's response to threat and fear, fit findings that conservatives react more strongly to signs of danger, including germs, contamination, and sudden blasts of white noise","Genes related to receptors for the neurotransmitter dopamine, tied to sensation-seeking and openness to experience, correlate with liberalism, echoing Montaigne's line that the only things he finds rewarding are variety and the enjoyment of diversity","John Jost's major review found that nearly all the traits distinguishing liberals and conservatives are conceptually related to threat sensitivity - conservatives react more strongly to reminders of death - or to openness to experience","Even though the effects of any single gene are tiny, the genes collectively give some people brains more reactive to threats and less pleased by novelty, and these are the two main personality factors that distinguish the sides","Women who called themselves liberals as adults had been rated by their nursery school teachers as more curious, verbal, and self-reliant, but also more assertive and aggressive, less obedient and neat","Genes contribute to just about every aspect of our personalities: how much you like jazz, spicy foods, and abstract art, your likelihood of divorce, your degree of religiosity, and your political orientation as an adult"],"difficulty_level":"Beginner"},{"asset_type":"DYNAMIC_DIALOGUE_SIM","domain":"Moral Psychology | Intuition vs Reason | Political Division","topic":"Dialogue: The Holiday Table Reunion","visual_frame_description":"A holiday table divided down the middle: on one side a wall of books about oppression and a poster of Obama's 2008 promise of change; on the other a bank ledger, a church pew, and a campaign button reading 'Country First'; at the head of the table an electric guitar stands unplayed.","player_mission":"Play both fraternal twins at the family table they have agreed not to talk politics over: the sister who majors in Latin American studies in New York and advocates for children of illegal immigrants, and the brother who stays close to home, works at the local bank, and hears 'Country First.' Then take the confession of Keith Richards - dumped from his school choir, made to repeat a year, his slow-burning fuse lit - and decide how much of each life was written in advance.","key_concepts":["The sister's genes gave her a brain lower than average in threat sensitivity and higher in pleasure at novel experiences, and in 2008 she is electrified by Barack Obama's concern for the poor and his promise of change","The brother, with the opposite settings, chooses to stay close to family and friends, attends the local branch of the state university, earns a business degree, works for a local bank, and becomes a pillar of his church and community","The moral matrices surrounding the brother are based on all six foundations, with the most common themes being personal responsibility - based on the Fairness foundation, not being a free rider or a burden on others - and loyalty to the many teams he belongs to","The brother resonates to John McCain's campaign slogan 'Country First,' and the twins' one point of agreement is that they must not talk about politics when the sister comes home for the holidays","On the day they were born the sister was not predestined to vote for Obama, nor was the brother guaranteed to become a Republican - their genes gave them different first drafts that led them down different paths into different moral subcultures","Dan McAdams distinguishes three levels of personality: dispositional traits, characteristic adaptations, and life narratives","Characteristic adaptations emerge as people respond to the specific environments they face, as when a strict, well-ordered school fits the brother but turns the sister angry and socially disengaged","The human mind is a story processor, not a logic processor, and life narratives are simplified, selective reconstructions of the past, often connected to an idealized vision of the future, that still influence behavior, relationships, and mental health","Analyzing narratives from liberal and conservative Christians with Moral Foundations Theory, McAdams found conservatives underscored respect for authority, allegiance to one's group, and purity of the self, while liberals emphasized human suffering and social fairness","Keith Richards, once a marginally well-behaved member of his school choir that won competitions, was dumped by the choir master when the boys' voices changed and was then informed he would have to repeat a full year in school","Richards called it a kick in the guts that made him, Spike, and Terry become terrorists against the country: 'There's them, the authorities. And a slow-burning fuse was lit'","Once Richards understood himself as a crusader against abusive authority, his life narrative fit the stories that all parties on the left tell, and there was no way he was ever going to vote for the British Conservative Party"],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate"},{"asset_type":"DECEPTION_AUDIT_FILE","domain":"Moral Psychology | Intuition vs Reason | Political Division","topic":"Audit: The Grand Narrative Files","visual_frame_description":"A file room where two narratives unroll like scrolls: one showing crowds storming the Bastille and freeing the prisoners, the other a family reclaiming its home from termites and repairing the joists; a rubber stamp reads 'Morality binds and blinds.'","player_mission":"File each claim under the grand narrative it serves: the liberal progress narrative of heroic liberation from oppression, or the Reagan narrative of defense against the federal bureaucracy. For every entry, record which moral foundations carry the moral force, and audit both sides' ability to understand the other before the empathy-asymmetry study is stamped.","key_concepts":["In Moral, Believing Animals, the sociologist Christian Smith shows how grand narratives identify and reinforce the sacred core of each moral matrix, each with a beginning, a middle in which a threat arises, and an end in which a resolution is achieved","The liberal progress narrative begins once upon a time with the vast majority of humans suffering in unjust, unhealthy, repressive societies, and ends with the struggle to dismantle the powerful vestiges of inequality, exploitation, and repression","The liberal progress narrative is a heroic liberation narrative whose moral force comes primarily from the Care/harm and Liberty foundations, with Authority mentioned only as an evil and no mention of Loyalty or Sanctity","Drew Westen's The Political Brain extracts the Reagan master narrative: once upon a time America was a shining beacon, then liberals erected an enormous federal bureaucracy that handcuffed the invisible hand of the free market","In the Reagan narrative, liberals siphoned money from hardworking Americans and gave it to Cadillac-driving drug addicts and welfare queens, tried to understand criminals instead of punishing them, and preached promiscuity instead of traditional values","The Reagan narrative is a heroism of defense - less like storming the Bastille than like a family reclaiming its home from termites and then repairing the joists","The Reagan narrative relies on at least five of the six moral foundations: a hint of Care for the victims of crime, plus clear Liberty, Fairness as proportionality, Loyalty to soldiers and the flag, Authority, and Sanctity","The obstacles to empathy are not symmetrical: since the left builds its moral matrices on a smaller number of foundations, there is no foundation used by the left that is not also used by the right","In a study with Jesse Graham and Brian Nosek, more than two thousand American visitors to YourMorals.org filled out the Moral Foundations Questionnaire as themselves, as they think a typical liberal would, or as a typical conservative would","Moderates and conservatives were most accurate in predicting the responses of typical partisans, while liberals were the least accurate - especially those who described themselves as very liberal","The biggest errors came when liberals answered the Care and Fairness questions while pretending to be conservatives, assuming conservatives would reject statements such as 'One of the worst things a person could do is hurt a defenseless animal'","The theater critic who wrote that Republicans should be exterminated before they cause more harm showed the inability of a man who enters fantastical imaginary worlds for a living to imagine that Republicans act within a moral matrix different from his own"],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate"},{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Moral Psychology | Intuition vs Reason | Political Division","topic":"Discrimination Matrix: The Sacred Value Sorter","visual_frame_description":"Three moral matrix diagrams glow side by side - a two-foundation liberal matrix, a liberty-centered libertarian matrix, and a six-foundation conservative matrix - while figures step onto the scale of their most sacred value, past a warning rail labeled 'the third rail that will get you electrocuted if you touch it.'","player_mission":"Sort each figure and quote onto the matrix that owns it: liberals with their sacred value of caring for victims of oppression, libertarians with their sacred value of individual liberty, and social conservatives with their sacred value of preserving institutions and traditions. Justify every placement by the foundation weights on the diagram before the third rail electrifies.","key_concepts":["For American liberals since the 1960s, the most sacred value is caring for victims of oppression, and anyone who blames such victims for their own problems or merely excuses prejudice against them can expect a vehement tribal response","Garrison Keillor captured the spirit and self-image of the left: 'I am a liberal, and liberalism is the politics of kindness'","The liberal moral matrix rests primarily on the Care and Liberty foundations, with thin but existing lines to Loyalty, Authority, and Sanctity - think of the sanctification of nature","Libertarians are the direct descendants of the eighteenth- and nineteenth-century Enlightenment reformers who fought to free people and markets from the control of kings and clergy, and liberty is their sacred value","In the late nineteenth century liberalism split: the new liberals, or progressives, saw powerful corporations as the chief threats to liberty, while the classical liberals continued to fear government, and progressives like Theodore Roosevelt and Woodrow Wilson broke up monopolies and created regulatory agencies","The libertarian writer Will Wilkinson suggests libertarians are basically liberals who love markets and lack bleeding hearts - a claim our research at YourMorals.org confirms","In a project led by Ravi Iyer and Sena Koleva, analyses of surveys from 12,000 libertarians showed they score higher than conservatives on openness to experience and lower than conservatives on disgust sensitivity and conscientiousness","Libertarians have generally supported the Republican Party since the 1930s because libertarians and Republicans share a common enemy: the liberal welfare society that they believe is destroying America's liberty and moral fiber","Social conservatives have the broadest set of moral concerns, valuing all six foundations relatively equally, and this breadth - especially high settings on Loyalty, Authority, and Sanctity - gives them insights into threats to moral capital that liberals cannot perceive","For social conservatives, preserving the institutions and traditions that provide our moral exoskeletons is the most sacred value, and they fight back ferociously when they believe change will damage institutions such as the family","As one formulation puts it, when the foundations of society are threatened, the conservative ideology reminds men of the necessity of some institutions and the desirability of the existing ones","John Stuart Mill said that a party of order or stability and a party of progress or reform are both necessary elements of a healthy state of political life"],"difficulty_level":"Advanced"},{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Moral Psychology | Intuition vs Reason | Political Division","topic":"Boss Battle: The Yin-Yang Arbitration","visual_frame_description":"A courtroom where two giant hands, one labeled Care and one labeled Liberty, hold a scale of externalities; exhibits line the bench - a leaded gas pump, a can of peas, a hospital checklist, a beehive with a single dying bee being rescued by a giant hand - while a banner reads 'Morality binds and blinds.'","player_mission":"Arbitrate the disputes of the age under Durkheimian utilitarianism: rule on the two liberal points - governments should restrain corporate superorganisms, and some problems really can be solved by regulation - and the two counterpoints - markets are miraculous, and you can't help the bees by destroying the hive. Weigh the evidence: the leaded gasoline phaseout, the can of peas, Goldhill's father, the hunkering turtles, and produce a verdict that turns Manichaean war into yin-yang disagreement.","key_concepts":["Haidt argues that corporations are actual superorganisms that will grow ever more powerful as they evolve, and the only force left on Earth that can stand up to the largest of them is national governments","Economists speak of externalities - the costs incurred by third parties who did not agree to the transaction - as when a farmer's new fertilizer increases his yield but causes damaging runoff into nearby rivers","When corporations are given the ring of Gyges - secrecy and public invisibility, and the ability to capture politicians and agencies - we can expect catastrophic results, and efficient markets require government regulation","The chemical industry blocked all efforts to ban lead additives from gasoline for decades, but after the Carter administration began a partial phaseout and a bipartisan group of congressmen stood up against the industry, lead was completely removed from gasoline by the 1990s","The lead phaseout may have been responsible for up to half of the extraordinary and otherwise unexplained drop in crime in the 1990s, as the generation of boys with lead-poisoned brains was replaced by young men with unleaded brains and better impulse control","From a Durkheimian utilitarian perspective, the cheapest and most humane way to fight crime may be to give more money and authority to the Environmental Protection Agency","David Goldhill's father was killed by an infection he caught while in the hospital, and Goldhill learned that the death rate can be cut by two-thirds when hospitals follow a simple checklist of sanitary procedures that most hospitals never adopt","Goldhill's 2009 Atlantic essay 'How American Health Care Killed My Father' argues that using insurance for routine purchases is absurd - we would never file a claim on our car insurance to pay for an oil change","Libertarians sometimes have a quasi-religious faith in free markets because competition rewards every innovation that shaves a penny off cost - a can of peas bought for under a dollar represents farmers, truckers, miners, and metalworkers coordinated by the invisible hand","Edmund Burke said in 1790 that to love the little platoon we belong to in society is the first principle of public affections, the first link in the series by which we proceed towards a love of our country and of mankind","Putnam's paper 'E Pluribus Unum' found that ethnic diversity reduces both bridging capital - trust between groups - and bonding capital - trust within groups - making people in diverse settings hunker down and pull in like a turtle, the exact opposite of hiving","America's political class has become far more Manichaean since the early 1990s, following the third-century Persian prophet Mani's vision of the world as a battleground between absolute good and absolute evil in which compromise is a sin"],"difficulty_level":"Advanced"}]}]};
